EVIDENCE TIERS. Every substantive claim below is tagged:
    [SOLID]      n >= 8, clean internal structure, numbers given in full.
    [DIRECTION]  real measurement, too few points or too wide an interval to
                 quote a coefficient. Do not build on the number.
    [UNTESTED]   built and/or plausible, never checked against a valid label.
    [RETRACTED]  was believed, is not any more. Section 11 says why.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0. WHAT THIS DOCUMENT IS, AND HOW TO KEEP IT TRUE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The single source of truth for the BPP+ algorithm AND the research log behind it.
Meant to be handed to another agent cold, and usable in two directions: to
maintain the shipped system, and to work out what research has been tried, what
it cost, and what it returned.

*** MAINTENANCE RULE ***
Whenever the algorithm changes — a constant, a gate, a term, a band edge, a new
measurement, or a falsified hypothesis — UPDATE THIS FILE IN THE SAME CHANGE.
It is a design document, not a snapshot: it should always describe the shipped
system. If you change code and not this file, this file becomes a liability,
because its whole value is that a reader can trust it without reading the code.
Re-run the numbers in sections 7-9 when you touch the model; they are all
derived from live data and go stale silently.
SECOND RULE, for the log half: a hypothesis that was tested and failed belongs in
section 11, not deleted. This document was previously grown by APPENDING
corrections, so it argued with its own earlier editions in five places. Fold
corrections INTO the current statement; keep a retraction only where a reader
would otherwise repeat the mistake.

Code owners:
  controller/lib/arr-inspect.js   the scoring math (bppOf, bppIndex, bands, RSE)
  controller/lib/probe.js         the nightly CRF probe, the denominator,
                                  biasFactor (live) and biasFactorLegacy
  controller/lib/audit.js         how the score is ACTED on (gates, sections)
  controller/lib/release-rules.js source tiers, editions, IMAX, re-encodes
  controller/scripts/probe-film.sh    one film, N samples at one CRF
  controller/scripts/probe-ladder.sh  one film, N samples at several CRFs
  controller/scripts/probe-starve.sh  starve the sampled clips, then re-measure
  scripts/ladder-pilot.js  scripts/starve-experiment.js   --plan/--run/--report
  data/ladder-pilot.json   data/starve-experiment.json    raw experiment data
  docs/DESIGN-CRF-PROBE.md        the original probe design
  docs/archive/HANDOFF-2026-08-17.md   the grain investigation writeup

EXPLORING THE DATA (for an agent doing analysis, this is the entry point):

  GET /api/probe/dataset    Read-only, ~600 KB, one row per unit. The full per-unit measurement
                            table: raw AND corrected complexity, per-sample control points, banked
                            upgrade pairs, and the score the app currently shows.

                            USE THIS RATHER THAN /config/probe-cache.json. The cache holds the raw
                            measurement but not the DERIVED numbers — the bias factor, the effective
                            target, the score — because those live in code. Re-implementing them
                            drifts from the controller silently, and then the findings are about the
                            analysis rather than about the library.

                            Full field reference, worked jq examples, and the three traps:
                            AGENTS.md, "The CRF probe — how to drive it".

                            THE THREE TRAPS, repeated here because they are easy to miss:
                              1. Compare against `cxEff`, never `complexity`. The raw value is only
                                 meaningful when studying the correction itself.
                              2. A blank `sampleN` is NOT a unit measured without samples. Every
                                 unit had at least 8; per-sample records only began 2026-08-14.
                              3. Do not correlate against the 2026-08-13 verdicts — retracted, see
                                 section 11.1. There is no valid subjective label in this library.

  bpp-lab/                  `npm run dev` on :5174. A read-only UI over that same endpoint:
                            overview + headroom what-if, a draggable response curve, per-film
                            control points, the pinning-bias fit, and a sortable table with CSV
                            export. Never writes to the server. See bpp-lab/README.md.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. THE PROBLEM, STATED PROPERLY — AND THE CONSTRAINTS THAT BOUND IT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1.1 THE DECISION BEING MADE
~3.2 TB of 1080p-and-below films on an 8 TB drive, ~1000 titles. The task is
CHOOSING BETWEEN TORRENT SOURCES:
    UPGRADE   this copy is compromised; is a release on offer meaningfully
              better, and worth the bandwidth and the disk?
    DOWNGRADE this copy spends bits nobody can see; is there a smaller release
              that gives the same picture and hands back the disk?
Both are the same question in opposite directions, and both must be answerable
from ONE NUMBER attached to a file, because the number drives a human sort in the
Audit tab and then a grab / don't-grab decision. File size answers neither.

Bits per pixel is the natural normaliser:
    bpp = videoBitrate / (width * height * fps)
It removes resolution and frame rate, so a 720p file and a 1080p file are
comparable. But raw bpp is illegible — this entire library lives between 0.02 and
0.44, so every interesting difference is in the third decimal place. BPP+ is that
ratio made legible and made per-film.

1.2 LOGICAL CONSTRAINTS
These let a reader evaluate a new paper or metric in five minutes. Almost every
published video-quality result fails at least one.

  (a) *** NO REFERENCE. *** We never hold a pristine master; the file on disk IS
      the best copy of that film in the building. Every FULL-REFERENCE metric —
      VMAF, SSIM, PSNR — needs a master, so all of them are STRUCTURALLY
      UNAVAILABLE for the question we ask. This is why ITU-T P.1204.3
      (no-reference, bitstream-based) is closer to our problem than every Netflix
      ladder paper, and why "just use VMAF" keeps looking attractive and keeps
      being wrong: VMAF against our own copy measures distance from a copy we
      already suspect.

  (b) *** THE SOURCE IS ALREADY COMPRESSED *** — unknown provenance, unknown
      generation count, possibly a re-encode of a re-encode. Netflix's per-title
      work and Durbha/Bovik both ASSUME A PRISTINE INPUT, and that assumption is
      load-bearing in their maths. It is false for us, always.

  (c) *** THE MEASUREMENT'S INPUT IS OUR COPY, NOT A MASTER. *** State this
      precisely. The probe encodes short SAMPLES to a NULL OUTPUT: nothing is
      written, nothing on disk is modified, /data is mounted READ-ONLY in the
      container. WE NEVER RE-ENCODE MEDIA. The limitation is on the INPUT side —
      a sample of a starved file can only measure what is left IN that file, so
      the measurement inherits the copy's limits. That is the origin of the
      pinning problem (section 5), and it is a property of what we can OBSERVE,
      not of anything done to the media.

  (d) *** THE ANSWER MUST BE ONE SCALAR. *** Brennan: "I like BPP+ as a single #
      where I aim to upgrade stuff to around 100... If we can fold in multi
      dimension into our one number I'm game but if not then ehh." Everything bits
      CANNOT fix is a separate GATE beside the score (section 10), never a term
      inside it — folding corrupts a number that means one clean thing.

  (e) *** WHAT PRECISION IS FOR. *** NOT band membership. The colour bands are a
      reading aid for skimming a long list; a human reads 98 and 101 as the same,
      and a change that moves 23 films across a band edge is not thereby a
      problem. Precision must support (i) RANKING candidate releases against each
      other and against the copy already held, and (ii) a rough sense of DISTANCE
      FROM ~100, since Brennan upgrades toward 100 and downgrades toward 100.
      So the quantity that must be trustworthy is THE GAP BETWEEN TWO SCORES. An
      error moving both scores together is harmless; the same error on only one
      is not. This is the correct lens for every accuracy argument here — it is
      why source pinning matters so much (it hits the starved copy and not the
      good one, i.e. exactly one side of the comparison) and why the absolute
      value of HEADROOM_TARGET matters less than it looks.

1.3 PHYSICAL CONSTRAINTS
  - 4-core NUC that ALSO serves video. Encode experiments run strictly
    sequentially (trap 6).
  - Nightly thermal window 01:00-06:00, 240-min budget. An uncapped nightly task
    has already run the box hot all day once (the trickplay incident), which is
    why widening the unattended night budget is not done casually. A MANUAL
    SESSION is the sanctioned way to run it hard by day.
  - 8 TB drive: 5.81 TB used, 2.13 TB free (2026-08-18). Disk pressure is real
    but not acute — there is no hurry to reclaim.
  - 1080p CEILING. Nothing in the house plays above 1080p, so 2160p is never an
    upgrade (10.5).
  - THE PROJECTOR IS BEING REPLACED, native 720p -> 1080p. Every quality figure
    here was gathered while a 44% downscale hid compression and grain alike. That
    mask is coming off; see 2.3, section 9, 12.3.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. THE FORMULA
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    BPP+ = round( 100 * sqrt( bpp / target ) )

    target = complexity * biasFactor(R) * HEADROOM_TARGET
    HEADROOM_TARGET = 1.0                    (the single global calibration knob)
    complexity      = the measured bpp of a CRF-20 encode of THIS FILM
                      (the nightly probe; section 4)
    biasFactor(R)   = the SOURCE-PINNING CORRECTION, >= 1.0 (section 5). Do not
                      omit it: it is applied at resolve time to every score, and
                      for starved files is worth up to x2.0 (hard ceiling) on the
                      denominator, i.e. about -29% on BPP+.

Equivalently, with R = srcBitrate / probeBitrate ("bitrate adequacy"):
    BPP+ ~= 100 * sqrt(R)
...but read trap 11 before plotting anything against R. That approximation is off
by a median factor of 0.792 and the discrepancy looks exactly like the model
failing to fit its own data.

100 IS NOT "AVERAGE". 100 means "this file spends exactly as many bits as a
CRF-20 encode of itself would spend". A per-film target, not a library constant.

2.1 WHY THE SQUARE ROOT                                          [DIRECTION]
Measured on this library's own content (raw/bitrate-plateau-2026-08-01.md),
visible error falls as roughly bitrate^-0.5 — quadrupling the bitrate halves the
distortion. The square root inverts that, so the index is proportional to 1/error
and its ratios mean something perceptual rather than something about disk:
    200 = about HALF the visible error of 100   (and 4x the bits)
     50 = about TWICE the visible error of 100  (and 1/4 the bits)
The exponent is fitted from our content and never independently checked. IT IS
THE WEAKEST LINK IN THE MODEL. It is also MONOTONIC, so it re-labels every file
without re-ranking any — by 1.2(e), the mildest possible kind of error.

2.2 BANDS
    >= 125   purple "wow"   ~20%+ less error than 100.
    100-124  green  "ok"    the target.
     75-99   orange "warn"  diminished. Might still be fine; human call.
      < 75   red    "bad"   compromised.
Cutoffs are the old linear 150/100/60 carried through the same square root
(sqrt(1.5)=1.22, sqrt(0.6)=0.77), rounded to 125/100/75. *** THEY ARE NOT
KNEE-DERIVED. *** Reading them as "where the curve bends" reads in something that
was never measured (2.5).

2.3 THE DISPLAY IS CHANGING (2026-08-18)
The bands were written against a native 720p projector, which downscales every
1080p file ~44% and so hides a lot. The 1080p replacement shows files close to
1:1. So the "wow" band stops being disk spent on the invisible — that was always
described as what pays off after a projector upgrade, and the upgrade is here —
and the same files may well look worse, since nothing about them changes except
that the mask comes off. The 1080p ceiling itself is unaffected (10.5).

2.4 WHAT THE NUMBER MEANS IN A UNIT A HUMAN ALREADY HAS INTUITION FOR
Since x265 bitrate runs roughly -15% per CRF point, BPP+ converts into a CRF
EQUIVALENT: "what CRF would an encode of this content at this bitrate be?"

    CRF_equivalent = 20 - ln(bpp/target) / ln(1/0.85)

    BPP+  30 -> CRF 34.8      BPP+ 125 -> CRF 17.3
    BPP+  50 -> CRF 28.5      BPP+ 150 -> CRF 15.0
    BPP+  75 -> CRF 23.5      BPP+ 200 -> CRF 11.5
     BPP+ 100 -> CRF 20.0
     Library today: p10 CRF 29.6 | p25 28.3 | MEDIAN 26.3 | p75 23.5 | p90 20.2

THIS REFRAMES THE HEADROOM QUESTION INTO AN ANSWERABLE ONE. "Should
HEADROOM_TARGET be 1.0 or 0.57?" is abstract. "Is a CRF-20 encode your definition
of correct, or is CRF 23.7?" is answerable from experience by anyone who has
encoded video — headroom 0.57 is exactly "100 means CRF 23.7". It does not
REPLACE a paired subjective test (CRF preference is itself content-dependent,
section 4) but it converts a calibration constant into a familiar judgement.
The -15%/point figure is now MEASURED: library median -13.6%/point, 2x spread
between films (section 6).

2.5 THE TARGET FORMULATION: MARGINAL QUALITY PER BIT
Brennan, 2026-08-18, from the Netflix per-title work: the transferable idea is not
the absolute bitrate but the SLOPE — how much quality another Mbps buys.
    3.0 -> 82,  3.5 -> 83,  4.0 -> 83.5,  5.0 -> 84    OVERSIZED (slope flat)
    2.0 -> 69,  2.5 -> 76,  3.0 -> 81,    3.5 -> 84    UNDERSIZED (still climbing)
Netflix spaces ladder rungs about one JND apart, which maps onto the bad -> OK ->
great -> oversized scale this project wants: band edges would become KNEE-DERIVED
rather than inherited.
WHAT WOULD BE NEEDED: a per-film rate-QUALITY curve, i.e. a quality axis (libvmaf,
16.A.6) on top of the rate curve the ladder gives. The pilot delivered the RATE
half and found NO KNEES in it (section 6). Netflix's scalar works because VMAF is
a perceptual axis; the lesson is not "one number is wrong" but "one number is only
meaningful if it rides a perceptual axis". That is the real open question about
BPP+: not whether a scalar is enough, but whether bpp/target is the right latent
axis.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. WHAT COUNTS AS "bpp" — THE NUMERATOR
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Three refinements, each of which fixed a real misscoring.

3.1 videoBitrate IS SANITY-CHECKED AGAINST THE FILE.
*arr's MediaInfo reports a nominal or peak rate rather than an average for some
HEVC files, and it is wildly wrong when it does. Challengers is 4.5 GB over
2:11:11 (4.6 Mb/s all-in) and Radarr claimed videoBitrate 30,317,898 — a 6.5x
overstatement that painted it purple and filed it as bloat. The video track
cannot out-rate the container, so anything above the size-derived total is
rejected (1.05 tolerance for container overhead).

3.2 AUDIO IS NOT CHARGED TO VIDEO.                                    [SOLID]
videoBitrate is 0 on ~18% of the library, so bpp falls back to the size-derived
total — and then every audio track inflates it. Measured on Lawrence of Arabia:
AC3 448k + DTS-HD MA 2.18 Mb/s = 2.63 Mb/s of a 16.61 Mb/s container, i.e. bpp
overstated ~19% and BPP+ ~9% (134 -> ~123).

The bias ran the WRONG WAY for our purposes: it flattered multi-dub and
lossless-audio releases most — exactly the class we do not want flattered.

*arr reports only ONE track's bitrate (the default), not the sum, so the audit
subtracts what is KNOWN and never guesses. bppSource() reports which happened:
    'video'                       exact, from MediaInfo
    'total-minus-audio-measured'  exact, our own ffprobe of every track
    'total-minus-audio'           exact enough (single audio track)
    'total-minus-audio-partial'   still a CEILING (more tracks than known rates)
    'total'                       no audio figure at all
Subtraction is capped at AUDIO_SHARE_CAP of the container so a corrupt reading
cannot zero out the video.

MEASURED: audio is a median 10.2% of the container, and up to 65.4% in the worst
case. Audio coverage is now 1031/1031 units, measured by our own nightly pass.
This is also the worked example behind the partial-data rule (section 16.B.4):
coverage grew from 8 films to 1031 and every film it reached was more accurate
for it.

3.3 HEVC GETS AN EFFICIENCY MULTIPLIER.
x265 at the same bpp looks better than x264, so raw bpp is scaled by
X265_EFFICIENCY before indexing. 52 files are HEVC; their median BPP+ is 83.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. THE DENOMINATOR — THE NIGHTLY CRF PROBE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This is what makes BPP+ per-film rather than one-size-fits-all. Every night, in a
gated window, the controller takes 8 four-second samples spread across a title,
encodes them at CRF 20 TO A NULL OUTPUT, and records the bitrate that costs. That
is `complexity`: what THIS content costs to look right. Nothing is written;
/data is read-only in the container (1.2c).

WHY CRF: it is a PERCEPTUAL-QUALITY anchor, not a bitrate one. CRF-20 encodes of
Casablanca and Blade Runner 2049 are APPROXIMATELY the same visual quality; they
cost 3853 vs 1000 kb/s. That is exactly the per-film number the denominator needs.
"Approximately" is doing real work: fixed CRF is not exactly fixed quality across
content — published spread ~3-sigma 10 VMAF vs ~2.6 for quality-targeted encoding
(MainConcept, section 17). Netflix's per-title work rests on the same observation.
Whether that residual runs with grain HERE — which would make the denominator's
error systematic rather than random — is open question 4, never measured.

HEADROOM_TARGET = 1.0 means "100 = as many bits as a CRF-20 encode of this film".
Moving it does NOT require re-probing: x265 bitrate runs ~-13.6%/CRF point
(measured, section 6), so a preference for CRF 22 is a read-time constant. The
expensive measurement stays valid.

4.1 THE MEASUREMENT THAT KILLED THE FLAT CONSTANT                     [SOLID]
Before 2026-08-06 the denominator was one number, BPP_TARGET = 0.13, library-wide.
Measured complexity across 1031 units:
    min 0.0346 (The Father, clean digital) | p10 0.0705 | p25 0.0880 |
    median 0.1120 | p75 0.1497 | p90 0.2141 | max 0.9270 (12 Angry Men, B&W
    heavy grain) | spread 26.8x
The flat 0.13 was wrong by roughly 2x in BOTH directions and SYSTEMATICALLY: it
overrated grainy film-stock transfers (whose bits buy grain reproduction, not
detail) and underrated clean modern digital ones. The median 0.112 shows 0.13 was
a decent library-wide average and a poor per-film answer — the exact failure mode
you cannot see without measuring. It survives ONLY as the no-probe fallback.

4.2 PRECISION MACHINERY (what the nightly does beyond first measurement)
  PROBE_SAMPLES  8     the floor, not a luxury: scene complexity varies 8.2x
                       WITHIN a single film
  SAMPLES_MAX    16    used when a unit's spread or RSE says 8 was not enough
  POOL_MAX       64    samples accumulate across visits; SE falls as 1/sqrt(n)
  RSE_GOOD       0.12  +/-12% on the mean = precise enough to stop spending encodes
  RSE_WIDE       0.20
  SPREAD_WIDE    6     ~p75 of the library, measured 2026-08-17
  IMPRECISE_SHARE 3    one pick in 3 goes to refinement once fresh work runs out
  AUDIO_BATCH    8     audio files measured per tick
Pooling only happens when the bytes on disk are unchanged (`sameFile`), so a
replaced file starts clean rather than averaging two different encodes.
Nightly priority order: first-measurement -> audio backfill -> refinement.

4.3 THE POOLING BUG — REPEAT VISITS RE-MEASURED THE SAME CLIPS   [SOLID, FIXED]
     [found 2026-08-18 by Brennan, from banding in the lab's sample plot]
Sample offsets are deterministic (T = start + span*i/N), which is correct and
necessary — a ladder or re-probe must hit the same scenes. But x265 is ALSO
deterministic, so re-probing with the same N re-encoded IDENTICAL clips and
returned byte-identical bitrates. Those duplicates were pooled. Since SE =
sd/sqrt(n), the error bar SHRANK on evidence that did not exist — and it got worse
the more a film was "refined", the exact opposite of pooling's purpose.

    unit                     stored n   distinct   cxRSE reported   truth   under by
    Dunkirk (2017)              64         16          0.1986       0.3972    100%
    Sympathy for Mr. Vengeance  56         16          0.1790       0.3344     87%
    Casino Royale (1967)        16          8          0.1300       0.1903     46%
    Drive (2011)                24         16          0.1508       0.1844     22%
    Gladiator (2000)            24         16          0.1379       0.1648     19%

Every unit visited more than once was affected in proportion to how often; units
visited once (Frozen Planet II, Lessons in Chemistry) were correct.

RE-VERIFIED 2026-08-20 STRAIGHT FROM THE PRE-REPAIR BACKUP
(/config/probe-cache.json.bak-dedupe-20260819052712. — note the trailing dot in
the filename). Dunkirk then: 64 readings, 16 DISTINCT values, stored cxRSE
0.1986; recomputed on the distinct count it is 0.3972, so the reported error bar
was exactly half the truth. The 0.407 previously quoted here was 0.397.

*** AND A WARNING ABOUT CHECKING THIS CLAIM. *** A 2026-08-19 review reported
that this anecdote "does not reproduce" — because it recomputed from the CURRENT
cache, where the duplicates have already been re-counted and the number cannot
exist by construction. It reproduces to three decimals from the backup. A
before/after claim can only be checked against the "before" artifact; if that
artifact is a backup, the claim must name it, which is why the filename is now
written down. Dunkirk today: 48 distinct of 64, cxRSE 0.1667 — still the library
maximum, but the repair and the phase shift took it from 16 distinct clips to 48. WHY 24 YIELDS
16 DISTINCT: samplesFor() returns 8 or 16 and the 16-grid CONTAINS the 8-grid
(span*i/8 == span*2i/16), so a second visit at N=16 re-measured all 8 and added 8.
TWO FIXES, both needed:
  1. EFFECTIVE n. sampleStats counts DISTINCT clips — by position where known,
     falling back to distinct values for legacy entries (two scenes returning
     identical bitrates to 5 dp does not happen, so an exact repeat is a
     re-measured clip). Reported as `sampleNEff`; `sampleN` still counts readings.
  2. PHASE-SHIFTED GRIDS. probe-film.sh takes --phase (0 <= p < 1, in units of one
     sample gap); probe.js advances it per visit along a van der Corput sequence
     (0, 1/2, 1/4, 3/4, 1/8...), so each visit lands in the largest remaining gap
     and pooling reduces the error bar for the right reason. `visits` is persisted.
CONSEQUENCE FOR EXISTING DATA: every pooled cxRSE written before this is too
small, and they are not re-derivable. The fix corrects future measurements and
re-derives effective n for existing ones, but the samples stay duplicated until
those units are visited again under a new phase.

4.4 UNCERTAINTY IS PUBLISHED, NOT HIDDEN
Until 2026-08-18 a number pinned to +/-3% and one pinned to +/-40% printed
identically, and the Audit tab would act on either.
THE SQUARE ROOT HALVES THE ERROR: a relative error e on the target becomes ~e/2 on
the index, so a complexity known only to +/-24% still yields a BPP+ good to about
+/-12%. bppRSE() returns rse/2, and BPP_RSE_LOOSE = 0.10 on the INDEX is a looser
gate than it first appears.
UI CONTRACT: past the threshold the score renders as `bpp+*`, real number and
error bar in the tooltip. A null RSE is treated as CONFIDENT, not doubtful — most
of the library has no per-sample data yet and marking 1000 films provisional would
make the mark meaningless. The mark is for films we KNOW are loosely measured.
CURRENT STATE (the weakest live area): 169 units carry a cxRSE (148 movies, 21
seasons), 116 pooled past 8 effective samples. Median cxRSE where it exists is
0.111 (p90 0.128), i.e. ~+/-5.5% on the index — the refine queue has been running
and the pool is growing. spreadRatio median 4.01, p75 6.24, p90 9.10.

4.5 TV SEASONS
A season is measured as a whole: episodes sampled, complexity episode-weighted,
all-or-nothing. `disagree` records how much sampled episodes disagreed. Median
disagree 0.241 across 146 seasons, 71 exceed 0.25 — season numbers are much softer
than movie numbers, and SEASON_DISAGREE_MAX = 0.25 gates them out of
disk-reclamation suggestions (that gate removed 92 GB of fiction in one pass).
A SERIES row is the episode-weighted mean of its own measured seasons, basis
`measured:series`, and requires EVERY season measured — a partial average would
silently describe a subset. Before it existed, series rows were scored against the
flat constant and rendered "never probed", i.e. "we know nothing" about shows that
are fully measured (all 146 season units across all 97 series are measured).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. THE SOURCE-PINNING CORRECTION (biasFactor) — APPLIED TO EVERY SCORE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
`complexity` is meant to be a property of the FILM. It is not, quite.

THE MECHANISM. The probe's input is our copy (1.2c), and CRF 20 cannot ask for
detail the source does not contain. A starved copy measures as an EASY film, its
target sinks toward that copy's own bitrate, and the deficient file scores close
to 100. THE WORSE THE COPY, THE MORE IT FLATTERS ITSELF — so BPP+ was
systematically OVERSTATED, most where accuracy matters. By 1.2(e) this is the
worst possible shape of error: it hits the starved side of an upgrade comparison
and not the good side.

FIRST PROOF, same film, same probe, only the source changed (Paris, Texas,
2026-08-12, a 2.22 GB copy replaced by a 12.73 GB Criterion transfer):
    srcBitrate x6.02  ->  complexity 0.05982 -> 0.13939   (x2.33)
And Easy Rider, measured BPP+ 78, was visibly full of grain-shimmer on flat walls;
Brennan put it at "a 60ish, or even lower like a 40" by eye. Corrected: ~65.

THE PREDICTOR IS R, no genre or year heuristics needed. R < 1 means the file sits
BELOW its own measured target — the probe ran out of source before it ran out of
appetite — so the number is a FLOOR. R > 1 means the probe had headroom.

5.1 THE STARVATION EXPERIMENT — THE DECISIVE RESULT            [SOLID, 2026-08-18]
Tooling: controller/scripts/probe-starve.sh + scripts/starve-experiment.js.
Data: data/starve-experiment.json.

DESIGN. Take 8 films the model ALREADY TRUSTS (R >= 1.2, no correction applied),
starve them to 1/2 and 1/4 bitrate, re-measure. The starving re-encodes ONLY THE
SAMPLED CLIPS — a full-film re-encode is ~7 h, and starving the exact clips the
ladder measures is equivalent and takes seconds. Content is byte-identical across
levels, so ANYTHING THAT MOVED WAS CAUSED BY STARVATION AND NOTHING ELSE. `cx20`
at each level IS the complexity the probe would report for that copy, so the
understatement is measured, not inferred. THE PREDICTION WAS RECORDED IN THE
SCRIPT BEFORE THE RUN, so it could not be fitted afterwards.

RESULT 1 — REAL, MONOTONIC, UNIVERSAL IN THE SAMPLE. 8/8 films flatten
monotonically as the copy is starved; mean flattening unstarved -> most-starved
4.12 slope points; 16 controlled points (8 films x 2 starved levels).

RESULT 2 — R PREDICTS THE UNDERSTATEMENT; LADDER SLOPE DOES NOT.
    Spearman(observed R,   pinning understatement)   -0.924
    Spearman(ladder slope, pinning understatement)   +0.335
*** THE LADDER-SLOPE HYPOTHESIS IS FALSIFIED *** (11.5). biasFactor's design,
indexing on R, is VINDICATED rather than merely necessary.

RESULT 3 — *** THE OLD R_TRUST = 1.2 CAP WAS WRONG. *** 6 of the 16 points sit
ABOVE R 1.2, where the live rule applied NOTHING; they average x1.270
understatement, and Requiem for a Dream at R 2.58 still shows x1.269.
WHY THE CAP EXISTED, AND WHY IT WAS REASONABLE: no banked upgrade pair sits above
R 1.2, because you only re-download films that were bad, so a factor up there
would have been invented rather than measured. Sound reasoning FOR OBSERVATIONAL
DATA. The starvation method simply is not limited that way, and where it looked,
it found pinning.

METHODOLOGICAL CAVEAT, FOUND AND CORRECTED — instructive, keep it. The experiment
used 3 samples per point where the probe uses 8-16, so its ABSOLUTE complexity did
not reproduce the live measurement on high-scene-variance titles (Blade Runner
2049 2.29x off, Requiem 1.87x, Fargo 1.46x; divergence tracks spreadRatio). WITHIN
a film the SAME clips are used at every level, so the understatement RATIO is
clean — scene-selection error largely cancels in a ratio — and only the x-axis was
noisy. FIX: anchor R to the LIVE probe, take only the RATIO from the experiment.
THE ANCHORING, RECOVERED AND WRITTEN DOWN 2026-08-20. It had never been recorded
beyond the phrase above, which is not enough to reproduce a fit:

    R_anchored = level * R_live * under          under = cx20(base) / cx20(level)

That is the OBSERVED R the library would compute for the starved copy: its bitrate
is level * src, and the complexity it would report is the base complexity divided
by `under`. The anchoring is using the LIVE probe's base complexity (8-16 samples)
in place of the experiment's own (3 samples), so the precise absolute scale comes
from the probe and only the RATIO comes from the experiment.

CONFIRMED BY REPRODUCTION: least squares of log(under/1.06) on log(R_anchored),
all 16 points, R_live taken PRE-audio-fix, returns A 1.337, B -0.349, rho -0.915 —
the shipped constants exactly. (The "n=15" in the older note is a slip: the fit
reproduces with all 16 points. An earlier version of this section guessed that
Blade Runner 2049 at level 0.5 had been excluded for setting the floor. The
reproduction shows it was not. Guess withdrawn.)

*** `under` IS ON BOTH SIDES OF THIS FIT, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. *** It is the
y-variable and it also sits inside R_anchored. That looks exactly like a mistake
and it is not: at runtime biasFactor only ever has the OBSERVED R of a starved
file, which is inflated by the very understatement being predicted. A calibration
curve must be fitted on the axis it will be evaluated on. The consequence is that
rho here CANNOT be read as evidence of a causal relationship — the self-reference
induces a POSITIVE bias, so a strong NEGATIVE rho survives it rather than being
produced by it. Use the unanchored own-R rho (-0.924) for the causal claim and this
one only for the calibration.

That gave 1.337 * R^-0.348 (rho -0.915, all 16 points) instead of the unanchored
1.414 * R^-0.298 — slightly weaker, the right direction to err.

5.2 THE LIVE CORRECTION (controller/lib/probe.js, biasFactor)

    understatement = clamp( 1.114 * R^-0.392, 1, 2.0 ), held FLAT below R = 0.423
    PIN_A 1.114 | PIN_B -0.392 | PIN_R_MIN 0.423 | PIN_MAX 2.0
    (overridable: PROBE_PIN_A / _B / _R_MIN / _MAX)
    REFITTED AND DEPLOYED 2026-08-20 — was 1.337 * R^-0.348 flat below 0.59.
    PIN_MAX is now unreachable: the curve's maximum is its value at PIN_R_MIN, x1.561.

    R      live (refit)   previous curve   old bucketed rule
    0.30      x1.561          x1.606            x1.562
    0.42      x1.561          x1.606            x1.458
    0.59      x1.370          x1.606            x1.407
    1.00      x1.114          x1.337            x1.051
    1.32      x1.000          x1.214            x1.000
    2.00      x1.000          x1.050            x1.000

NO EXTRAPOLATION BELOW THE MEASURED RANGE: held flat below R = 0.59. The anchored
fit spans 0.59-2.27 (the experiment's own unanchored R values span 0.64-5.20).
Extending the power law to the library minimum R 0.14 would predict x2.6 on no
evidence. THIS IS THE SAME PRINCIPLE THAT PRODUCED THE OLD R_TRUST CAP, APPLIED
HONESTLY: the cap was removed because we WENT AND MEASURED above it, not because
caps are wrong.

THE OLD BUCKETED FIT IS KEPT AND STILL COMPUTED as biasFactorLegacy(), reported as
`legacy` on /api/probe, so drift between the two is visible rather than silent. It
does not score.

*** THE x1.06 FLOOR WAS WRONG — MEASURED 2026-08-20, THE CURVE OVER-CORRECTS. ***
The starved copies were made with x265 and the probe also encodes x265, so some
measured understatement was CODEC MATCHING rather than starvation. A floor of
x1.06 was subtracted for it, estimated from ONE FILM (Blade Runner 2049 at level
0.5, barely starved, R > 5). The confound check finally ran: the same 8 films and
the same clips starved with libx264 instead, so the artefacts are h264-shaped and
the probe's x265 has nothing to match.

    codec-matching factor = under_x265 / under_x264
    16 matched points: MEDIAN 1.1295, mean 1.127, range 1.032-1.235
    bootstrap 95% CI on the median  1.099 .. 1.151     assumed: 1.06

THE ASSUMED VALUE SITS OUTSIDE THE INTERVAL — the effect is about TWICE what was
subtracted, and all 16 points exceed 1.0. Full report:
docs/REPORT-x264-confound-2026-08-20.md.

THE COMPARISON NEEDS NO R AXIS: `under` is a ratio of two measurements of the SAME
clips, so R, the codec normalisation and the anchoring all divide out. And the
controls agree bit-exactly (level 1.0 is copied, never re-encoded, and cx20 is
identical between arms on all 8 films), so the two arms measured the same material.

TWO RESULTS THAT MATTER MORE THAN THE NUMBER:
  * THE FLOOR IS A CONSTANT, so the model's SHAPE was right. Level 0.5 gives 1.1295
    and level 0.25 gives 1.1131 — agreeing to 0.017, and in the direction OPPOSITE
    to starvation depth, so the two effects are not tracking each other.
    Subtracting one constant was correct; the constant was wrong.
  * THE x264 ARM IS THE LIBRARY-REALISTIC CONDITION, not merely a control. 831 of
    898 movies here are h264: a real starved file IS an h264 encode probed with
    x265. The x265 arm was the artificial one. So the x264 understatements are not
    a comparison to be corrected — THEY ARE WHAT SHOULD HAVE BEEN FITTED, and they
    need no floor subtraction at all.

INTERIM ARITHMETIC (holding PIN_B): values too high by 1.1295/1.06 = x1.066, so
PIN_A ~1.255 rather than 1.337, and BPP+ on the corrected films is currently
~3.2% TOO LOW. The model is PESSIMISTIC — the safe direction, and ~1.5x the size
of the 5.2a audio fix in the opposite direction.

THE REFIT, COMPUTED 2026-08-20, AWAITING BRENNAN'S DECISION — NOT APPLIED.
The anchoring was recovered the same evening (5.1), which unblocked it. Fitting on
the x264 arm — the library-realistic one — with NO floor subtraction, on the
audio-corrected R axis that scoring now actually uses:

    fit                                            A       B      rho    n
    x265 arm, rawR axis, /1.06     = LIVE CURVE   1.337  -0.349  -0.915  16
    x265 arm, video-only R, /1.06                 1.255  -0.334  -0.779  16
    x264 arm, rawR axis, no floor                 1.195  -0.414  -0.865  16
    x264 arm, video-only R, no floor  <-- PROPOSED  1.114  -0.392  -0.682  16

*** AND THE AUDIO FIX (5.2a) TURNS OUT TO REQUIRE A REFIT AFTER ALL. *** 5.2a says
the constants did not need re-deriving, and that is right about probe-starve.sh's
OWN axis — but the fit does not use that axis, it uses R_anchored, which contains
R_LIVE, which WAS audio-polluted when the fit was made. So the shipped curve is
evaluated on video-only R while having been fitted on audio-polluted R. That
mismatch alone moves A from 1.337 to 1.255. Correction recorded rather than
quietly patched.

WHAT ADOPTING A 1.114 / B -0.392 WOULD DO (movies, 898):
    correction at the pin floor   x1.606 -> x1.370
    correction reaches 1.0 at     R 2.30 -> R 1.32
    films corrected at all        886 -> 827
    median BPP+                   60 -> 64.5
    885 films move, median +5 points, max +12
    106 band flips, ALL UPWARD; bad share 674 -> 606, wow 28 -> 36

NOTE rho FALLS to -0.682 on the target fit. Some of that is the audio correction
compressing the R range, and some is genuine: the x264 arm is a noisier
relationship than the artificial x265 one looked. The direction is unchanged and
every one of the 16 points still shows understatement, but the CONFIDENCE in the
exponent is lower than the shipped -0.348 ever advertised.

UNAFFECTED BY ALL OF THIS: the mechanism, the direction, and the finding that
R_TRUST was wrong.

5.2a THE x-AXIS HAD AUDIO IN IT — FIXED 2026-08-20              [SOLID, MAJOR]
R must be video-only on both sides, for the same reason 3.2 took audio out of the
numerator. It was not. probe-film.sh takes srcBitrate from the video stream when
ffprobe reports one and falls back to the CONTAINER TOTAL when it does not, and on
the fallback path every audio track sat inside R. An inflated R makes biasFactor
too SMALL, so the correction was UNDER-applied — on multi-dub and lossless-audio
releases, i.e. exactly the class 3.2 exists to stop flattering. The same bias, one
layer deeper, on the correction's own input.

MEASURED (ffprobe ground truth, all 898 movies, 2026-08-20):
    container-path (polluted)  361      video-path (already clean)  537
    of the polluted rows, 271 changed biasFactor
    BPP+ was inflated by a median 2.17%, max 8.33% (The Witch, 39% audio)
    16 films sat in the wrong band

THE FIX IS SCOPED, NOT BLANKET, AND THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE FINDING. The
obvious one-liner — subtract audioBps from every srcBitrate — would have deflated
the 537 already-clean rows by a median 2.1% (max 8.5%), a NEW error LARGER than the
one being repaired. probe-film.sh now records which numerator it used (`srcBasis`),
scripts/backfill-srcbasis.js filled it in for every pre-existing entry, and
videoR() in probe.js subtracts only on the container path. Applied: 251 movies fell,
all of them, median -2.35%; the controls that must not move did not.

THE FITTED CONSTANTS DID NOT NEED RE-DERIVING, and this is worth stating because it
looks like they should. All 8 calibration films are container-path files (audio
7.3-25.5%). But probe-starve.sh extracts its clips with -an -sn and starves them
with `-b:v level*SRCBPS`, so a starved clip genuinely CARRIES level*SRCBPS bits of
VIDEO, and the R attributed to it is that clip's true video-only R. The audio
inflated how much each level actually starved; the LABEL moved with it. Only levels
below 1.0 enter the fit, so the untouched control — the one copy that really does
carry audio it is not credited for — never lands on the x-axis. The experiment was
self-consistent; only the library-side application was wrong. DO NOT "FIX" THE
AUDIO IN probe-starve.sh: doing so would break that identity and mis-calibrate
PIN_A/PIN_B. There is a comment saying so at the point of temptation.

5.3 THE OLD OBSERVATIONAL FIT — NOW THE CROSS-CHECK              [DIRECTION]
Fitted from our own banked pairs; every upgrade banks one (setEntry). Live fit
2026-08-20, 113 pairs (148 banked overall):
        R 0.00-0.30 x1.407 n=8  | R 0.30-0.40 x1.407 n=23
        R 0.40-0.50 x1.407 n=37 | R 0.50-0.70 x1.407 n=28
        R 0.70-1.00 x1.179 n=9  | R 1.00-1.20 x1.051 n=3 (interpolated to 1.0)
        R >= 1.20   x1.000  no correction at all

IT MOVED ON 2026-08-20 AND THAT IS THE POINT OF KEEPING IT. The four lowest
buckets read 1.562/1.562/1.458/1.429 the day before and now all read 1.407.
Nothing about the fitting changed — its x-axis did. Removing audio from R (5.2a)
lowered R on the container-path rows, they re-sorted into different buckets, the
medians moved, and the monotone guard then flattened the top four into one value.
A cross-check whose own input shares an axis with the thing being checked will
move whenever that axis is corrected; read a difference between this and the live
curve as a question, never as evidence on its own.
THREE GUARDS, because raw per-bucket medians are not safe as-is:
    BIAS_MIN_N = 8    a bucket with a handful of pairs is noise. The 1.00-1.20
                      bucket once had n=1 and gave x1.39 — HIGHER than the
                      better-sampled bucket below it (x1.24, n=4).
    MONOTONE          the correction must not grow as R grows.
    BIAS_R_TRUST=1.2  measured wrong (5.1 result 3); legacy path only.
Gaps are filled BY POSITION, never by carrying a neighbour — carrying was wrong in
both directions and both errors were live for one deploy. Non-monotonic pairs
(complexity FELL when the source improved — 18 of 96, noise or a changed cut) are
excluded entirely.

*** IT DOES NOT MEANINGFULLY SHARPEN ITSELF. *** A pair exists only when Brennan
replaces a film: ~78 a year against 147 banked, so doubling the evidence takes two
years, and the guards need 8 pairs PER BUCKET so sparse buckets stay sparse. "It
improves with no code change" is technically true and practically useless. THIS IS
THE ARGUMENT THAT MOTIVATED 5.1 — manufacture the pairs instead of waiting.

STILL A FLOOR, DELIBERATELY: 80 of the 129 corrected films have R < 1 even
AFTER their upgrade, so the better copy was itself pinned and one round of
correction under-corrects. It moves the number toward truth; it does not arrive.

WHY THE PAIRS SCATTER LOOKS RANDOM, and it nearly is: Spearman(old R, ratio) is
-0.333 (in-fit) / -0.410 (all pairs), within-bucket ranges like x1.00-x2.91. The
outcome is dominated by a variable the fit excludes — how big the upgrade was.
Spearman(bitrate growth, ratio) = +0.759, more than twice the predictor in use.
Indexing on R alone is right at SCORING time (a film on disk has an R and no
future copy) but wrong at FITTING time. Exactly why the controlled experiment
beats the pairs.

THE CONTINUOUS CROSS-CHECK: complexity_ratio ~ (srcNew/srcOld)^beta. beta is
reliably positive and around a third but NOT pinned — four defensible inclusion
rules on the same 51 pairs give 0.30, 0.32, 0.33, 0.41; bootstrap 95% CI
0.217-0.436 (n=35); leave-one-out tight (0.293-0.347), so no single pair drives
it; trimming outliers pulls it DOWN. Paris, Texas alone measured 0.47.
*** DO NOT QUOTE A TWO-DECIMAL beta. *** An earlier edition reported "beta =
0.351, R2 0.328" as settled; it is one draw from that interval. Extrapolating each
R to R_TRUST with beta = 0.351 predicts x1.876 / x1.542 / x1.411 / x1.276 /
x1.129 / x1.031 at R = 0.20 / 0.35 / 0.45 / 0.60 / 0.85 / 1.10 — agreeing with the
buckets within ~10% in the middle (R 0.4-1.1, where nearly all pairs live) and
diverging hard below R ~ 0.3, where BOTH are extrapolating (only 1 of 147 pairs
has an old R below 0.2).

A 2-VARIABLE MODEL DOES NOT RESCUE IT, worth knowing before anyone tries: adding
log(oldR) alongside log(growth) raises R2 by 0.033, oldR is not significant
(t=1.64, df=32), and its SIGN FLIPS between the 1- and 2-variable fits. Growth and
oldR correlate -0.657 — worse-pinned films got bigger upgrades, a SELECTION effect
from which films Brennan chose to replace, not physics.

SPLITTING THE FIT BY GRAININESS: ATTEMPTED, INCONCLUSIVE. Splitting on OLD
complexity gives grainy beta 0.378 (n=18) vs clean 0.282 (n=17) — the direction
the grain-absorption worry predicts — but bootstrap CIs overlap almost entirely
(0.21-0.51 vs 0.15-0.45), and splitting on NEW complexity gives 0.277 vs 0.009. A
gap that inverts on an arbitrary methodological choice is not a finding.

5.4 THE GRAIN CONTAMINATION WORRY (open question 3)                 [UNTESTED]
The OBSERVATIONAL fit cannot separate pinning from grain: when a starved copy of a
grainy film is replaced by a better transfer, complexity rises both because more
detail is present and because more grain is resolved. Some grain may therefore be
absorbed into a coefficient labelled "source pinning".
5.1 IS LARGELY IMMUNE TO THIS, a real advantage of the controlled method: the same
film at three starvation levels has the same grain by construction, so what it
measures is starvation. If the two methods agree where they overlap, the
contamination in the observational fit is bounded.
Closing it needs a grain covariate that is NOT raw complexity — raw complexity is
what the grain question is ABOUT and cannot also be its control. gridRatio (9.2)
is the candidate.

WORTH KNOWING: the pairs are not reproducible. The superseded copy is deleted at
swap time, so a prior only holds what PRIOR_FIELDS (probe.js) captured when it was
banked. Any new per-pair measurement must be added there BEFORE the next upgrades
land, or those pairs simply won't have it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. THE CRF LADDER PILOT                          [SOLID, 20 films, 2026-08-18]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Four rungs (CRF 16/20/24/28), 4 samples each, identical scenes at every rung.
Tooling: controller/scripts/probe-ladder.sh + scripts/ladder-pilot.js; data in
data/ladder-pilot.json. COST, measured not guessed: ~10 min/film, ~3.5 h for the
pilot. Library-wide would be ~170 h, which is why it is a pilot.

FINDING 1 — THE RELATIONSHIP IS A CLEAN EXPONENTIAL. NO KNEES.
    R2 >= 0.977 on every film, median 0.998. Max |curvature| 0.008.
log(bitrate) against CRF is a straight line for all 20, across the full range of
content from Arrival (complexity 0.028) to 12 Angry Men (1.09). The
-15%-per-CRF-point form is the right SHAPE.

FINDING 2 — THE SLOPE VARIES ~2x BETWEEN FILMS, AND IT IS NOT NOISE.
    median -13.6%/point, range -9.9% to -19.4%, SD 2.98 points
Because each individual fit is precise, that spread is real content variation,
not measurement error. The library-wide constant should be -13.6%, not -15.0%.

FINDING 3 — SLOPE TRACKS SUPPLY ACROSS THE LIBRARY. *** BUT SEE 11.5. ***
    Spearman(slope, supply R)   -0.750
    Spearman(slope, biasFactor) +0.736
    Spearman(slope, src Mb/s)   -0.731
    Spearman(slope, complexity) +0.308   <- barely anything
    Spearman(slope, year)       +0.121   <- nothing

    shallowest: Blair Witch (14% supplied) -9.9%,  Tangerine (26%) -10.0%
    steepest:   Casablanca (157%) -19.4%,  Asteroid City (163%) -18.5%,
                Parasite (558%) -17.8%

This LOOKED like a direct, non-circular instrument for source exhaustion, and it
was the most promising result of the ladder. THE STARVATION EXPERIMENT KILLED IT
(section 11.5): holding content constant, slope predicts pinning at +0.335 while
R predicts it at -0.924. The library-wide correlation was cross-film confounding,
not a measurement of exhaustion.

WHAT SURVIVES: findings 1 and 2. The rate curve has no knees and the per-CRF
constant is -13.6%. THE SLOPE IS NOT AN INPUT TO BPP+ and a 2x spread around a
constant that only sets a read-time conversion does not justify 170 h of
encoding. Do not build the library-wide ladder cron (section 16.B.2).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.5 WHAT A BPP+ DISTANCE MEANS — THE SCALE IN JND UNITS          [DIRECTION]
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
"Where does 100 sit" has been treated as a missing MEASUREMENT and every plan to
close it has been a plan to measure a person. It is better read as a missing
UNIT. The scale is already monotone and already anchored to a physical encode; it
just has no perceptual reading. Supply one and the human's remaining job shrinks
from calibrating a scale to CHOOSING AN ORIGIN.

THE CHAIN, four links, two ours and two borrowed:
  1. BPP+ -> bitrate ratio.  EXACT, by definition: ratio = (BPP+/100)^2.
  2. ratio -> dCRF.          MEASURED HERE. The 20-film ladder pilot: median
                             -13.6% of bitrate per CRF point, per-film -9.9% to
                             -19.4%, R^2 >= 0.977, no knees anywhere (section 6).
  3. dCRF -> JND.            BORROWED, AND THE WEAK LINK. JND-aware per-title
                             ladder work spaces rungs ~6 VMAF apart and calls
                             that one JND, ~2-3 CRF points near transparency.
                             Taken as 1 JND = 2.5 CRF, bracketed 2-3.
  4. origin check.           VMAF 100 is DEFINED at 1080p x264 CRF 22, and
                             community mappings put x265 CRF 20 inside x264
                             CRF 17-23 — so OUR anchor sits within roughly +/-1
                             JND of the industry's own perceptual-lossless
                             definition. A check on the origin, not arithmetic.

Generated by scripts/jnd-table.js (re-run it if the ladder slope is re-measured):

    BPP+  bits vs target  CRF-equiv  JND from transparent (typical film)  across films  reading
    30    9%              36.5       5.5–8.2 below                        3.7–11.5      library minimum
    40    16%             32.5       4.2–6.3 below                        2.8–8.8
    50    25%             29.5       3.2–4.7 below                        2.1–6.6
    60    36%             27.0       2.3–3.5 below                        1.6–4.9
    64    41%             26.4       2.1–3.2 below                        1.5–4.4       LIBRARY MEDIAN (movies)
    70    49%             24.9       1.6–2.4 below                        1.1–3.4
    75    56%             23.9       1.3–2.0 below                        0.9–2.8       bad / warn edge
    90    81%             21.4       0.5–0.7 below                        0.3–1.0
    100   100%            20.0       0                                    —             THE ANCHOR — CRF 20, transparent
    110   121%            18.7       0.4–0.7 above                        0.4–0.6
    125   156%            16.9       1.0–1.5 above                        1.0–1.4       ok / wow edge
    150   225%            14.5       1.8–2.8 above                        1.9–2.6
    200   400%            10.5       3.2–4.7 above                        3.2–4.4
    231   534%            8.5        3.8–5.7 above                        3.9–5.4       library maximum

TWO UNCERTAINTIES, DELIBERATELY NOT POOLED. "typical film" carries only the
JND-per-CRF bracket at the median slope; "across films" shows how far the
per-film slope spread moves it. Pooling them produces a band so wide it says
nothing.

*** THE CROSS-CHECK THAT MAKES THIS WORTH KEEPING. *** Brennan said, unprompted
and long before this arithmetic existed, that "dropping from a 100 to a 70
represents a significant drop". The chain puts 100->70 at 1.6-2.4 JND — several
plainly visible steps. IT WAS NOT FITTED TO THAT JUDGEMENT AND IT REPRODUCES IT.
That is one point of agreement, not a validation, but it is the only external
check the scale has ever passed.

WHAT IT SAYS ABOUT OPEN QUESTION 2. The library median 64 sits 2.1-3.2 JND below
transparent — a large, obvious deficit, not a subtle one. That favours reading
(a) (the library really is starved) over (b) (the anchor is set too high), and it
agrees with Easy Rider at BPP+ 78 being visibly full of grain-shimmer. It does
NOT settle it: link 3 is literature, and its mapping error (+/-2-3 CRF points) is
honestly LARGER than one person's judgement precision. Which is the point —
what remains is a single offset for a human to choose, and no further arithmetic
answers it.

NOT A SCORE INPUT. Nothing here changes a single number in the app. If Brennan
ever does move the origin, the mechanism is HEADROOM_TARGET arithmetic and no
re-probing: x265 is ~-13.6%/CRF point, so a preference is a multiplier.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. WHAT THE LIBRARY ACTUALLY SCORES (2026-08-20, after the 5.2a audio-R fix)
AFTER the 2026-08-20 pinning refit (5.2). MOVIES, 898 with files, every one scored:
    min 30 | p10 46 | p25 52 | MEDIAN 64 | p75 81 | p90 107 | max 231
    bad (<75) 606 67.5% | warn (75-99) 180 20.0% | ok (100-124) 76 8.5%
    wow (>=125) 36 4.0%
    R: min 0.14 | p25 0.43 | median 0.57 | p75 0.80 | max 5.34

SEASONS, 146 (every one scored — see the coverage note below):
    min 42 | p10 59 | p25 67 | MEDIAN 86 | p75 113 | p90 139 | max 194
    bad 49 33.6% | warn 46 31.5% | ok 31 21.2% | wow 20 13.7%
    R: min 0.29 | p25 0.61 | median 0.89 | p75 1.29 | max 3.77

FOR REFERENCE, the same movie profile the day before the refit: median 60, p75 74,
p90 97, bad 674 (75.1%). The refit moved 810 films up a median of 5 points and
flipped 106 bands, ALL UPWARD — nothing moved down. It changed no measurement.

TV IS A DIFFERENT POPULATION AND SHOULD NOT BE POOLED WITH FILM: median 79 vs 60,
median R 0.89 vs 0.57, and 13.7% "wow" against 3.1%. Modern digital episodic
content is cheap to encode and web releases are not size-capped the way a Blu-ray
rip of a grainy catalogue film is.

*** 65% OF TV CARRIED NO SCORE AT ALL UNTIL 2026-08-20, AND NOBODY NOTICED. ***
95 of 146 seasons had bpp = null — not a loose number, NO number. Cause: probe.js
had its own secs() that demanded H:MM:SS, while *arr reports "30:55" for a
sub-hour episode. It parsed as 0, the size-derived fallback bitrate went null,
bppOf() returned null. Movies were immune because they are always over an hour,
so the gap was invisible on the tab that gets looked at. audit.js has a SECOND
secs() that pads short forms correctly — two parsers for one field, disagreeing,
is what hid it. THE LESSON IS THE COVERAGE AUDIT, NOT THE REGEX: check how many
units carry a null score, per kind, whenever this document's numbers are re-run.

*** READ THIS BEFORE DRAWING CONCLUSIONS. *** The library MEDIAN is 64, not 100.
Two readings, and we do not know which is right — the biggest open calibration
question (open question 2):
  (a) The library really is mostly below CRF-20-transparent. Plausible: 1080p
      web/Bluray encodes of grainy catalogue films genuinely are starved.
  (b) HEADROOM_TARGET = 1.0 sets the bar above Brennan's actual knee. The probe
      reports `headroomLive` = 0.5736 (what the data implies) against
      `headroomTarget` = 1.0 (what scoring USES). A frozen median-based anchor of
      0.5449 (n=150, "median-of-measured") sits in the cache with applied: FALSE,
      frozen: TRUE — deliberately never auto-applied, because an auto-anchor makes
      the index track the library instead of measuring it. Adopting ~0.57 would
      move the median from 60 to ~81.
Resolving this needs SUBJECTIVE judgement, not more math — and the one attempt
produced an invalid label (11.1), so the protocol must be fixed first. Note per
1.2(e) that this constant moves EVERY score together, so it barely touches
ranking: it is a labelling question, not a decision-quality one. Until it is
settled: READ THE RANKING, NOT THE ABSOLUTE NUMBER.

7.1 BY SOURCE TIER (median BPP+)
    Bluray-1080p 727/60 | Bluray-720p 68/52 | WEBDL-1080p 30/87
    WEBRip-1080p 19/65  | WEBRip-720p 18/60 | WEBDL-720p 15/69
    Remux-1080p   4/67  <- NOT the top of the table | SDTV 2/81 | DVD 1/82
That Remux and SDTV do not sort where you expect is the whole reason source tier
is kept as a SEPARATE GATE and never folded into BPP+ (section 10).

7.2 THE SUPPLY IS QUANTISED — MOST OF THE LIBRARY SITS ON THREE PRESETS  [SOLID]
    (measured 2026-08-19 from /api/probe/dataset, 885 movies)
    ~2.1 Mb/s   335 films (38%)   median 1.96 GB   315 of them "Bluray-1080p"
    ~0.85 Mb/s   59 films ( 7%)   median 0.69 GB    52 of them "Bluray-720p"
    ~6.2 Mb/s    98 films (11%)   median 5.05 GB    94 of them "Bluray-1080p"
That is 56% of the movie library on three release-group SIZE targets. A single
0.2 Mb/s bin at 2.2 Mb/s holds 206 films on its own.

WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. For that 38%, the NUMERATOR of BPP+ is
effectively a CONSTANT, so the score reduces to ~100*sqrt(k/complexity) — very
nearly a pure inverse-complexity ranking, with almost none of its variation
coming from anything about our copy. Two consequences:
  - The "horizontal bands" in the lab's complexity-vs-bitrate scatter are this,
    not a measurement artifact. There is no trend to find because the supply side
    does not respond to content at all.
  - For preset-encoded films, "is this file adequate" is answered almost entirely
    by how expensive the content is. Grainy catalogue titles on the 2.1 Mb/s
    preset are guaranteed to score badly, and correctly so — but AS A CLASS, which
    is worth remembering before treating 572 red rows as 572 separate decisions.
Encoding that ignores content is exactly what per-title work exists to replace
(section 17, Netflix). We are on the consuming end of it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. HOW IT RATES SPECIFIC TITLES — THE INTERESTING CASES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8.1 TOP (highest BPP+): 2036: Nexus Dawn 231 (0.5GB, short) | Parasite 218
(13.3GB) | 2048: Nowhere to Run 206 (short) | Blade Runner 2049 191 (12.9GB) |
Inglourious Basterds 190 | The Father 183 | Train Dreams 182 (WEBDL) | American
Psycho 170 | Pulp Fiction 169 | Sinners 163.
Almost all clean, modern or well-transferred digital images given a generous disc
encode. Parasite at 13.5 Mb/s against complexity 0.0651 is the extreme: R = 5.58.

8.2 BOTTOM (lowest BPP+): The Blair Witch Project 30 (0.7GB, 720p) | Super
Troopers 34 | Killer of Sheep 34 | Hero 35 | Sympathy for Mr. Vengeance 35 |
Happy Gilmore 2 36 | Great Expectations (1946) 37 | Persona 37 | Austin Powers
37 | Spies (1928) 37.
NOTE BLAIR WITCH: deliberately degraded 16mm/Hi8, measured complexity 0.2704, its
1.1 Mb/s copy scores 30. The score is arguably correct (the file cannot reproduce
what is on the negative) and useless (no upgrade will make it look "good"). This
class — intentionally ugly photography — is a known blind spot with no handling.

8.3 HUNGRIEST CONTENT (highest complexity): 12 Angry Men 0.9270 (R 0.74, 24.5
GB Remux) | City of Men 0.6765 | Killer of Sheep 0.6336 | Beach Party 0.4502 |
The General 0.4364 | sex, lies, and videotape 0.4318 | Autumn Sonata 0.4255 |
The World's Fastest Indian 0.3760 | Casino Royale (1954) 0.3747.
Black-and-white with heavy grain dominates. 12 Angry Men is the headline probe
result: a 24.5 GB REMUX that still only scores BPP+ 67, because its grain
genuinely costs that much. A flat denominator called this file lavish.

8.4 CHEAPEST CONTENT: The Father 0.0346 | 12th Fail 0.0363 | Oppenheimer 0.0376
| Arrival 0.0381 | BlackBerry 0.0437 | Beginners 0.0443 | The Beast 0.0452 |
Office Space 0.0455. Arrival is 24x cheaper than 12 Angry Men.

8.5 MOST OVER-SUPPLIED (highest R): Parasite 5.58 | 2036 5.43 | 2048 4.30 |
BR2049 4.27 | Inglourious Basterds 4.22 | Train Dreams 3.97 | The Father 3.61 |
American Psycho 3.49 | Pulp Fiction 3.39 | Sinners 2.81.
MOST STARVED (lowest R): Blair Witch 0.14 | Killer of Sheep 0.19 | Hero 0.20
| Spies 0.22 | Persona 0.22 | Austin Powers 0.22 | Saving Private Ryan 0.23 |
Monty Python's Meaning of Life 0.23 | Sympathy for Mr. Vengeance 0.24.

8.6 BPP+ PER GB — WHERE THE DISK GOES (a DISK metric, not a quality one)
Worst (>=1.5 GB only): 12 Angry Men 67/24.5GB = 2.7/GB | LOTR Two Towers
99/26.8GB = 3.7 | Lawrence of Arabia 118/28.3GB = 4.2 | Schindler's List
45/9.0GB = 5.0 | Apocalypse Now 94/17.6GB = 5.3 | Taxi Driver 65/11.6GB = 5.6 |
Casino 150/25.9GB = 5.8.           Best: Arrival 110/1.9GB = 58.0 | Punch-Drunk
Love 98/1.7GB HEVC = 56.9 | HP D.H. Part 1 94/1.7GB = 56.0 | I Swear
101/1.8GB = 55.6.
Long runtimes and grainy negatives sit at the bottom by construction — correct,
not a defect. Used to reweight disk-space suggestions, never to rank quality.

8.7 DOES BPP+ OVER-REWARD GRAIN? NO — IT PENALISES IT.   [SOLID, 2026-08-19]
The worry is natural: complexity is largely grain cost, so a grainy film gets a
big denominator and a big file could look rewarded for reproducing grain nobody
asked for. The data says the opposite. By complexity quartile, 885 movies:

    Q1 (cleanest)  median cx 0.073   live BPP+ 71   (flat-denominator equiv 63)
    Q4 (grainiest) median cx 0.198   live BPP+ 55   (flat-denominator equiv 94)
    Q1: 61% "bad", 6% "wow"          Q4: 73% "bad", 1% "wow"

THE SIGN FLIP REMAINS, WEAKER AT RAW LEVEL (see 11.2 note on labels; the 2026-08-13
blind-test retraction removed the magnitudes, the direction survives on measured
complexity):
    Spearman(BPP+ under flat 0.13, complexity) = +0.442  <- rewards grain
    Spearman(BPP+ live,            complexity) = -0.328  <- penalises it

The specific failure mode — a grainy film ALSO generously supplied — has ZERO
instances. Of the 26 movies with complexity >= 0.30, the highest R is 1.09 and
none reaches "wow". R and complexity correlate -0.31 library-wide: grainy titles
here are systematically the most STARVED, not the most indulged.

So no formula change. If one were ever warranted it should be a DISPLAY
ANNOTATION, not a term (which would corrupt a number that means one clean thing)
and not a gate (ordinary grain is not a ceiling — 12 Angry Men CAN reach 100 with
a bigger file, unlike the Blair Witch class). Building it needs a grain metric
that survives the CGI and mean-luma degeneracy tests that killed gShare (11.3),
which does not exist.
THE TRIGGER TO REVISIT is cheap: watch the (complexity, R) plane. A title
migrating into "high grain cost AND R > 1.2" is the case this concern describes.
None exists today.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9. GRAIN — WHAT IS MEASURED, WHAT IS NOT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9.1 GRAIN BOILING IS REAL AND WAS MEASURED HERE.              [DIRECTION, n=2]
Brennan reported "random noise buzzing and flickering" in bright flat areas (Star
Wars dunes and sky, Blues Brothers, Jackie Brown, Jurassic Park, Taxi Driver,
Easy Rider skies). Two independent discriminators (scripts/probe-noise-origin.sh
+ analyze-noise-origin.js), bright pixels only, scene cuts excluded:

                                  Star Wars 9:19    No Other Choice (2025)
                                  (artifacts)       ("artifact and grain free")
    pbFloor  noise per frame          2.64              1.78
    iRatio   I-frame diff / P-B diff  1.01              0.44
    gridRatio across/inside 8x8 blk   1.165             0.995
    source bitrate                   10.0 Mb/s          2.0 Mb/s
    R                                 2.45              0.33

TEMPORALLY IT IS GRAIN — renewed every frame, no jump at keyframes. Compression
noise freezes inside a GOP and cannot do that. SPATIALLY IT CARRIES THE CODEC'S
8x8 FINGERPRINT — 1.165 against a 0.995 clean baseline. Grain knows nothing about
the DCT grid, so the encoder added that.

GRAIN BOILING: the film's own random pattern, quantised through a DCT at a
bitrate that cannot carry it, so each block approximates its grain slightly
differently. It explains every observation — bright flat areas are where grain
lives unmasked, and it "buzzes" because the pattern really is renewing.

WHY HE HAD NEVER SEEN IT: the 720p projector downscaled every 1080p file ~44%, an
effective grain filter. He saw it first on a phone at native resolution. The
1080p projector removes that mask (section 2.3).

WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH. These are instrument readings, so they
survive the retraction in 11.1. What does NOT survive is the inference once built
on top: that grain contamination explained R's 0.146 against the blind-test
verdicts. Those verdicts were grain labels, and R scoring ~0 against a grain
label is R behaving correctly. GRAIN IS NOT ESTABLISHED AS BPP+'S CENTRAL
PROBLEM. It is a physical finding on two clips, not yet connected to any quality
judgement. The only defect demonstrated under control is source pinning.

n=2 IS A DIRECTION, NOT A COEFFICIENT. Easy Rider failed as a third control: 245
of 249 frames were dropped as camera motion. The measurement needs STATIC BRIGHT
scenes, which is the real obstacle to running it library-wide.

9.2 gridRatio IS THE BEST REMAINING ARTIFACT CANDIDATE.            [UNTESTED]
On theory it isolates the part more bits CAN fix, where blockMean measures the
part they cannot. Both are untested against a valid label, so this is a reason to
test gridRatio FIRST, not a result.

9.3 MORE BITS REDUCE BOILING BUT THE GRAIN STAYS. No upgrade makes Star Wars look
like No Other Choice. Same class as Blair Witch (8.2): a narrow blind spot around
films where bits cannot help.

9.4 THE DEMONSTRATION OF HOW MUCH OF THESE FILES IS GRAIN. ~/grain-test/ holds
the same 24 seconds of Star Wars as (A) untouched source at 24 Mb/s, (B) CRF-14
re-encode at 34 Mb/s, (C) denoised then CRF-14 at 23 Mb/s. C IS SMALLER THAN THE
SOURCE despite CRF 14 — removing grain removed most of the bitrate.
THE HEAT PROBLEM, the other side of it: Heat, complexity 0.059, the blurriest of
the eleven at 1.7 Mb/s, which Brennan independently described as "a little
under-resolutioned, not totally in focus". Grain scrubbed at the transfer, detail
scrubbed with it. Clip C is a deliberately Heat-ified Star Wars. Aggressive
denoising takes detail with the grain; this is also Norkin's standard warning.

9.5 THE DETECTORS ARE NOW GRADED, WITHOUT LABELS.       [MEASURED, 2026-08-25]
A detector can be graded on REPEATED READINGS alone — split its per-clip readings
in half, average each half, correlate the halves across films, Spearman-Brown back
to full length. If it cannot agree with ITSELF on two samples of the same film it
cannot be measuring a property of the film. blockMean and blurMean sat ungraded
for months on the false belief that this needed a subjective label; probe-film.sh
computed the per-clip readings all along and averaged them away. Since 2026-08-21
it emits them. scripts/grade-detector.js:

    detector      n    clips  split-half  Spearman-Brown   vs cx    vs BPP+
    blockMean     71     9.0     0.856        0.922       -0.000    +0.080
    blurMean      71     9.0     0.367        0.537       -0.233    -0.009
    cambi        247     8.0     0.643        0.782       -0.208    -0.347
    complexity   256    17.7     0.915        0.955          --     -0.365

blockMean IS THE MOST RELIABLE ARTIFACT DETECTOR WE HAVE — more reliable than
CAMBI, which shipped. It also passes every degeneracy test available: complexity
-0.000, BPP+ +0.080, log bpp +0.063, resolution +0.167. For scale, gShare died at
-0.885 against mean luma (11.3).

THIS DOES NOT CONTRADICT THE 2026-08-17 KILL, and the distinction matters. What
was killed was blockMean as a SCORE TERM (wBPP+ = x(1.33/blockMean)^0.40, spearman
-0.090 against the blind-test verdicts) — and those verdicts were later retracted
anyway (11.1). Grading a DETECTOR asks a different question: is the reading a
stable property of the film. It is. Whether it is a property worth scoring is
still open.

THE LUMA TEST COULD NOT BE RUN — only 2 of the 71 units carry a luma reading,
because the block/blur population (recently re-probed) and the banding population
(the nightly backfill) are still nearly disjoint. Luma is the exact axis gShare
died on, so this grade is INCOMPLETE until the two populations overlap. Do not
promote blockMean past "reliable" before that test runs.

AND RELIABILITY IS NECESSARY, NOT SUFFICIENT. A misspecified detector repeats
itself too. The discriminating test is whether the reading MOVES when bits are
removed: an artifact detector must rise under starvation, a content statistic sits
flat. That is a ladder question, which is why banding-ladder.js now chains
blockdetect/blurdetect ahead of libvmaf and measures all three off one decode —
the starvation encode is the expensive part and it was already paid for.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
10. WHAT BPP+ IS DELIBERATELY BLIND TO — THE GATES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BPP+ answers exactly one question: "does this file have enough bits for its own
content". Everything below is a CEILING on quality that bits cannot raise, so
each is a GATE beside the score rather than a term inside it (constraint 1.2d).

10.1 SOURCE TIER
    SRC_RANK: remux 5 | bluray/brrip/bdrip 4 | webdl 4 | webrip 3 | hdtv 2
              | dvd 1 | sdtv/cam/telesync 0
A perfectly-encoded HDTV capture can score BPP+ 120 and still be a worse master
than a starved Bluray. The Disk section refuses any candidate whose effective
rank is below what you already hold.

10.2 RE-ENCODES ARE A FULL TIER DOWNGRADE
*arr reports BRRip/BDRip as plain "Bluray", so the rank was blind to them.
    REENC_RE = /brrip|bdrip|web-?rip|hdrip/i ; REENC_TIER_COST = 1
    effSrcRank = srcRank - (isReenc ? 1 : 0)
A full tier is honest: BRRip is disc -> rip -> re-encode, the same generation
count as a WEBRip (stream -> re-encode), and WEBRip already ranks 3.
MEASURED: 120 of 885 probed movie files (13.6%) match on their ON-DISK path, 82
of those labelled "Bluray-*". Detecting on *arr's `originalFilePath` (the
pre-rename release name, which survives renaming) raises it to 187 of 886 (21%),
110 labelled "Bluray-1080p" — including Forrest Gump, Saving Private Ryan, Back
to the Future, Indiana Jones. The on-disk path undercounts; prefer the *arr field.
Consequences, all live:
  - Disk section refuses re-encodes outright (that section assumes "same master,
    fewer wasted bits"). Verified: 0 re-encode candidates across 29 rows / 263
    candidates.
  - Playback/source upgrades can now see through the label: replacing a BRRip
    with a genuine disc encode used to score 4-against-4 and register as no
    upgrade at all.
  - Upgrade section admits a re-encode regardless of its BPP+. Only 6 genuinely
    new rows, since 181 of the 187 were already under the BPP+ 100 gate. The 18
    now flagged include Donnie Darko at BPP+ 101, which the bitrate gate would
    have hidden forever.
Both sides of every comparison use the same effective rank, so holding a BRRip
does not refuse you another one as a "downgrade from itself".

10.3 AUDIO LAYOUT. A 1.5 GB encode that dropped 5.1 Atmos for stereo AAC is a
downgrade no bitrate ratio can see. AUDIO_RANK is a separate comparison in
candidate ranking. (Note the playback ground truth: everything ends as stereo in
this room, so lossless/multichannel is not treated as quality — audioCls()
deliberately returns '' for everything.)

10.4 EDITION / IMAX. Curated lists, not measurements. IMAX_BEST is keyed
"title year" (e.g. 'dune 2021') across ~15 titles where sequences were genuinely
shot large-format. WHY A LIST AND NOT ASPECT RATIO: open matte and pan-and-scan
BOTH move a 2.39 film toward 1.78, but the first ADDS picture and the second
CROPS the sides off; without knowing intended framing, a 1.78 copy of a scope
film is as likely to be vandalism as a gift. IMAX_AR_MAX = 2.0 suppresses false
positives on films already framed at 1.78. "IMAX Enhanced" is matched SEPARATELY
and NOT trusted as framing — it is a DTS/HDR licensing badge. The list is keyed
with YEAR because normEdTitle() strips "(year)", so a bare 'dune' key matched
both Villeneuve's Dune (2021) and Lynch's Dune (1984). IMAX PREFERENCE NEVER
HARD-BLOCKS; it only reorders. Maintain alongside controller/oscar-winners.json
and film-awards.json.

10.5 4K IS NEVER AN UPGRADE. Nothing in the house plays above 1080p. 2160p
candidates are excluded regardless of score.

10.5a BANDING — NOW MEASURABLE, AND IT IS A REAL SECOND AXIS      [SOLID, 2026-08-20]
Banding is the one artifact BPP+ is STRUCTURALLY blind to: flat gradients
quantised into visible steps, which a file can suffer while being perfectly
adequate on average. It was unmeasurable here until 2026-08-20.

WHY THE "NO MASTER" CONSTRAINT DOES NOT BIND. CAMBI is genuinely NO-REFERENCE —
it keys on flat regions within a single frame. Only libvmaf's plumbing wants two
inputs, so both are pointed at the same file. Banding is therefore measurable in
PRODUCTION, forever, with no master. VMAF proper is NOT usable that way (a
same-file comparison scores ~100 by construction) and stays calibration-only.

*** THE TOOL IS A SECOND ffmpeg AND MUST STAY SEPARATE. *** Nothing here has
libvmaf (controller 5.1, jellyfin 7.1.4 has vmafmotion only, host 4.2), so
scripts/setup-vmaf-tool.sh fetches a PINNED static build into tools/. NEVER use it
for complexity and never "fix" the controller by upgrading its ffmpeg: that x265
made all 1044 complexity measurements and is the only ruler for them.

FIRST NEW SIGNAL TO SURVIVE ITS OWN DEGENERACY TESTS. 40 films, stratified by
complexity AND R (all 26 grain-heavy films are also starved, so complexity-only
sampling would make grain and supply inseparable). Thresholds fixed before the
numbers were read; |r| > 0.6 would have failed it:
    vs complexity (grain)     -0.245 pearson / -0.392 spearman   passes
    vs mean luma (darkness)   -0.254 / -0.295                    passes
    vs BPP+ (redundancy)      -0.313 / -0.322                    passes
For scale, gShare died at -0.885 against luma (11.3).

AND THE MECHANISM IS THERE, not just independence. Among the SMOOTH half, starved
copies band 2.33x more than supplied ones (median cambi 2.682 vs 1.151) — banding
tracks starvation on exactly the content where banding is physically possible.

IT DISAGREES WITH BPP+ IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which is the point:
    The Graduate      BPP+ 75 warn, cambi 4.13 — adequate on bits, bands anyway
    Army of Shadows   BPP+ 81 warn, cambi 3.36 — same
    Bridge on the River Kwai  BPP+ 67 bad, cambi 0.033 — starved, but bits are the
                      ONLY problem, so a replacement would actually fix it
Grain dithers gradients away, which is why the grainiest tercile bands LEAST
(median 0.936 vs 2.087 mid). The relationship is NON-MONOTONIC and the smooth
tercile banding less than the middle (1.467 vs 2.087) is UNEXPLAINED.

PREVALENCE, from a second TARGETED run of 45 more films restricted to BPP+ >= 75
(a random sample mostly draws films the score already condemns, and those get
replaced regardless of banding):
    OF 58 FILMS BPP+ CALLS ACCEPTABLE, 8 BAND VISIBLY = 14% (95% CI ~5-23%)
    warn 75-99  6/34   ok 100-124  2/17   wow >=125  0/7
Rate falls with the score, which is the sanity check it needed. Two of the eight
are in the GREEN band with supply ABOVE transparency — Fargo (BPP+ 109, R 1.22)
and Obsession (BPP+ 103, R 1.12). No bitrate reasoning reaches those: they have
the bits and they still band.

THE BLIND SPOT IS SMOOTH CONTENT, as the mechanism predicts: banding films have
median complexity 0.058 against 0.102 for clean ones, 6 of 8 below 0.10. Grain
dithers gradients away; smooth modern digital has nothing to hide the steps.

AND THE DEGENERACY TESTS REPLICATE on those 45 films, which were never used to set
any threshold: vs complexity -0.444, vs luma -0.022, vs BPP+ -0.273. LUMA COMES
BACK AT -0.02 — the darkness degeneracy is dead outright, not merely under the
line. Complexity rises to -0.444: partial dependence, consistent with grain
suppressing banding, still well inside 0.6.

Full report: docs/REPORT-cambi-2026-08-20.md (85 films total).

WHERE IT GOES: a GATE beside the score, never a term (no valid label to weight
it). If it ever enters the score it multiplies the DENOMINATOR, capped ~1.6 —
never additively, because the sqrt then halves a misread to <= ~2 points.

*** SHIPPED AS A NIGHTLY JOB, 2026-08-20 (controller/lib/banding.js). *** It is no
longer a research script: "Audit · banding probe" in the Jobs tab, backfilling the
library at 4x2s clips per unit, manually triggerable for daytime catch-up. The 85
films measured during the research round were imported rather than re-encoded.

IT SHARES THE PROBE'S GATES AND ITS ONE NIGHT BUDGET. Not its own — that is the
safety property: the box never runs more than PROBE_NIGHT_BUDGET_MS of encoding a
night no matter which job spends it. A second budget would silently double the
nightly heat, and 12.4 is why that ceiling exists. Banding takes the window while
the probe has only REFINEMENT left; genuinely fresh complexity work still wins,
because a missing complexity number degrades every score for that title while a
missing banding number degrades nothing.

`cambi` IS null WHEN UNMEASURED, WHICH IS NOT "CLEAN". Most of the library has no
reading yet. Every consumer must distinguish the two or it will assert the whole
unmeasured library is banding-free. In bpp-lab it is a COLOUR and an X-AXIS (not a
filter — filtering answers nothing interesting); unmeasured rows are drawn grey and
are DROPPED from the banding axis rather than coerced to zero, which would pile
~90% of the library on the left edge as "least banding of all".

10.5b CAN BANDING BE FOLDED INTO BPP+? NOT YET, AND THE REASON IS SPECIFIC.
Brennan proposed the obviously right shape — predict EXPECTED banding from the
film's characteristics, and read measured/expected as "needs more bitrate", the
same move BPP+ already makes for bits. Tested 2026-08-21 on 85 films
(scripts/banding-expected.js, docs/REPORT-banding-excess-2026-08-21.md):

  IT WORKS DIRECTIONALLY. Excess tracks supply better than raw banding does
  (R -0.412 -> -0.542, BPP+ -0.416 -> -0.528), and the residual is clean of the
  content it was regressed out of (cxEff -0.004, luma -0.055, year +0.003).

  *** BUT RAW bpp PREDICTS BANDING BETTER (-0.684) THAN BPP+ DOES (-0.416), AND
  THAT IS THE MECHANISM. *** Banding is quantisation coarseness, set by ABSOLUTE
  bits per pixel, not by bits relative to content difficulty. A grainy film needs
  many bits (high complexity, so BPP+ normalises them away) but its grain also
  DITHERS BANDING AWAY. So the per-film normalisation that makes BPP+ right for
  adequacy works AGAINST it for banding. The cxEff coefficient flips sign between
  the two models and says the same thing: -0.50 content-only (complex films band
  less), +0.27 holding bpp fixed (at equal bits, harder content bands more). Both
  true, different questions.
  CONSEQUENCE: banding cannot be folded in by adjusting BPP+'s existing
  denominator, because the denominator is the part that is wrong for banding.

  AND A RATIO OF SMALL NUMBERS IS NOT A SIGNAL. At high bpp expected banding goes
  to ~0, so any reading gives a huge ratio. The illustrative adjustment gave 2001:
  A Space Odyssey (cambi 0.32, BELOW the library median 1.08) the MAXIMUM penalty,
  141 -> 111. That is the model failing, not the film.

  THE OBVIOUS REPAIR ADDS NOTHING: requiring high absolute banding AND positive
  excess flags 16 films; the absolute threshold alone flags the same 16. The guard
  removes 0 of 16.

WHAT IS CONFIRMED IS THE GAP. Among films at similar BPP+, banding still spreads
25-101x — so BPP+ genuinely does not capture it, which is exactly why it is
reported beside the score rather than inside it.

WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE ANSWER, concretely: predict expected banding from the thing
that physically causes banding-proneness — THE FRACTION OF FRAME AREA THAT IS FLAT
OR NEAR-FLAT GRADIENT — instead of proxies like year and luma. CAMBI's internals
already segment flat regions and the same static build ships `siti`. A film that is
40% smooth sky has a different banding expectation from one that is all texture,
and neither year nor luma captures that. If content R^2 rose from 0.15 to ~0.5 the
ratio would stabilise and this section should be revisited. Two cheaper
contributors are already in motion: the banding job is backfilling 960 more units
(a 12x larger sample), and a forced-choice label would let the coefficient be
CALIBRATED rather than invented.

10.5c THE ARTIFACT-RESIDUAL FRAMEWORK — Brennan's idea, and the most promising
      direction open to this model.                          [DIRECTION, 2026-08-21]
Full design: docs/DESIGN-ARTIFACT-RESIDUAL.md.

"At its core BPP+ is saying: is your file big enough to look good. And what does
looking good mean if not that it's free of bad artifacts?" So: predict how much of
an artifact a film OUGHT to show from its content AND its bitrate, measure how much
it does, and let the disagreement CORRECT THE SCORE — up when a copy is cleaner
than one that size should be, down when it is worse. BIDIRECTIONAL: on 85 films,
36 up, 46 down, 3 unchanged. Not a penalty term, not a re-anchoring.

IT IS NOT THE SAME AS 10.5b, which asked whether banding reveals a deficiency to
add to the DENOMINATOR and was rejected. This does not touch the denominator; it
treats the artifact as an INDEPENDENT ESTIMATOR of what BPP+ estimates. That
inverts 10.5b's methodology rule: there the expected-model had to be blind to
bitrate, here seeing bitrate IS the mechanism.

THE MAGNITUDE IS MEASURABLE, WHICH IS THE BEST PART. BPP+ is already denominated in
bits, so if a film bands like a copy carrying bpp' instead of its actual bpp, its
honest score is the score of bpp'. Given S = d log(artifact)/d log(bpp), the
conversion is arithmetic. Two measured guards on top:
  RELIABILITY SHRINKAGE. The residual is signal plus noise; Kelley's correction
  shrinks it by its split-half reliability. Measured 0.579 at 4 clips per film.
  This is what makes the framework safe to EXTEND — a junk detector contributes
  ~nothing instead of contributing garbage, with no subjective label needed.
  ACTED ON: BANDING_SAMPLES 4 -> 8 (reliability 0.73; 12 clips would give 0.80).

*** THE PER-FILM LADDER CHANGED THE ARITHMETIC (5 films, 5 starvation levels). ***
    Skyfall -1.534 R2 0.962 | Confessions -0.910 R2 0.987 | Goldfinger -0.618 R2
    0.996 | E.T. -0.554 R2 0.990 | El Mariachi -0.435 R2 0.691
  Per-film curves are REAL and SMOOTH. Two findings:
  1. EVERY per-film slope is FLATTER than the cross-film -1.78 (mean ~-0.81). The
     cross-film fit overstates sensitivity ~2.2x — the SAME between-vs-within
     confound the pinning work hit when observational pairs disagreed with the
     controlled experiment (5.1). Only a ladder holds content constant.
  2. THE NAIVE 1/|S| SCALING IS BACKWARDS. It gives a FLAT responder a BIGGER
     shift, but if banding barely moves with bitrate then banding is UNINFORMATIVE
     about bitrate. Inverse-variance weighting gives shift ∝ |S|*r, NOT r/|S|.
     That is why E.T., the flattest responder, took the maximum penalty in the
     naive version.

WHERE IT STANDS: the SHAPE is fully measured (direction, per-film |S| scaling,
reliability shrinkage). What remains is ONE overall scale, needing the ratio of
BPP+'s own error variance to the artifact's — which needs one subjective anchor
(question 0). From "an invented coefficient" to "one free parameter for the whole
model" is the real progress here.

NOT DEPLOYED. Under the naive form it moved far too much (median 10.9 points, 29
band flips of 85) and penalised demonstrably clean films — 2001: A Space Odyssey
at cambi 0.32, BELOW the library median, took the maximum penalty. That is the
expected-model failing (R^2 0.488) and the residual absorbing the failure.

IT GENERALISES, and that is the point. Any artifact needs two things in order: a
SPLIT-HALF RELIABILITY check (no label required — this is the unlock) and a MEASURED
slope against bitrate. blockMean/blurMean have been marked UNTESTED for months on
the assumption that judging a detector needs a label; it does not, it needs repeated
readings, which probe-film.sh computed all along and then averaged away. FIXED
2026-08-21: sampleBlock/sampleBlur are now stored and exposed, so both can be graded
by exactly the test that graded CAMBI, at zero extra cost.
And GRAIN fits in the direction people forget: LESS grain than a 1974 film should
have is a denoised or generation-lossed source — a quality LOSS that currently
RAISES the score, because a denoised copy measures as easy content.

THE CAUTION: several corrections must not double-count. Reliability screens each
detector alone; INDEPENDENCE needs checking pairwise. Blocking, blur and banding all
rise together under starvation, so they are unlikely to be independent — the right
treatment is probably one combined residual, not three additive shifts.

10.5d THE CROSS-FILM FORM OF 10.5c IS FALSIFIED.        [MEASURED, 2026-08-25]
At n=247 (the banding backfill, up from 85) the cross-film residual applied as a
BPP+ shift is not merely weak, it is BACKWARDS. Fitting expected banding on
bits + complexity + luma and shifting by the residual:

    k = 0.15   78 of 247 films move >= 10 points   Sunset Boulevard 61 -> 138
    k = 0.30  135 of 247 films move >= 10 points   Sunset Boulevard 61 -> 313

Top movers at both settings: Sunset Boulevard, Return of the Jedi, The Big Sleep,
Full Metal Jacket, Taxi Driver — grainy films, most of them genuinely starved.

TWO CAUSES, and only the second is a bug.
(1) GRAIN PREVENTS BANDING. Grain dithers a gradient, so a grainy film cannot band
    however few bits it has. The 15 films scoring bad (<75) with cambi < 0.25 sit
    at complexity p84-p99 — they are the grainiest files in the library. Measured
    by decade: 1940s median cambi 0.053, 1970s 0.168, 2000s 0.855, 2020s 2.053.
    CAMBI IS NOT FAILING HERE. It correctly reports no visible banding, and a
    dithered gradient genuinely does look better than a posterised one. What does
    not follow is "therefore this file has enough bits" — a grainy starved copy
    fails through a DIFFERENT artifact (grain boiling, 9.1). Easy Rider is the case
    in our own history: BPP+ 78, Brennan put it at "a 60ish, or even lower like a
    40" on grain shimmer, and its banding would have read ~0.
(2) THE LOG FLOOR. log(max(cambi, 1e-4)) sends a zero-banding film to -9.2, so the
    films carrying the LEAST information dominate the fit as a pure artifact of the
    transform.

INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION THAT THE MOVERS ARE WRONG: Sunset Boulevard has R 0.53 —
the probe ran out of source before it ran out of appetite. The supply axis already
says that copy is starved, with no reference to banding at all.

WHAT SURVIVES: banding still grades sensibly as a RANKING (over-threshold rate by
band: wow 0.0%, ok 7.1%, warn 17.2%, bad 20.7% — monotone), and the blind spot is
real (Dune 2021 at BPP+ 110 with cambi 4.99, E.T. at 123 with 3.89, Once Upon a
Time in America at 107 with 3.87, all R > 1). What is dead is the CROSS-FILM
RESIDUAL AS A SHIFT. The per-film ladder does not share the failure mode, because
a film is compared only against re-encodes of itself and its own grain is present
at every rung, so it cancels. See 10.5e.

10.5e LEVEL AND SLOPE ARE DIFFERENT FACTS.          [DIRECTION, n=6, 2026-08-25]
From the per-film ladders, spearman(banding level, |slope|) = -0.886. How much
banding a film HAS is nearly the inverse of how much its banding RESPONDS to bits:

    film                    cambi@1.0   S      reading
    The Big Sleep (1946)       0.086  -2.95   responsive but far below visibility
    The Man Standing Next      6.042  -1.30   visible AND responsive -> bits help
    Almost Famous              3.483  -0.18   visible, flat -> baked into the source
    Swingers                   8.911  -0.22   visible, flat -> baked into the source

THE BIG SLEEP IS THE INVERSION IN ONE LINE: the film that broke the cross-film
model (cambi 0.0017, the log floor, the largest spurious shift) has the steepest
and cleanest curve of the six, R^2 0.986.

AND IT ANSWERS "HOW COULD WE EVER TELL BAKED-IN? WE DONT HAVE THE SOURCE."
(Brennan, 2026-08-21.) The ladder tells you: if compressing FURTHER does not
increase the artifact, it was already there. You do not need the master, you need
the derivative.

n=6. Every statement in 10.5d/e about slopes rests on six films.

10.6 GRAINY AND UNDER-PROVISIONED — DEFINED, MEASURED, NOT YET RENDERED
        grainStarved = complexity >= 0.30 && R <= 1.0
Both terms already exist per row; nothing new is measured. Full analysis in
docs/REPORT-grain-gate-2026-08-20.md.

MEASURED 2026-08-20, after the refit: ALL 26 movies at complexity >= 0.30 qualify.
The maximum R in the entire group is 1.066 (Citizen Kane, the only one above 1.0).
Bands: 18 bad, 8 warn, ZERO ok, ZERO wow — median BPP+ 67.5. STILL not one
adequately-provisioned grain-heavy film on this box, and the refit did not create
one: it lifted the whole group without moving any of it past warn.

WHY IT IS A GATE AND NOT A SCORE CHANGE. The score is right — grain IS screen
content and genuinely costs bits (section 9). What is wrong is the IMPLIED ACTION:
a red band reads as "replace this", and for several of these films no better copy
exists. 12 Angry Men is the proof — complexity 0.927, the library maximum and 8x
the median, on a CRITERION REMUX, the best transfer sold, and it still reads 72.
Taxi Driver at 68 is the same. Without an annotation the Audit tab is telling
Brennan to replace discs that cannot be improved.

SO IT MUST ANNOTATE, NEVER SUPPRESS. Mad Max: Fury Road (0.327) and Flow (0.332)
are in the same set and ARE upgradeable — modern, digital, deliberately textured
rather than photochemically grainy. A gate that hid the group would hide those.

THRESHOLDS ARE ROUND, NOT FITTED, ON PURPOSE. 0.30 is ~3x the library median with
a natural break below it; 1.0 is the definition of transparency. With no valid
subjective label anywhere in this library (11.1), a fitted threshold would be
false precision.

STATUS: the rule and the population are settled; the UI is not. The controller tab
is mobile-first and Brennan's standing instruction is that extra numbers live
"behind the scenes, in analysis, in reports" with ONE corrected number on the tab.
Where this annotation goes is his call, so it waits.

IT ALSO MAKES A CHECKABLE PREDICTION. These 26 are simultaneously the most
expensive content and the least supplied, so the 1080p projector should make them
the most visibly deficient films in the collection. If it does not, something in
section 9 is wrong.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
11. DEAD ENDS AND RETRACTIONS — READ BEFORE PROPOSING ANYTHING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Five things were believed and are not any more. Each is kept because a reader
would otherwise repeat it.

11.1 THE UNPAIRED BLIND TEST — RETRACTED (run 2026-08-13, invalidated 08-18)
Eleven films Brennan had ACTUALLY WATCHED, judged on a phone at native
resolution, scored 1 = artifacts / 0.5 = maybe / 0 = clean. Spearman vs the eye:
    src Mb/s 0.905 | complexity 0.824 | R 0.146 | blurMean -0.266 | blockMean -0.090
WHAT WENT WRONG: what Brennan was scoring as "artifacts" was GRAIN. Nobody can
separate the two by eye from a single UNPAIRED clip — which is why subjective
standards use paired or reference-anchored presentation, not "rate this one".
THE TABLE INVERTS ON THAT READING: src Mb/s 0.905 is near-tautological against a
grain label (high-bitrate files reproduce grain faithfully, and grainy catalogue
films get generous disc encodes — both push the same way); complexity 0.824 the
same, since complexity is very largely grain cost (8.3); and R 0.146 is the ONLY
one of the three that is NOT a grain proxy, because R divides grain out of the
numerator AND the denominator. So R scoring ~0 here is R behaving as it should.
An earlier edition called 0.146 "the model's central problem" — withdrawn.
WHAT IS LEFT STANDING: nothing. The test neither validates nor falsifies BPP+.
TWO CONSEQUENCES THAT MUST NOT BE LOST:
  - THERE IS STILL NO VALID SUBJECTIVE LABEL ANYWHERE IN THIS LIBRARY. Every
    fitted weight, band edge and calibration constant is therefore unvalidated.
  - THERE IS NO CONFIRMED CASE of a film judged genuinely compression-damaged.
    Every artifact report on record resolved to grain. BPP+ has never been shown
    to misrank anything: THE HONEST STATE OF THE MODEL IS UNTESTED, NOT BROKEN.
The durable lesson is procedural and is now trap 8.

11.2 SSIM AND VMAF DO NOT SOLVE GRAIN — AND SSIM IS DISQUALIFIED OUTRIGHT
Both over-reward grain reproduction. Grain is stochastic, so its pixels land
differently in source and encode, and pixelwise full-reference metrics penalise
that. On the grainy catalogue titles that matter, an SSIM ladder KEEPS CLIMBING
AND NEVER SHOWS A KNEE — already on record ("your SSIM-keeps-improving-at-247-
BPP+ result", RESEARCH-quality-metrics-2026-08-01.md). Netflix's own VMAF blog
lists FILM GRAIN FIRST among unaddressed areas, and Netflix/vmaf issue #1192 is
an open bug about exactly this. A ladder measured with either never bends on a
grainy title. Do not substitute SSIM for VMAF "for now"; that rebuilds a known
dead end. (Both are also full-reference, i.e. excluded by constraint 1.2a for the
upgrade question — they can only ever measure distance from our own copy.)


UPDATE 2026-08-20, AND KEEP THE DISTINCTION SHARP: this entry is about VMAF and
SSIM, and it still stands for both. It does NOT apply to CAMBI, which ships inside
libvmaf but is a different thing — no-reference, banding-specific, and now measured
here as an independent axis (10.5a). VMAF needs a master and is calibration-only;
CAMBI needs none and works in production. Do not cite this entry to argue against
the banding axis.
11.3 gShare — FALSIFIED (docs/REPORT-grain-2026-08-13.md)
A grain metric built by encoding with x265 `-tune grain` and differencing
bitrates. It did not measure grain: Up (2009), rendered CGI with no photochemical
grain at all, scored 0.407 against Casablanca 0.422 and Alien 0.421, and the
metric was largely a restatement of DARKNESS (r = -0.885 against mean luma). DO
NOT REVIVE IT. Any replacement must pass the same two degeneracy tests — does it
separate CGI from film stock, and is it independent of mean luma.

11.4 blockMean / blurMean — UNTESTED, NOT FALSIFIED. KEEP THIS DISTINCTION SHARP.
Their correlations (-0.090 and -0.266) came from the retracted labels and were
withdrawn WITH them, along with the "do not revive blockMean" verdict and the
false-positive case list behind it. blockMean's -0.090 says blockiness and
grain-visibility are unrelated, which tells us nothing about whether it detects
blocking. THAT IS NOT AN ARGUMENT TO REVIVE THEM: the evidence either way does
not exist. They remain stored and displayed, and inputs to no score.

11.5 THE LADDER-SLOPE INSTRUMENT — FALSIFIED (2026-08-18)
THE HYPOTHESIS: a starved source has thrown detail away, so lowering CRF buys
nothing back and the bitrate stops responding — a SHALLOW slope; a well-supplied
source keeps costing bits per CRF point — a STEEP slope. Therefore ladder slope
measures source exhaustion DIRECTLY, on any film, in ~10 min, and crucially
WITHOUT CIRCULARITY: R = srcBitrate/probeBitrate has the possibly-pinned
complexity in its denominator, whereas slope comes from the ladder's SHAPE.
WHY IT LOOKED STRONG: rho -0.750 against supply across 20 films (section 6, finding 3),
against rho -0.333 for the observational pair fit.
HOW IT DIED: the starvation experiment holds CONTENT CONSTANT and varies supply.
Under that control, slope predicts the understatement at +0.335 while observed R
predicts it at -0.924. The library-wide correlation was cross-film confounding —
grainy old films are both starved and shallow-sloped for separate reasons.
DO NOT REVIVE IT. And note the general lesson: a correlation measured ACROSS the
library is not evidence about a mechanism WITHIN a film. Only a controlled
experiment separates them, and one costs ~10 minutes per film.

11.6 THE COVERING SET AND THE BINDING ARTIFACT (2026-08-25/26)  [PARTLY SOLID]
Brennan's framing, and the thing that replaced every hand-set guard in the blend:
BPP+ answers "is this file big enough", which is a proxy for "does it look fine".
Where the two disagree, the artifact is the better witness. Each artifact gives

    m_a = (T_a / L_a)^(1/S_a)     bitrate multiplier bringing artifact a to its
                                  visibility threshold
    p_a = P(ln m_a > all others)  probability that artifact a is the one binding
    ln m = sum_a p_a ln m_a       the MIXTURE, not the max
    BPP+_adj = BPP+ / sqrt(m)     BPP+ is sqrt(bpp/target), so a bitrate multiplier
                                  converts straight into score units

NOT SUM: correlated detectors cannot double-count, which was the standing objection
to blending anything at all.

AND NOT A HARD MAX EITHER — that was a cut-off in disguise and it was caught in use.
Brennan, 2026-08-26, moving the grain slider one step: "at 0.91 big lebowski is +254
but at 0.92 its +0". A max over POINT estimates ignores how well each is known. The
Big Lebowski's banding estimate is sharp (m 0.061, sigma ~0.1); its grain estimate
is useless (sigma ~8, because S_grain is 0.025 and everything divides by S^2). At
T=0.915 banding was larger and the film moved +254 on a confident number; one step
later grain edged ahead, won the max, and froze the film at +0 on a number that
means nothing. The physics is right — the first artifact to appear does bind — but
"which is largest" is the wrong question when one of the two is barely measured.
The mixture weights by the PROBABILITY each artifact is binding (closed form for
two, fixed-seed Monte Carlo above two), and its variance carries both the spread
within each artifact and the disagreement between them, so a film whose detectors
disagree is reported as uncertain rather than confidently wrong. As the sigmas
shrink it converges on the hard max, so the physics is recovered exactly in the
limit where the measurements are good. Measured after the fix: The Big Lebowski
moves 150 -> 151 across the WHOLE slider range, and the library median multiplier is
1.00 / 1.01 / 1.01 at T = 0.90 / 0.93 / 0.96.

NO CUT-OFFS, and this is a design rule, not a preference. Four guards (L_FLOOR
0.25, M_UP_FLOOR 0.5, M_CAP 50, FLAT 0.25) were added one at a time after watching
the lab produce specific absurdities, and all four were deleted once the variance
was computed properly:

    Var(ln m) = [ Var(ln L) + (ln m)^2 Var(S) ] / S^2  +  (c * outside)^2 / S^2

The 1/S^2 makes an unresponsive artifact lose confidence smoothly instead of being
caught by a threshold; (ln m)^2 penalises extrapolation quadratically; the distance
term is measured from the CENTROID of that film's rungs, so a wide ladder is
penalised less than a narrow one for the same answer.
THE DISTANCE MUST NOT BE HINGED. It was first written as max(0, loEdge - lnM,
lnM - hiEdge) — exactly zero inside the ladder, switching on outside it. Divided by
S^2 that kink is amplified by 1/S^2, which for The Big Lebowski's grain slope of
0.025 is 1548x, turning a kink into a cliff. It was a hard cut-off wearing a
variance costume and it is gone; |lnM - xBar| is smooth everywhere and is the
standard OLS prediction-variance form. Both constants are MEASURED, not chosen:
  Var(ln L) = 0.059   21 films carry two independent readings of their own banding
                      (nightly probe vs ladder lossless rung). The additive noise
                      floor fitted to ZERO — CAMBI is scale-free, agreeing with
                      itself to ~24% relative across three orders of magnitude, and
                      The Big Sleep reads 0.0032 vs 0.0017. There is no floor to
                      find, which FALSIFIED the expected fix. Note this replaced an
                      INFERRED 0.612 from split-half reliability; reliability
                      answers "how much of the between-FILM spread is real", not
                      "how far is one reading from another of the same film".
  c = 0.306 per ln-unit   fitting each ladder on 0.7-1.3 only and scoring the wide
                      rungs. Residuals: sd 0.059 at distance <0.4, 0.198 at 0.4-0.8,
                      0.219 at 0.8-1.5 with mean -0.206.
Shrinkage is precision-weighted empirical Bayes, tau^2 = (sum w_i D_i^2 - k)/sum w_i,
UNCENTRED so the prior sits at no change. An unweighted version pinned tau^2 to zero
because War of the Worlds fits S = 0.00 and implies 1e79 with sigma 1105; one
unusable row read as "no film moves".

11.7 BANDING SATURATES, AND CAN REVERSE (2026-08-25)  [SOLID]
The first direct test of the extrapolation the upward direction rested on. Fit on
0.7-1.3, predict the wide rungs: ratio 1.00 at every rung inside the window, 0.90
at 0.5x, 0.62 at 0.3x. Knocked Up goes further and REVERSES — 5.475 at 0.85x,
4.989 at 0.30x — because at low bitrate the encoder stops producing banding and
starts producing blocking, smoothing the gradients away. So banding is not
monotonic in bits, and for many films the crossing point does not exist at all.
m = (T/L)^(1/S) has no way to say "never" and returns a huge number instead. THAT,
compounded with the covering hole below, is what produced The Godfather at 782.

11.8 THE COVERING HOLE IS GRAIN-SHAPED (2026-08-25/26)  [SOLID]
    corr(|S_block|, complexity) = -0.459 (n=21), replicated -0.315 (n=29)
Blocking goes inert as grain rises: The Godfather -0.03, Ocean's Eleven -0.03, The
Big Sleep -0.06. On grainy films BOTH readable detectors are asleep, so the max was
over an empty set and banding's leftover headroom won by default. This is not a
numerical defect to be capped — it is a missing column, exactly where Brennan
predicted it. Grain retention was added as a fourth ladder artifact and points the
right way: corr(S_grain, complexity) = +0.319, opposite sign to blocking, median
fit r2 0.968 over 29 films. With grain in the max, films reading over 150 on
banding alone fall from 19 to 8.

11.9 THE GRAIN AXIS SERVES TWO ARTIFACTS AT ONCE — OPEN (2026-08-26)  [WEAK]
DO NOT TREAT THE GRAIN AXIS AS SETTLED, and do not mis-diagnose why. Brennan, by
eye: "our slider currently is moving films that I dont consider to really have much
grain at all." He is right; the first explanation offered was wrong and is recorded
here so it is not re-derived.

THE WRONG DIAGNOSIS. grainOf at the lossless rung reads ~1.0 for nearly every film
(0.61-1.29 across a 10x span in complexity; Schindler's List cx 0.548 -> 1.087,
Poor Things cx 0.141 -> 1.001), so it looked as though retention were a ratio to a
meaningless number. IT IS NOT THE MECHANISM. The absolute level CANCELS out of m
exactly — verified by scaling every reading of a film by a constant:
    Full Metal Jacket  m = 0.7463 at 1x, 0.7463 at 2x, 0.7463 at 0.5x
m depends only on S and on the RATIO grain(level 1)/grain(lossless), both within-film.

WHAT DOES ENTER is ADDITIVE contamination — the edge/texture floor the literature
requires masking for — and it biases m DOWNWARD, i.e. toward not binding:
    Full Metal Jacket  m = 0.7463 at +0, 0.5343 at +0.3, 0.3816 at +0.6
So contamination makes grain UNDER-fire. Flat-region masking would make MORE films
move on grain, not fewer, which is the opposite of what the complaint needs.

THE ACTUAL PROBLEM IS TAXONOMY. One measurement is serving two artifacts. On a
grainy film the residual plausibly tracks GRAIN LOSS. On a grainless one — Obsession
(2026), cx 0.051, S_grain 0.089, a genuinely steep slope — it tracks how fast FINE
DIGITAL DETAIL disappears. Both are real; both are being pushed through a single
threshold labelled "grain kept". No value of T_grain fixes that, because 7% loss of
35mm grain and 7% loss of digital detail are not equally visible. It is therefore
neither a bug nor a dial: it is a MISSING DISCRIMINATOR.

THE LIKELY FIX, and it fits the no-cut-offs rule: gridRatio (section 9.1) is not a
rival level measure, it is the WEIGHT. ~1.00 means the residual is grain, >1.15
means it is coding artifact. Applying the grain threshold in proportion to how much
of the residual is actually grain is continuous, needs no branch, and degrades to
"grain does not apply here" on its own for digital-clean films.

T_grain remains unsettled and consequential: 0.85 -> 0.97 moves the median library
multiplier from x2.26 to x0.88, because grain's shallow slope (S 0.019-0.100) puts T
under an exponent of 10-50. It is a SLIDER in the lab, not a constant in the model.
Brennan's call 2026-08-26: settle it from literature and analysis, NOT an A/B.

11.10 THE EXCHANGE RATE MUST COME FROM LABELS, NOT FROM 1/S (2026-08-26)  [SOLID]
Brennan, when the four-artifact blend moved nothing: "If our formula makes it so
that no films move ... that means our blending formula is wrong."
He was right, and the error is nameable. m = (T/L)^(1/S) uses the FILM'S OWN SLOPE
to convert an artifact into bits. But the slope answers "how fast does this artifact
change with bits", not "how much does this artifact matter". Dividing by a shallow
slope is what produced blocking at m = 1e-58 for Witness for the Prosecution, and a
disagreement of 17-130 log units between artifacts, which the mixture then carried
into the variance and drove tau^2 to zero:
    cambi alone              tau^2 0.110    51 of 101 films move
    cambi + block            tau^2 0.000     0
    all four                 tau^2 0.000     0
Adding an artifact made the model LESS confident, which is the signature of a wrong
combiner rather than of missing data.
THE FIX. CVQAD measures the right quantity directly, within-sequence:
    bits-equivalent of a = [d(label)/d(ln a)] / [d(label)/d(ln bpp)] * (ln L_a - ln T_a)
    d(label)/d(ln bpp) = 1.592
    cambi -0.658   block -1.421   blur -6.703   grain +12.899
No per-film division, so nothing can diverge. Applied over the worst artifact the
multiplier runs p05 0.79, median 1.24, p95 1.77, max 2.22, and it PASSES the
independence check that killed the cross-film residual: corr with log BPP+ 0.278,
with log bpp 0.364. Summing rather than maxing explodes (median 4.77x) because
grain's rate is large, so max stays.
The +24% median shift is a level offset between our Bluray library and CVQAD's UGC
sources. Brennan 2026-08-26: he has long suspected the ~60 median was too low and is
content for it to move now that there is an independent reason.

11.11 BUT NO DETECTOR BEATS BITS-PLUS-CONTENT ON CVQAD — READ THIS BEFORE SHIPPING
ANY OF 11.10  [SOLID, and it is the most important negative result in this section]
Within-sequence correlations look strong (blur -0.839, grain +0.773). They are
mostly the detectors tracking bitrate. Removing bits with progressively fairer
models:
    bits shape removed        cambi    block     blur    grain
    global linear             -0.194   -0.145   -0.492   +0.283
    global cubic              -0.143   -0.071   -0.456   +0.258
    per-sequence LINEAR       +0.048   -0.198   -0.466   +0.478
    per-sequence QUADRATIC    -0.062   -0.111   +0.027   +0.031
And the cleanest test of all — same source, same delivered bitrate, different
encoder preset, so bits are held exactly constant — gives coin flips:
    cambi 48%   block 49%   blur 49%   grain 51%   (n=86-89 pairs)
So once each content's own quality-vs-bits curve is modelled with any curvature,
every detector adds nothing.
WHAT THAT ACTUALLY MEANS, and it is not "the framework is dead". Blur and grain
survive a per-sequence LINEAR fit and die under a QUADRATIC one. The quality-vs-bits
relation saturates; blur and grain are curved functions of bits; so they are proxies
for WHERE A FILM SITS ON ITS OWN DIMINISHING-RETURNS CURVE, not for an artifact that
bits cannot see. That is consistent with everything else measured: they are inert on
our narrow ladders (1.013 and 1.016 swing at a 30% cut), strong across CVQAD's 90x
range, and vanish when the curve is modelled.
AND THAT IS STILL THE QUESTION WE CARE ABOUT. BPP+ is linear in log bits against a
complexity-derived target and models no saturation at all. Brennan's original
framing — "if increasing the bits DOESNT improve the viewing experience then our
perception of the film being bit-starved is wrong" — is precisely a question about
position on the knee. So the honest reframing is that we do not have artifact
detectors that beat bits+content, and we may have SATURATION detectors, which is a
different instrument aimed at the same decision.
CAVEAT ON THE CORPUS: CVQAD is 1080p UGC and broadcast, and its per-sequence curve
is a near-perfect within-content fit on ~6 points. BPP+'s content model is a
cross-film complexity estimate carrying real error. "Adds nothing over a perfect
content model" is not "adds nothing over BPP+", and that gap is not yet measured.

11.12 THE COVERING SET IS VALIDATED — THE PANEL SEES WHAT BPP+ CANNOT (2026-08-26)  [SOLID]
THE STRONGEST POSITIVE RESULT IN THIS PROJECT, and it exists because Brennan
refused to accept the negative in 11.11 and demanded a reason rather than a dataset:
  "Tell me through logical reasoning why it doesnt work. Not via data but by logic.
   If we cant reason as to why it cant work, then it can, and we need to FIND the
   experiment and the formula to make the concept work."

THE FALSE PREMISE I HAD BEEN REASONING FROM: "artifacts are caused by bits and
content, therefore they carry nothing beyond bits and content." That is wrong. The
causal chain is
    artifacts = f(bits, content, ENCODER, SETTINGS, MASTER, GENERATIONS, PREPROCESSING)
and BPP+ sees the first two ONLY. Two files with identical bits and identical content
can differ in quality by up to 2x through the other five. A library spanning Bluray
remuxes to WEBRips, a dozen release groups and twenty years of encoder practice
varies enormously in exactly those. So artifacts MUST carry information BPP+ lacks.

AND IT EXPLAINS THE 11.11 NULL. Every clip in a CVQAD sequence is THE SAME MASTER
through THE SAME ENCODER, varying only bitrate and one preset step. That corpus holds
constant precisely the variables that make artifacts informative, so it is
structurally incapable of showing the effect. The null was a fact about its design.
Do not cite 11.11 as evidence against the framework; cite it as a lesson about
corpus design.

THE EXPERIMENT (scripts/encoder-ladder.js). Hold bits and content EXACTLY constant,
vary only encoder quality, and use an ordering nobody disputes because it is what
presets ARE: x264 veryfast < medium < slower, all at the same bitrate, at 0.6x the
film's own rate so every encoder must make visible compromises. BPP+ scores all three
identically and cannot distinguish them at all.

RESULT, 8 films spanning complexity 0.032 to 1.206:
    detector   mean rank corr   films perfect
    cambi           1.000            8/8
    block           1.000            8/8
    blur            1.000            8/8
    grain           1.000            8/8
All four detectors, perfect ordering, every film. No labels anywhere.

THE CONFOUND, CHECKED AND DEAD. x265 uses transforms up to 32x32 against x264's
4x4/8x8, so blockdetect might read a CODEC FINGERPRINT rather than quality. The
figures above are x264 presets ONLY — identical transform sizes, so nothing but
encode quality varies. Including x265 drops cambi to 0.700, blur 0.875, grain 0.900;
block stays 1.000. That drop is NOT detector failure: HEVC genuinely bands more on
smooth content (larger transforms, stronger deblocking) while genuinely blocking
less. The panel is reporting reality and the assumed ordering was too crude for
banding specifically.

EFFECT SIZE, veryfast vs slower, same codec, same bits, same content:
    12 Angry Men   cambi 4.78x  block 1.10x     The Birds   cambi 3.87x  block 1.24x
    Mr. Robot      cambi 1.11x  block 1.36x     Hugo        cambi 1.29x  block 1.22x
Banding has the widest dynamic range for encode quality; blocking is the most
consistent. Both are invisible to BPP+ by construction.

WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT ESTABLISH. It establishes that the panel ORDERS encode
quality correctly at fixed bits and content — the thing BPP+ cannot do. It does not
yet establish a calibrated MAGNITUDE. scripts/bdrate-check.js is the next step: sweep
both codecs across bitrates and ask what ratio makes their artifacts match. If the
detectors independently land near the published x265-vs-x264 BD-rate of 1.4-2.0x,
the panel is calibrated against a decade of coding literature with no human labels.

11.13 THE PANEL HAS TWO AXES, NOT ONE — AND THAT IS THE COVERING SET (2026-08-26)  [SOLID]
The architectural result, and it explains every absurdity the blend produced today.
Two swings, both measured on real films:
    BITRATE  level 2.5x -> 0.3x on the ladders, 8.3x change in bits, encoder FIXED
    ENCODER  x264 veryfast -> slower at ONE bitrate, bits FIXED

    detector   bitrate swing   encoder swing   ratio   lives on
    cambi          3.182x          1.328x       6.7    BITRATE
    block          1.224x          1.223x       1.0    ENCODER
    blur           1.078x          1.026x       3.0    bitrate, but both swings tiny
    grain          1.105x          1.041x       2.5    both

ONE PRESET STEP MOVES BLOCKING EXACTLY AS MUCH AS AN 8.3x CHANGE IN BITRATE DOES.
Blocking is not a bits detector. It is an encode-competence detector.

WHY THIS EXPLAINS THE FAILURES. Every version of the blend converted every artifact
into a bitrate multiplier, because BPP+ is a bits measure. For an artifact that does
not live on the bits axis that operation is meaningless, and it fails LOUDLY: the
conversion divides by the artifact's bitrate slope, which for blocking is 0.103, so
1/S is 9.73 and any excess explodes. That is the source of m = 1e-58 (11.10), of the
7.08x implied preset gap, and of the tau^2 collapse when blocking was added to the
mixture. Not a numerical defect — the wrong operation applied to the wrong quantity.

SO THE COVERING SET IS NOT "MANY DETECTORS FOR MANY FAILURE MODES". It is detectors
covering the two AXES on which a file can be deficient:
    BITS AXIS      is there enough data for this content?   BPP+ answers this, and
                   banding independently confirms or corrects it
    ENCODER AXIS   was the data spent competently?          BPP+ CANNOT SEE THIS AT
                   ALL, and blocking measures it directly
This is exactly Brennan's framing — "if a copy is artifact free when it in theory
shouldnt be ... then its a higher #". A well-encoded file at modest bits genuinely IS
better than a badly-encoded file at the same bits, and blocking is what sees it.

EXCHANGE RATES, each derived on the axis the detector actually lives on:
    BANDING, from the bits axis. Pooled median |S| over 101 ladders = 0.585, so one
      log-unit of banding is 1.71 log-units of bits. VALIDATED FOR FREE: applied to
      the measured veryfast->slower banding gap of 1.328x it implies a 1.62x bitrate
      gap, against a published x264 preset BD-rate of roughly 1.3-1.5x. Close, and
      derived from our ladders with nothing imported.
    BLOCKING, from the encoder axis. One preset step moves blocking 1.223x and is
      worth ~1.4x bitrate, so the rate is ln(1.4)/ln(1.223) = 1.67 log-bits per
      log-blocking — SIX TIMES smaller than the 9.73 the bitrate slope gave. This one
      does import a published figure, in the same way 2.817 is imported for CAMBI.
    POOLING IS WHAT REGULARISES EITHER OF THEM. A per-film 1/S explodes whenever that
      film's slope is shallow; the pooled median does not. Do not go back to per-film.

DO NOT convert blur or grain to bits yet. Their bitrate swings (1.078x, 1.105x over
8.3x of bits) are barely above measurement noise, and 11.9 is still unresolved for
grain — the axis serves two artifacts at once.

11.14 THE ENCODER RESULT REPLICATES AT n=20, AND THE FAILURES ARE EXPLICABLE  [SOLID]
x264 presets only, so transform sizes are identical and no codec fingerprint is
possible:
    detector   films   mean rank corr   perfect   median veryfast->slower gap
    cambi        20         0.975        19/20            1.452x
    block        20         0.900        19/20            1.262x
    blur         20         0.975        19/20            1.035x
    grain        20         0.900        16/20            1.044x
The 8-film run gave 1.000 across the board; 19/20 at n=20 is the expected regression
from a small sample, not a weakening of the result.

THE EXCHANGE RATE FOR THE ENCODER AXIS REPLICATES. Blocking's preset gap is 1.262x
here against 1.223x at n=8, giving a rate of ln(1.4)/ln(1.262) = 1.44 log-bits per
log-blocking, against 1.67 from the smaller sample. Both land in 1.4-1.7 and both are
around six times smaller than the 9.73 the BITRATE slope would have given — which is
the whole point of 11.13.

EVERY FAILURE IS EXPLAINED, and one is a boundary condition worth carrying:
  MISSION: IMPOSSIBLE - GHOST PROTOCOL fails on cambi (0.50) AND block (-1.00), the
    only film to fail both. Its blocking runs BACKWARDS: 4.2221 -> 4.5371 -> 4.7081
    as the encoder improves, at 867 kbps for a 1080p action film. It was already one
    of only two films where NOTHING moved across an 8.3x bitrate span (11.8).
    THE MECHANISM: below some bitrate every encoder is catastrophic, and a better one
    stops producing FEWER artifacts and starts producing DIFFERENT ones — it
    preserves detail that blockdetect then finds edges in, while veryfast smooths
    everything into mush. So the panel's ordering guarantee has a floor. Do not apply
    an encoder-axis correction to a file already deep in that regime; the sign is not
    reliable there.
  THE FOUR GRAIN FAILURES are resolution, not disagreement: 0.8407 -> 0.8540 ->
    0.8506 and three others of the same shape. Grain's entire dynamic range across a
    preset span is 1.044x, so a 0.0004 wobble flips a rank. This is the same weakness
    recorded in 11.13 and is a reason to keep grain out of the blend for now, not a
    reason to distrust the axis.

11.15 A SECOND PROVENANCE AXIS: GENERATION COUNT (2026-08-26)  [SOLID]
Same design as the encoder ladder, a different axis, an ordering that is PHYSICAL
rather than conventional: each pass quantises what the previous pass already
quantised, so error accumulates and cannot decrease. One, two and three encode passes
all delivered at the SAME final bitrate, so BPP+ scores every row identically.

    detector   films   mean rank corr   perfect
    cambi        10         0.950         9/10
    block        10         1.000        10/10
    blur         10         1.000        10/10
    grain        10         0.800         9/10

Median degradation over three generations: cambi 1.156x, block 1.136x, blur 1.049x,
grain 1.014x. Every film moved monotonically on cambi/block/blur and grain fell, as
it must — grain is destroyed by repeated quantisation.

WHY IT MATTERS HERE SPECIFICALLY: a WEBRip IS a re-encode of a WEB-DL, and a scene
x265 release is a re-encode of a Bluray. Generation count is common in this library,
real, and invisible to BPP+ by construction.

NOTE BLUR'S BEHAVIOUR. Its generation effect (1.049x) EXCEEDS its preset effect
(1.035x) and approaches its whole 8.3x-bitrate effect (1.078x). Blur is a poor bits
detector and a poor encoder detector but a comparatively good GENERATION detector.
That is a third axis, and it is the reason not to retire blur despite 11.8.

SO THE PANEL NOW HAS THREE VALIDATED AXES, all invisible to BPP+:
    BITS         banding, rate 1.71 log-bits per log-artifact (11.13)
    ENCODER      blocking, rate 1.44-1.67 (11.13, 11.14)
    GENERATIONS  blocking and blur, 10/10 each
This is what "covering set" actually means: not many detectors for many failure
modes, but detectors spanning the axes on which a file can be deficient.

11.16 THE DENOISE AXIS — GRAIN REHABILITATED, AND TWO DETECTORS DOING WHAT NEITHER
CAN DO ALONE (2026-08-26)  [SOLID]
none / light / heavy hqdn3d before encoding, same bitrate after, 10 grainiest films.
BPP+ scores every row identically.
    detector   mean rank corr   perfect   median none->heavy swing
    cambi           0.800        9/10           2.437x
    block           0.800        8/10           1.098x
    blur            0.950        9/10           1.052x
    grain           0.950        9/10           1.121x

GRAIN IS REHABILITATED. Its swing here is 1.121x against 1.044x on the encoder axis
and 1.014x on generations. It IS a grain detector; it was written off twice on axes
it had no reason to be good at. Judge a detector on the axis it lives on.

MY PREDICTION WAS WRONG AND THE REASON MATTERS. I expected denoising to make encoding
EASIER and banding to improve. Banding gets far worse instead — 2.437x median, and
11.8x on The General. The mechanism is 11.9's masking effect running in reverse:
grain was DITHERING the gradients, and removing it exposes the banding underneath.
Only 2 of 10 films showed cambi or block improving. This corroborates Norkin DCC 2026
(cited in data/research/GRAIN-ARTIFACT-REPORT.md), which is why AV2 added debanding
to the grain-synthesis step.

AND IT PRODUCES THE FIRST REAL CASE OF THE COVERING SET EARNING ITS NAME. High
banding is AMBIGUOUS on its own — it means either too few bits or a denoised source,
and those demand OPPOSITE actions:
    too few bits    -> a bigger file of the same release helps
    denoised source -> a bigger file does NOT help; a different release is needed
GRAIN DISAMBIGUATES. A denoised file reads LOW grain; a bit-starved file keeps its
grain and bands anyway. Neither detector can call it alone; together they identify
the cause. That is what a covering set is for, and it is the first measured instance.

FOUR AXES ARE NOW VALIDATED, all invisible to BPP+ by construction:
    BITS         banding          3.182x over 8.3x of bitrate
    ENCODER      blocking         1.262x per preset step, 19/20 films
    GENERATIONS  blocking, blur   10/10 each
    PREPROCESS   grain, blur      1.121x / 1.052x, 9/10 each

11.17 THE FIELD RESIDUAL IS REAL, AND ITS CAUSE IS STILL UNATTRIBUTED (2026-08-26)
[SOLID on reliability, OPEN on cause]
The controlled axes (11.12-11.16) all hold bits and content exactly constant. The
library never does, so the field version must read a RESIDUAL against a bits+content
model. Two hypotheses had to be separated: the residual is PROVENANCE (build on it)
or MODEL ERROR (do not).

FIRST: IT IS NOT NOISE. Two independent readings of the same film's banding — the
nightly probe's 8 clips and the ladder lossless rung's 4 different clips — give
    residual correlation 0.861,  Spearman-Brown 0.926,  n=101
So ~86% of a single reading's residual is a stable property of the FILE. Anything
built on it is building on something real. This was the cheap test that a null on the
provenance comparison could not have dodged, and it passed.

SECOND: NEITHER CRUDE PROXY EXPLAINS IT.
    content features  (luma, resolution, cx^2)      7.2% of the residual
    provenance labels (source tier, codec, year)    5.7%
    -> ~73% stable and unattributed
Do NOT read the low provenance number as evidence against provenance. Source tier is
a terrible proxy: "Bluray-1080p" covers a careful remux-derived encode and a lazy one
alike, and encoder settings, generation count and denoising appear in no label.

THIRD: THE RELEASE-GROUP TEST IS UNINFORMATIVE FOR A DATA REASON, NOT A MODEL ONE.
Group identity is the best available provenance proxy — groups have house styles, and
content has no reason to cluster by who encoded it. Result: F = 1.07, 4.8% explained,
null. But the sample cannot show the effect:
    - 65% of units were RENAMED by *arr to "Title (Year) Bluray-1080p.mkv" and the
      group is unrecoverable from the filename
    - the groups that survive are YTS(30) YIFY(29) LAMA(5) ETRG(4) — ALL small-file
      re-encoders with near-identical philosophy. The careful groups (SPARKS, RARBG,
      remuxes) are exactly the ones that were renamed.
That is restricted range: the same class of error as the CVQAD null in 11.11, where a
corpus held constant the variable under test. A null from a sample with no variation
in the treatment is not evidence.

THE UNBLOCK IS A DATABASE QUERY, NOT AN EXPERIMENT. Radarr and Sonarr retain the
original release name (sceneName / originalFilePath) even after renaming the file on
disk. Recovering it restores the group for the ~65% that lost it AND brings the
careful groups back into the sample, which is what the clustering test needs.

11.18 THE WEBRip TEST IS A DEAD END, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL (2026-08-26)
[SOLID negative]
150 units measured (53 WEBRip, 97 WEB-DL) to ask whether the field residual carries
provenance. Pre-registered before the data landed, including the rule that a null
would be UNINFORMATIVE rather than evidence against. It is null:
    detector   diff    SE     t    expected
    cambi     0.147  0.223  0.66      ?
    block     0.031  0.074  0.42      +
    blur      0.031  0.032  0.94      +
    grain     0.141  0.072  1.94      -  (WRONG DIRECTION)

WHY IT COULD NOT HAVE WORKED, and this is the durable lesson: WEBRip and WEB-DL
confound generation with BITRATE. People re-encode IN ORDER TO make files smaller, so
the two vary together by construction — median bpp 0.0505 against 0.0884, a factor of
1.75, and BPP+ 65 against 92. Separating "re-encode damage" from "fewer bits" then
requires the bits model to be correct, which is the thing under test. Circular.

AND THE LOOSE CONTROL NEARLY PRODUCED A FALSE POSITIVE. A "matched bpp band" of
0.0465-0.1257 is 2.7x wide, and WEBRips cluster at its bottom, so bitrate leaks
straight back in. Tightening it until the medians actually agree:
    band width   bpp rip / dl        cambi    block     blur
       3.0x    0.0525 / 0.0721       1.668    1.095    1.020
       2.0x    0.0549 / 0.0721       1.703    0.983    1.044
       1.5x    0.0694 / 0.0659       0.571    0.872    1.059   <- matched
       1.3x    0.0701 / 0.0645       0.558    0.814    1.105
The cambi ratio collapses from 1.70 to 0.57 AND INVERTS exactly when the bitrates
match. A 2.35x "generation effect" would have been reported from the loose band and
it does not exist. Any future matched-band control must verify the medians agree, not
assume the band did it.

WHAT SURVIVES AT MATCHED BITS: nothing reliable. n falls to ~12 per group and the
detectors disagree — blur says WEBRips are worse (1.10-1.22, strengthening as the
confound clears), block says better (0.80), cambi says better (0.56).

CONSEQUENCE FOR THE PROGRAMME. Field validation of the residual cannot be done
observationally in THIS library, because every natural provenance contrast is also a
bitrate contrast. The controlled experiments (11.12-11.16) remain the evidence, and
applying them to the library requires assuming the model transfers — an assumption
that is now explicit and unvalidated rather than hidden. The remaining routes are a
genuinely bits-matched natural experiment (none is known to exist here) or human
judgement on named files.

11.19 A FIELD ENCODE-QUALITY MEASUREMENT THAT SURVIVES ONE CONTROL AND DIES TO THE
NEXT (2026-08-26)  [WEAK, do not build on it]
11.18 concluded field validation was impossible observationally because every natural
provenance contrast is also a bitrate contrast. There IS one place that is not true,
and the ladder data already contained it:
    lossless rung   the file's artifacts as the release group made it
    level 1.0 rung  OUR x264 medium re-encode AT THAT SAME BITRATE
Their ratio measures how much the release encoder beat a fixed reference, with the
bitrate confound absent BY CONSTRUCTION. Measured 2026-08-26: median 2.00x, range
1.04x to 86.8x across 101 films. Best: Sunset Boulevard 86.8x, The Big Sleep 47.9x.
Worst: Knocked Up 1.04x, Poor Things 1.07x. (This is the quantity earlier called
"generation loss" and dismissed as a nuisance. It is a measurement.)

THE TEST, AND ITS THREE CONTROLS, IN ORDER:
    raw corr(banding residual, encode quality)          -0.697
    ... but corr(residual, -log lossless) alone is      -0.705   SHARED TERM
    after removing log(lossless) from both sides        -0.445
    after also removing complexity                      -0.420
    after also removing bits                            -0.188   <- THE HONEST NUMBER
Each control ate roughly half the effect. -0.188 at n=101 is borderline and cannot
carry the Blend page. DO NOT cite the -0.697 or the -0.445.

WHY THE SHARED TERM IS FATAL AND WAS NOT OBVIOUS: the residual contains
+log(cambi_nightly) and the encode-quality ratio contains -log(cambi_lossless), and
those two readings correlate at 0.908 because both measure the same file's banding.
The negative correlation is therefore forced arithmetically. Any future test that
builds both sides from the same artifact reading has this defect.

THE PATTERN WORTH RECORDING, because it repeated three times in one evening:
    "2.35x generation effect"   -> was bitrate (11.18)
    "-0.736 iso-damage bias"    -> was BPP+ dividing by complexity, i.e. its own definition
    "-0.697 field validation"   -> was a shared term, then content, then bits
EVERY headline number tonight shrank or vanished under its own controls. Treat a
first number as a hypothesis about where to look for a confound, never as a result.

11.20 A CLEAN PER-FILM ENCODE-QUALITY MEASUREMENT — USE BLOCKING, NOT BANDING
(2026-08-27)  [SOLID, and the first usable field quantity]
11.19 found a field measurement of encode quality with the bitrate confound absent by
construction — the file as delivered against OUR x264 medium at the SAME bitrate:
    eq = artifact(level 1.0 rung) / artifact(lossless rung)
and then failed to validate it against the banding residual. The measurement itself
was never the problem; the DETECTOR was.

    detector    n    median      range        corr(eq, its own denominator)
    banding   101     2.00x   1.04 .. 86.8         -0.648    CONTAMINATED
    blocking   97     1.16x   0.81 .. 2.0          -0.159    largely clean
    blur       97     1.02x   1.00 .. 1.1          +0.018    clean but inert

BANDING'S eq IS MOSTLY 1/DENOMINATOR. Sunset Boulevard's 86.8x is its near-zero
lossless reading, not an encoder fact — the fourth appearance of divide-by-small in
one evening. Do not use banding for eq.

BLOCKING'S eq IS THE ONE TO USE, and that is not luck: 11.13 established blocking as
the ENCODER-axis detector, so it is exactly the detector that should yield a clean
encoder-quality number. Bounded 0.81-2.0x, median 1.16x, only mildly related to the
film's own blocking level.

THE SIGN READS CORRECTLY, which is a check worth stating: a GOOD original has little
blocking, so our re-encode adds a lot -> HIGH eq. A BAD original is already blocky, so
there is little left to degrade -> LOW eq.
    best   Donnie Brasco 1.95  Heat 1.82  Dark Knight 1.77  Marty Supreme 1.73
    worst  Spirited Away 0.81  It's a Wonderful Life 0.88  All the President's Men 0.90
Modern carefully-encoded releases at the top, old catalogue transfers at the bottom.

CORRECTION, 2026-08-27 01:35 — THE "NO BITRATE CONFOUND" CLAIM ABOVE IS WRONG.
This section originally argued eq was a per-film measured quantity free of the bitrate
confound "by construction", because both terms are measured at the same bitrate. That
reasoning is subtly false and the data says so:
    corr(eq, log bpp)        -0.536      NOT weak
    corr(eq, complexity)     -0.407
    corr(eq, BPP+)           -0.150
Holding the bitrate equal on both sides removes the bitrate DIFFERENCE between the two
files. It does not remove the dependence of the DEGRADATION MAGNITUDE on the absolute
bitrate level: at low bpp our x264 medium adds proportionally more blocking, so eq
rises. Part of eq is simply "this film has few bits", which BPP+ already knows.

AND THE APPARENT STRUCTURE WAS BITS AND CONTENT TOO. eq correlated with release year
at 0.421, which looked like "modern releases are better encoded". Stripping controls:
    nothing removed             corr(eq, year) 0.421
    bits removed                               0.207
    bits + complexity removed                  0.088
Nothing meaningful survives.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY LEFT: 77% of eq's spread survives removal of bits and complexity, so
there IS substantial variation of unknown cause — the SAME position as the banding
residual in 11.17, not an improvement on it. The residual-eq ordering (Donnie Brasco,
The Dark Knight, Heat at the top; Spirited Away, Witness for the Prosecution at the
bottom) looks plausible, and "looks plausible to me" is not evidence.

STATUS DOWNGRADED from SOLID to WEAK. The blocking-vs-banding comparison at the top of
this section stands — banding's eq really is dominated by 1/denominator and blocking's
is not. What does not stand is that blocking's eq is confound-free. Do not apply
sqrt(eq) to BPP+.

SEPARATELY STILL UNRESOLVED: the reference is our x264 medium, whereas BPP+'s target
comes from an x265 CRF-20 complexity probe. Even a clean eq would need those two
references reconciled before it could touch the score.

11.21 THE ENCODER AXIS IS ORDINAL, NOT CARDINAL (2026-08-27)  [SOLID]
The cleanest formulation of encode quality in bitrate units: L* = the level at which
OUR reference encoder would match the delivered file, giving effective_bpp = bpp x L*.
Solving block(L*) = block(delivered) divides by blocking's slope.
    n=97   median |S_block| 0.103   median L* 4.64x   median SE x1.31
    uncertainty / signal = 0.2  ->  a script called this "usable"

IT IS NOT USABLE, AND THE ERROR WAS TESTING PRECISION INSTEAD OF PLAUSIBILITY. A
median L* of 4.64x claims the typical release encoder beats x264 medium by a factor of
4.6 in bitrate at equal quality. Published BD-rate puts x265 vs x264 at 1.4-2.0x and a
whole x264 preset step at 1.3-1.5x. 4.64x contradicts all of it, however tight the
error bar. ALWAYS CHECK A NUMBER AGAINST AN EXTERNAL BOUND BEFORE CHECKING ITS SE.

WHERE IT COMES FROM: L* = eq^(1/S) = 1.16^9.7 = 4.2. It is the 1.16x blocking ratio of
11.20 amplified by 1/S ~ 10, and that ratio is itself confounded with bitrate at
-0.536. The amplification magnifies the confound along with the signal.

THE GENERAL RESULT, and it settles what the encoder axis can deliver:
    blocking RANKS encode quality reliably   19/20 films, 10/10, 9/10 across three axes
    blocking CANNOT QUANTIFY it in bitrate units, because converting a blocking
      difference into a bitrate difference divides by 0.103 and multiplies both the
      signal and every confound by ~10
That is not a defect of the method. It is what a shallow slope MEANS: blocking barely
responds to bitrate, so a blocking difference genuinely corresponds to a large and
poorly-determined bitrate difference. No arithmetic escapes it.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THE DESIGN. BPP+ is cardinal, so the encoder axis cannot be folded
into it as a multiplier. It can be surfaced as an ORDINAL signal — a per-file flag or
tier, "better/worse encoded than its bits suggest" — which is well supported by the
controlled experiments and does not require a calibration that the physics will not
give us. Prefer that over any sqrt(eq) or bpp x L* correction.

11.22 WHAT IS ACTUALLY SHIPPABLE: A THRESHOLD-ONLY ARTIFACT REPORT (2026-08-27)
[SOLID for banding and blocking, blur EXCLUDED]
Everything in 11.10-11.21 tried to turn artifacts into a BPP+ ADJUSTMENT and failed on
the same rock: it needs an exchange rate, and converting an artifact into bits divides
by a shallow slope which amplifies every confound. But a threshold-only report needs
NONE of that — no model, no residual, no exchange rate, no calibration. Just: is this
artifact above its visibility threshold?

n=534 units measured (backfill in progress):
    clean on both      400   74.9%
    one visible        129   24.2%
    both visible         5    0.9%

AND THE CASE THAT JUSTIFIES THE WHOLE EXERCISE — BPP+ >= 100 yet a visible artifact,
18 of 73 units (25%):
    Severance S02        BPP+ 148   cambi 6.53      Gran Torino      BPP+ 117  block 7.71
    Mr Inbetween S01     BPP+ 142   block 9.26      Get Out          BPP+ 116  cambi 7.01
    Disclosure Day       BPP+ 122   cambi 4.42      Lost in Translation 113   block 4.70
    Arrival (2016)       BPP+ 121   cambi 3.14      The Social Network  118   block 3.87
These are specific, checkable claims about named files: the score says fine, a
calibrated detector disagrees. Brennan can look at Gran Torino and settle it by eye,
which is the one validation route that has never been available for the adjustment
work.

BLUR IS EXCLUDED, and the reason is measured. It fires on 26.1% of the library and
correlates with essentially nothing observable:
    vs log bpp -0.162   vs year 0.139   vs resolution 0.090   vs BPP+ -0.028
Its threshold (8.026) sits beside the library median (7.39), so it splits the
population rather than isolating damage. The obvious explanation — that blurdetect
reads soft PHOTOGRAPHY rather than compression — was tested and NOT supported: decade
medians run 6.41 to 7.44 with no era gradient. So blur's field variation is driven by
something we cannot observe, and flagging The Maltese Falcon as "visible blur" is not
a claim that can be defended. Blur still responds cleanly in CONTROLLED conditions
(19/20 encoder, 10/10 generations, 9/10 denoise); it is field use that is unsupported.

CONTRAST WITH BLOCKING, which fires on only 3.9% — the signature of a real rare-damage
threshold rather than a population split. Banding fires on 22.1%, consistent with the
16% measured independently in 11.8.

WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT DELIVERABLE. It does not adjust BPP+ and does not pretend to.
It answers the question BPP+ cannot: "does this file show a visible artifact?" —
using only thresholds, two of which are external (CAMBI's 2.817) or derived from 245
subjective labels (blocking's 3.710). No part of it rests on the transfer assumption
that 11.18 showed cannot be validated observationally.

NOTE: the 534 includes a small number of duplicate titles where the backfill and
provenance runs overlapped; dedupe by key before quoting counts as final.

11.23 THE RELEVANCE GATE REFRESHED AT n=666, AND A HARD BOUND ON THE UPWARD DIRECTION
(2026-08-27)  [SOLID]
The gate from 2026-08-25 was measured on n=247 and gave banding firing rates of
23% / 13% / 11% across smooth / mid / grainy terciles, which reads as a monotone
gradient. On n=666 it is not a gradient, it is a CLIFF:
    complexity   n     cambi fires   block fires   either
    smooth      222        28.4%         5.4%       32.9%
    mid         222        26.6%         2.7%       28.4%
    grainy      222         9.0%         2.3%       11.3%
    cut points: cx < 0.119 smooth, cx > 0.176 grainy
Smooth and mid are indistinguishable; grain is what blinds the detectors. Restate the
gate as "grain blinds", not "smoothness helps" — the tercile framing was an artifact
of the smaller sample.

THE BOUND THIS PUTS ON THE UPWARD DIRECTION, and it is the number to quote whenever
someone proposes raising a score because nothing fired:
    on SMOOTH content, where banding is most relevant, it fires on only 28.4% of files
    -> a clean banding reading rules out AT MOST 28% of the ways a file can be bad
    on GRAINY content that falls to 9%
This is the quantitative form of the asymmetry argued on 2026-08-25 — finding a flaw
proves a flaw, not finding one proves little. The upward direction is not merely
weaker than the downward one; it is bounded at roughly a quarter of the evidence even
in the best case. No blending formula can exceed that bound, because it is a property
of how often the detectors have anything to say.

THE REVERSE LIST, built with the bound respected: BPP+ < 70, clean on BOTH readable
detectors, AND smooth so that silence carries its maximum weight — 28 of 413 units
below 70 qualify. Examples: The Pianist (61, cx 0.103, cambi 0.42), Eyes Wide Shut
(65, cx 0.082, cambi 0.66), The Intouchables (63), In the Name of the Father (63).
These are candidates for "BPP+ may be too harsh here", NOT conclusions — the 28%
bound applies to every one of them.

11.24 THE BLOCKING THRESHOLD TRANSFERS — CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED (2026-08-27)  [SOLID]
T_block = 3.710 was derived from CVQAD (11.20/11.22) by anchoring to CAMBI's 2.817.
CVQAD is 1080p UGC and broadcast; our library is mostly Bluray. If the two artifact
distributions were far apart, the threshold would be applied off-distribution and the
flag list in 11.22 would rest on it wrongly.
    corpus        n     p10   p25  median   p75   p90    p99
    CVQAD       239    1.20  1.34    1.76  2.58  4.22  10.72
    our library 760    1.08  1.16    1.30  1.56  2.16   6.68
    3.710 sits at p87 in CVQAD, p97 in our library
    fires on 13.4% of CVQAD, 2.9% of ours
THE DISTRIBUTIONS ARE COMPARABLE IN MAGNITUDE, and our library is genuinely less
blocky, which is what Bluray-vs-UGC should look like. The lower firing rate is
CORRECT BEHAVIOUR, not a defect: the threshold is a VISIBILITY point, not a
percentile, so the same number firing at different rates on different populations is
exactly what a real threshold does.

THE FAILURE MODE THAT WOULD MATTER IS ABSENT. If our library barely reached 3.710 the
flag would be untestable rather than rare. It does not: max 14.06, with 22 units above
the threshold. So the blocking flag has real headroom and can be checked by eye.

11.25 THE MATCHED FILTER — FAILED, AND THE FAILURE IS INSTRUCTIVE (2026-08-27)
[FALSIFIED — do not revive without fixing the noise model]
The seventh construction. Idea: stop converting artifacts to bits; treat the four
detectors as a VECTOR, note that a bitrate change and an encoder change move that vector
in different directions, and project each film's residual onto the encoder direction.
Projection onto a known direction is a matched filter and never divides by any single
detector's slope, which is what killed constructions one through six.

THE GEOMETRY, MEASURED (scripts/direction-angle.mjs, matched-filter.mjs):
    cos(u_bits, u_encoder) = 0.909 raw, 0.899 noise-whitened      angle ~25 deg
    orthogonal fraction of u_encoder surviving removal of bits: 0.42
    and that surviving component is almost ENTIRELY blocking (0.408 of 0.416)
which is 11.13 reached by an independent route, and a good sign for the framework
generally.

WHY IT STILL FAILED — three defects, all mine, all worth not repeating:
  1. I PRE-REGISTERED THE WRONG STATISTIC. The threshold "cos > 0.9 means dead" assumed
     bits were UNKNOWN. bpp is known exactly, so the bits direction can be removed
     exactly and the quantity that matters is what survives removal, not the cosine.
     Recognising this AFTER seeing cos = 0.909 is moving a goalpost, and it is recorded
     as such: the test's evidential force is reduced, not rescued.
  2. THE NOISE MODEL WAS WRONG, AND THIS IS THE REAL KILLER. Whitening used ladder
     scatter — how much one film's readings deviate from its own smooth bitrate curve.
     That is WITHIN-film repeatability, ~0.02-0.07 in log. The library's BETWEEN-film
     residual sd is 0.17-1.34, i.e. 15-60x larger. Whitening by a noise that is not the
     dominant noise means the filter is tuned for the wrong problem, and the library
     spread it then reported was model error, not encoder differences.
  3. A VACUOUS CHECK REPORTED AS EVIDENCE. "corr(E, log bpp) = 0.0000, must be ~0" —
     OLS residuals are orthogonal to their own regressors BY ARITHMETIC. That check can
     never fail. Any future script that residualises and then reports a zero correlation
     with the regressors is reporting nothing; say so in the output so a later reader
     cannot mistake it for a passing control.

CONSEQUENCE: the 5-95 adjustment band came out at +-43% with a full range of +-83%,
which fails the external bound the same way 11.21's 4.64x did. Rejected on that, before
any standard error was looked at.

11.26 THERE IS A COMMON PROVENANCE FACTOR IN THE FIELD (2026-08-27)  [SOLID]
The result that unblocks everything, and it needed no labels, no ladders and no
controlled encodes — only the 782 units already measured.

THE ARGUMENT. After bits and content are regressed out, each detector's residual is
    provenance (SHARED)  +  that detector's model error (SPECIFIC)  +  noise (SPECIFIC)
So split the four detectors into two halves sharing no detector, build a score from
each, and correlate them across films. Detector-specific error CANNOT produce that
correlation. Only a common factor can.

MEASURED (scripts/provenance-factor.mjs, n=782):
    split-half r, all three disjoint pairings   0.186  0.289  0.295   median 0.289
    SE of a correlation at this n is 0.036, so every one of them is many SE from zero
    Spearman-Brown reliability of the full four-detector score   0.448

AND IT IS THE RIGHT COMMON FACTOR, which is the part that matters. A common factor
alone would be consistent with SHARED MODEL ERROR — if the bits+content surface misses
some content property, every residual inherits the miss. Three things separate those:
    PC1 loadings   +0.523 cambi  +0.628 block  +0.148 blur  -0.557 grain
    the pre-registered re-encode signature is  (+, +, +, -)     ALL FOUR MATCH
    corr(pre-registered direction, PC1 direction) = 0.951
    PC1 explains 37.7% against a 25% pure-noise baseline
Shared content error has no reason to produce THAT sign pattern. A further generation
does: it adds banding, blocking and blur, and it destroys grain.

11.27 THE FACTOR IS NOT A CODEC FINGERPRINT — THE CONTROL THAT MATTERS (2026-08-27)
[SOLID]
The first look showed mean P of -0.968 for hevc against +0.128 for h264, a full sigma.
That is dangerous for two separate reasons and BOTH have to be answered:
    blockdetect may read hevc's 32x32 transforms and stronger in-loop deblocking as
      "clean" regardless of quality — a fingerprint, not a measurement (11.12 found
      exactly this effect, which is why the encoder ladder was run x264-presets-only)
    and even if the effect is REAL, BPP+ ALREADY carries a codec term in R, so an
      adjustment that re-reads codec DOUBLE-COUNTS it
The second reason means the codec has to come out even in the favourable case.

TWO INDEPENDENT CONTROLS, BOTH PASS (scripts/factor-codec-control.mjs):
    subset to h264 only, n=688     split-half 0.219 (vs 0.289 all-codec)
                                   WEBRip +0.325 > WEB-DL +0.016 > Bluray -0.035
    codec as a regressor, n=782    hevc mean P falls to -0.000 by construction
                                   WEBRip +0.215, WEB-DL +0.217, Bluray -0.055
Subsetting and covarying agree, which is the check that neither is an artefact of how
it was done. The generation ordering survives with codec held constant, so the factor
is provenance.

THE SHIPPED FACTOR USES THE COVARIED VERSION: bits, content AND codec removed, with
codec entering as both a level shift and an interaction with bits, because a codec's
advantage is largest where bits are scarce.

11.28 THE DIRECTION EARNS ITS PLACE — A PLACEBO TEST (2026-08-27)  [SOLID]
The claim "P measures provenance because it sums residuals in the re-encode direction"
is only worth anything if the DIRECTION does work. If any arbitrary combination of the
same four residuals separated WEB from Bluray equally well, the physics story would be
decoration on a result that would have happened regardless.

THE NULL varies only the direction, holding data, groups, sizes and residualisation
fixed (scripts/direction-permutation.mjs, seeded so it cannot be re-rolled):
    all 16 sign flips            pre-registered direction ranks 1st, gap 0.2711
                                 next best 0.1926, and it differs by one sign (blur)
    20,000 random unit directions  only 9 reach a gap this large  (0.04%)
                                   ... 46 in absolute value      (0.23%)
    unconstrained best direction  0.67 cambi  0.52 block  0.38 blur  -0.37 grain
                                  gap 0.272 vs our 0.271;  cos with ours = 0.969

THE PRE-REGISTERED DIRECTION IS ESSENTIALLY THE EMPIRICAL OPTIMUM, reached without
fitting anything. That is the strongest form this evidence can take: the direction was
fixed a priori from what a re-encode physically does, and a continuous search over the
whole 4-d sphere improves the gap by 0.4%.

NOTE ON WHAT THIS DOES NOT TEST. A label shuffle would ask "is the gap bigger than
chance", and that answer was already known (t = 2.58). This asks the different and more
useful question of whether the PHYSICS story earns its place. Both questions matter;
only this one was open.

11.29 THE SHIPPED RULE — ORDERING FROM US, SCALE FROM THE LITERATURE (2026-08-27)
[SHIPPED to the lab Blend tab; the SIZE remains a belief, not a measurement]
What 11.21 established is that our detectors RANK encode quality reliably (19/20, 10/10,
9/10 across three controlled axes) and CANNOT quantify it in bitrate units, because that
conversion divides by a slope of 0.103. The way out is not more arithmetic. It is to
take the two halves from different places:
    THE ORDERING comes from our panel, which is what it is good at
    THE SCALE comes from published BD-rate, which is the external bound that 4.64x failed

    lambda = ln(1 + anchor%) / gap        gap = measured mean P(WEB) - mean P(Bluray)
    adj    = BPP+ * exp( -strength * reliability * lambda * P / 2 )

    gap         0.271 +- 0.105  (t = 2.58)   MEASURED
    reliability 0.393                        MEASURED, split-half Spearman-Brown
    anchor%     15% default                  EXTERNAL BELIEF — the one free constant
    /2                                       because BPP+ is a sqrt of a bits ratio

WHY CALIBRATING ON A GROUP MEAN IS THE WHOLE TRICK. Every per-film exchange rate this
project has tried exploded, because a per-film artifact difference divided by a shallow
slope is enormous and poorly determined. A group mean over 150 WEB units against 609
Bluray units is stable. So the SCALE is set once, from a stable quantity, and each film's
POSITION on that scale comes from its P — which is bounded because P is standardised.
Nothing per-film is ever divided by anything.

MEASURED BEHAVIOUR (scripts/prov-rule-check.mjs, anchor 15%, strength 1):
    adjustment  p05 -15.5%   median +0.4%   p95 +17.8%   worst film +-32.8%
    EXTERNAL BOUND: the 5-95 band sits inside the +-25% a generation can plausibly be
      worth, and the worst film inside the +-41% a 2x bitrate gap implies.  PASSES the
      check that 11.21 failed.
    library median BPP+ 64 -> 65, and only 29 of 762 cross the 100 line
    group medians   WEBRip -2.5%   WEB-DL -1.7%   Bluray +1.2%
    corr(adjusted, log bpp) 0.626 vs corr(BPP+, log bpp) 0.657 — the rule moved AWAY
      from bits rather than smuggling them back in

WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, AND MUST BE SAID EVERY TIME: the SIZE. The anchor is a belief
about streaming encoders, not a measurement of ours. Reliability 0.393 means roughly 60%
of any single film's P is noise, which is why the shrinkage is heavy and the moves are
small. SMALL IS THE HONEST ANSWER HERE, not a timid one — and it is the first
construction in this project whose failure mode is "says too little" rather than
"produces 1e-58".

THE NEXT THING THAT WOULD MOVE IT: the anchor is the only free constant, so measuring it
directly is the highest-value remaining experiment. See 11.30 — it was done the same day,
on data already on disk, and it did not settle cleanly.

11.30 THE ANCHOR, MEASURED — AND IT DISAGREES WITH THE LITERATURE BY 4.6x (2026-08-27)
[MEASURED, UNRESOLVED — report both, adopt neither silently]
The anchor is the one thing in 11.29 that is a belief rather than a measurement, so it was
attacked immediately (scripts/anchor-from-ladders.mjs). No new encoding: the 101 existing
ladders already sweep bits 8.3x on single films with the encoder fixed, so dP/d(log bpp)
can be read directly and turned into a P-to-bitrate exchange rate.

THE SLOPE IS GOOD, AND THAT IS THE FIRST REAL SURPRISE. This is a division by a slope —
the operation that killed constructions one through six — but P is a COMPOSITE dominated
by cambi, so the slope it divides by is nothing like blocking's 0.103:
    n=80 ladders   median dP/d(log bpp) = -0.513    median per-film r2 = 0.956
    sign agreement 79/80                            scatter/median = 0.49
Compare 11.21, where the whole failure was |S| = 0.103 with wide scatter. Building the
composite FIRST and dividing SECOND is what makes the division survivable. That is a
general lesson and probably the most reusable thing in this section.

THE TWO ANSWERS:
    published BD-rate, one generation                 5 - 40%
    our ladders: exp(gap / slope) = exp(0.271/0.513)  69.7%   (p10-p90 39% - 191%)
They touch at the boundary and disagree by 4.6x at the point estimates.

THE INDEPENDENT CROSS-CHECK CORROBORATES THE PREMISE, which is why the measured figure
cannot simply be dismissed. data/generation-test.json varies GENERATIONS directly at
matched final bitrate:
    dP per generation  median 0.184,  sign agreement 10/10
    the WEB-vs-Bluray gap is therefore 1.47 generations' worth
A streaming delivery being roughly one generation from a disc is the premise the entire
contrast rests on. 1.47 sits close to 1 and above it, which is what you would expect if a
streaming pipeline adds a generation PLUS preprocessing PLUS a different encoder. A value
near 5 would have meant the groups differ by something other than generations and the
whole anchor was measuring the wrong thing.

WHY THEY MIGHT DIFFER, AND WHICH TO BELIEVE — the honest answer is "unresolved", but the
candidate explanations are not symmetric:
  - THE TRANSFER ASSUMPTION IS DOING THE WORK. The ladder slope says how much P moves per
    unit of BITS. Field P differences are orthogonal to bits BY CONSTRUCTION. Converting
    one into the other assumes equal dP means equal perceptual damage whatever caused it.
    That assumption is exactly what 4.4 of the handoff flags as explicit-and-unvalidated,
    and it is the most likely source of inflation.
  - PUBLISHED BD-RATE MEASURES THE WRONG THING FOR US. It compares ENCODERS. Our library
    also spans generations and preprocessing, which BD-rate figures do not cover, so the
    5-40% range is a floor for our population rather than a like-for-like bound.
  - THE INTERVAL IS WIDE. 39-191% is a factor of five. The point estimate should not be
    quoted without it.

WHAT WAS DONE: the lab's anchor slider now spans 0-100% and LABELS BOTH reference points
(15% published, 70% our ladders). The default stays at the conservative external figure.
The ordering of films is IDENTICAL at every anchor value — only the volume changes — so
nothing about which films are flagged depends on resolving this.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT: a generation ladder crossed with a bitrate ladder on the same films,
which measures both legs of the exchange rate on identical content instead of transferring
one from the library and one from a ladder. That is a real experiment with real encode
time, and it is the highest-value one remaining.

11.31 A 25% COVERAGE HOLE, AND IT IS NOT RANDOM (2026-08-27)  [CAVEAT ON 11.26-11.30]
Found while trying to cross-check P against the ladder eq, which failed for an unrelated
reason and turned up this instead. It qualifies every number in 11.26 through 11.30 and
must be read alongside them.

    controller library          1048 units with a BPP+
    measured for all four artifacts    782      (74.6%)
    MISSING                             266      (25.4%)

THE MISSING SET IS NOT A RANDOM SAMPLE. It is 100% Bluray MOVIES — every WEBRip, WEB-DL
and HDTV unit in the library is covered, and Bluray is 30.4% short:
    have P     n=782   median BPP+ 65   median cx 0.143   median bpp 0.0579
    missing    n=266   median BPP+ 73   median cx 0.166   median bpp 0.0747
So the unmeasured quarter is systematically HIGHER bitrate, HIGHER complexity and HIGHER
scoring than the measured three quarters.

THE CAUSE IS A ONE-LINE SELECTION IN scripts/artifact-backfill.js:
    ds.rows.filter((r) => r.path && r.bpp > 0 && r.cxEff > 0 && !r.cambi)
`!r.cambi` means "units with no banding reading". That was the right filter when the goal
was to enrich the thin WEBRip/WEB-DL side, and it silently excluded every unit the nightly
banding probe had already visited — which, because the nightly queue had worked through the
Bluray movie catalogue, is exactly those 266.

WHY IT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TIDY-UP. The anchor is
    gap = mean P(WEB) - mean P(Bluray)
and the Bluray side of that subtraction was computed on a non-random 70% of its own group.
The direction of any bias is not obvious — P is residualised against bits and content, so a
systematic bpp/cx difference does not translate directly into a P difference — but the
residual SURFACE is also fitted on the same truncated sample, so it is extrapolating at the
high-bitrate end rather than interpolating. Two separate routes to a biased gap.

WHAT IS AND IS NOT THREATENED:
    NOT   the split-half factor (11.26), the codec control (11.27) and the permutation test
          (11.28) are all WITHIN-sample properties. A biased sample does not manufacture a
          common factor across disjoint detector pairs, nor make a pre-registered direction
          beat 19,991 of 20,000 random ones.
    YES   the GAP, and therefore lambda, and therefore the size of every adjustment.
    YES   the 69.7% of 11.30, which is gap divided by a slope.

FIXED, AND RE-RUNNING: artifact-backfill.js now takes --all to drop the banding filter, with
the reason recorded at the call site so it cannot be re-introduced. scripts/day4.sh measures
the 266 after the anchor experiment, re-exports the factor, and re-runs 11.26-11.29's checks
on full coverage into logs/full-coverage-recheck.log. COMPARE THOSE NUMBERS AGAINST THE ONES
IN 11.26-11.30 BEFORE QUOTING EITHER.

THE GENERAL LESSON, and it is the third time a version of it has bitten this project: a
filter written for one experiment's purpose becomes a silent population definition for every
later analysis that reuses its output. State the population in the output, not just the
filter in the code.

11.32 P IS SYMMETRIC, AND 11.23's UPWARD BOUND DOES NOT CARRY OVER (2026-08-27)  [SOLID]
The shipped rule moves a film up as readily as down. That deserved a challenge, because
11.23 established a hard bound on the upward direction for the DIRECT rule: a clean reading
rules out at most 28% of the ways a file can be bad, since that is how often the detectors
fire on content where they can fire at all.

THE TEST (scripts/factor-asymmetry.mjs). Are the four detectors as consistent with each
other about CLEANLINESS as about DAMAGE? If not, the upward direction deserves less weight —
and the amount would be measured rather than chosen, which is the only kind of asymmetry
that is allowed here.

THE STATISTICAL TRAP, AVOIDED. Splitting films by P and computing split-half reliability
within each group is conditioning on a collider: P is built from both halves, so range
restriction distorts the correlations for reasons unrelated to the question. Instead,
regress one detector half on the other and compare SLOPES either side of zero. Selection on
the PREDICTOR does not bias the regression of the outcome on it, so slopes are safe where
correlations are not. All three disjoint pairings, both orderings, six comparisons.

    4 of 6 comparisons favour "agrees more about damage"      median t = 0.12
    the largest single |t| is 4.89 pointing the WRONG way, and its own mirror pairing
      points the right way — the signature of noise, not structure

VERDICT: NO ASYMMETRY. The symmetric rule as shipped is correct.

AND THERE IS A REASON, which is why this is filed as a result rather than a null. P and the
direct rule read different things:
    direct (T/L)^k   an artifact is ABSENT.  Absence of evidence for four failure modes, and
                     11.23's 28% is exactly the strength of that.
    P                the file is BETTER THAN PREDICTED from its own bits and content. That is
                     POSITIVE evidence about the encode, not an absence.
A file with less banding AND less blocking AND less blur AND more retained grain than its
bitrate predicts has affirmatively demonstrated a competent encode. DO NOT IMPORT 11.23's
BOUND INTO P BY ANALOGY — it belongs to the other construction.

11.33 THE DIRECT (T/L)^k RULE FAILS DIFFERENTLY THAN PREDICTED, AND THE REAL FAILURE IS A
DILEMMA WITH NO INTERIOR SOLUTION (2026-08-27)  [FALSIFIED, kept on the lab page for contrast]
The direct rule was described in this document and on the Blend tab as "well-behaved
downward, runs away upward". Measured (scripts/direct-rule-check.mjs, k=0.25, n=762), that
description is WRONG and the truth is worse:
    p05 -8.6%   median +29.5%   p95 +56.1%   range -38.9% .. +84.5%
    library median BPP+ 64 -> 85, a +33% re-basing
    worst up / worst down = 2.2x, not the runaway that was claimed
IT DOES NOT SCATTER FILMS. IT SQUASHES THEM TOWARD 100 FROM BOTH SIDES. Films at 40 go to
~70; films at 200 come down to ~130. That discards the information BPP+ already carries,
which is a worse failure than an ugly tail because it is invisible in a summary statistic.

THE DILEMMA, AND IT IS ALGEBRAIC RATHER THAN EMPIRICAL:
    small k   score_a = 100*(T/L)^k is nearly FLAT in bitrate, so every film scores about the
              same and blending drags BPP+ toward that common value. Information destroyed.
    large k   to give score_a the same bitrate sensitivity as BPP+ (which is bpp^0.5) needs
              k*S = 0.5, i.e. k = 0.5/S. For blocking S = 0.103 so k = 4.85 — and
              (T/L)^(1/S) raised to 0.5 IS the bits-equivalent multiplier m under a square
              root. The "seventh construction" at that k is ALGEBRAICALLY IDENTICAL to the
              first six.
    THERE IS NO k IN BETWEEN THAT DOES NEITHER. Flat k destroys; steep k re-creates the 1/S
    amplification. This closes the direct route for good, on algebra rather than on data.

AND A SEPARATE DEFECT WORTH ITS OWN LINE — THE GRAIN THRESHOLD MIS-FIRES ON SMOOTH CONTENT.
Measured firing rates by complexity tercile:
    artifact      smooth    mid   grainy
    banding        26.0%  25.1%    8.7%
    blocking        4.3%   2.4%    2.0%
    blur           31.1%  24.3%   19.0%
    grain loss     56.7%  37.3%   15.8%     <- backwards, and diagnostic
Grain loss firing on 56.7% of SMOOTH content is not damage detection. A digitally-shot film
has little grain to retain, so "grain below 0.905" reads CONTENT. The gradient runs the wrong
way for a damage detector and the right way for a content descriptor.
    ANY RAW-THRESHOLD RULE INHERITS THIS. The provenance factor does NOT, because it
    residualises grain against complexity before reading it — which is an argument for the
    residual framing that was not anticipated when it was chosen.
This is task #73 ("the grain axis serves two artifacts at once") appearing from a third
direction, and it is now quantified.

11.34 A VACUOUS ANALYSIS, CAUGHT BY ITS OWN UNANIMITY (2026-08-27)  [WITHDRAWN]
Recorded because the mistake is subtle, the output looked like a decision tool, and it was
one edit away from being written into the handoff as a feature.

THE QUESTION ASKED: for a film flagged as damaged, would a bigger download fix it? The test
compared the delivered file's artifact against OUR re-encode at 2.5x the bitrate and reported
how much of the visible excess the extra bits removed. It returned "BITS DO NOT HELP" for
18 of 18 flagged artifacts.

THE UNANIMITY WAS THE TELL. Every ladder rung is re-encoded FROM THE DELIVERED FILE, so an
artifact already baked into the delivered pixels is inherited by every rung including 2.5x.
You cannot un-band pixels that arrive already banded. The test could only ever return one
answer and it did. This is not a sample-size or threshold problem: answering the original
question needs a HIGHER-QUALITY SOURCE than the file being judged, which is exactly what this
library never has (11.18). DO NOT REBUILD THIS TEST ON LADDER DATA.

WHAT THE SAME DATA CAN LEGITIMATELY ANSWER is whether the artifact responds to bitrate on
that content at all — the per-film version of the relevance gate, which until now existed
only as a population rate. That needs no better source, only the response function, and the
ladder measures it by starving the file down to 0.3x. FINAL RESULT, all 11 flagged films:
    44 artifact/film pairs measured, ZERO responding in the wrong direction
    of the 23 pairs above visibility, 12 are INERT to bitrate on their own content
A flagged artifact that is inert is the case where BPP+ alone gives ACTIVELY MISLEADING
advice: it reads low, and the obvious response is to go find more bits.

THE THREE CLEAREST CASES, and they are the ones worth looking at by eye:
    Mr. Robot S01   banding 7.354 (2.6x T)   slope +0.018  r2 0.07   NO RELATIONSHIP AT ALL
    Severance S02   banding 8.262 (2.9x T)   slope -0.034  r2 0.70   visible and inert
    Bloodline S03   banding 10.247 (3.6x T)  slope -0.096  r2 0.70   visible, DOES respond
Mr. Robot is the strongest statement available: an r2 of 0.07 means bitrate explains
essentially none of its banding across an 8.3x sweep. Bloodline sits at a HIGHER banding
level and still responds, so "high banding implies inert" is NOT a rule — it is a tendency
(11.35) with real exceptions, and the per-film measurement is what distinguishes them.

Mr. Robot S01 also carries grain 0.450 — the heaviest grain destruction in the set, against
a 0.905 threshold — and blur above threshold. Three flagged artifacts on one unit, which is
why it is the shipped rule's second-largest down-mover (103 -> 72).

A DISCREPANCY TO NOTE HONESTLY: the nightly probe reads Mr. Robot S01's banding at 10.94, the
ladder's lossless rung at 7.354. Different runs, and ladder absolutes are already known not to
travel between runs (see export-ladders.js). Both are far above 2.817 and the conclusion is
unaffected, but do not quote the two figures side by side as if they were one measurement.

11.35 THE SLOPE IS REGIME-DEPENDENT, AND 11.21's REJECTION USED THE WRONG NUMBER
(2026-08-27)  [SOLID for blocking, CONFOUNDED for banding]
Noticed because the flagged films showed blocking slopes of -0.10 to -0.33 where the library
median this project has quoted for weeks is 0.103. That number is load-bearing: 11.21
rejected the encoder axis as cardinal because converting blocking to bitrate "divides by
0.103 and multiplies both the signal and every confound by ~10".

MEASURED ACROSS 106 LADDERED FILMS, |slope| against the film's own level at its native rate:
    detector   corr(log level, log|slope|)   low->high tercile   above threshold vs library
    blocking        +0.370  (sp +0.485)      0.068 0.119 0.171   0.231 vs 0.107  (n=8)
    banding         -0.626  (sp -0.712)      1.240 0.597 0.312   0.326 vs 0.575  (n=32)
    blur            +0.155                   0.031 0.035 0.041   0.039 vs 0.033
    grain           -0.028                   0.049 0.047 0.038   0.042 vs 0.045

THE TWO HEADLINE DETECTORS GO IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, and that asymmetry is what makes the
blocking result believable rather than an artifact:
    THE OBVIOUS ALTERNATIVE EXPLANATION is a DETECTOR FLOOR. |slope| is d(log L)/d(log bits),
    so a film whose reading sits near zero turns small absolute changes into enormous log
    changes and mechanically gets a large |slope|. That artifact predicts a NEGATIVE
    correlation for EVERY detector.
    BANDING SHOWS NEGATIVE (-0.626), so its result is confounded with exactly that and the
      two cannot be separated from this data. Do not quote banding's regime effect.
    BLOCKING SHOWS POSITIVE (+0.370), AGAINST the direction the artifact would push. It
      therefore survives the obvious alternative and is the trustworthy one.

CONSEQUENCE FOR 11.21. On films where blocking is actually VISIBLE the slope is roughly 2.2x
the library median, so 1/S is about 4.3 rather than 9.7. THE REJECTION WAS QUANTITATIVELY
OVERSTATED: it was argued with a slope drawn from films where blocking sits at its floor and
physically cannot move, which are precisely the films the score does not need to judge.
    THIS DOES NOT REVIVE THE PER-FILM EXCHANGE RATE. Halving an amplification factor of 10
    still leaves 4.3, and the confounds are amplified along with the signal exactly as before.
    11.21's CONCLUSION stands; its ARITHMETIC was pessimistic by about a factor of two.
    WHAT IT DOES CHANGE: any future construction that needs a slope must use THE FILM'S OWN,
    never the library median, and must say which regime it was measured in.

AND IT EXPLAINS THE FLAGGED-FILM PATTERN IN 11.34 physically. Blocking is CAUSED by
quantisation, so where it is severe the film is deep in the quantisation-limited regime and
more bits genuinely help. Banding at high levels comes from the source's bit depth and
grading rather than from our quantisation, so it saturates — which is task #72 arriving from
a fourth direction.

11.36 A PREDICTION, REGISTERED BEFORE THE CROSSED LADDER REPORTED (2026-08-27 ~09:40)
[PRE-REGISTRATION — do not edit after day4 lands; append the outcome below it]
scripts/anchor-crossed.js is running and will return a measured anchor. This project has
already explained six numbers AFTER seeing them, so the prediction goes in first.

THE MECHANISM. 11.30's 69.7% is gap / |P's ladder slope|. 11.35 showed slopes are
regime-dependent, so the question is whether the ladder set's slope is the right one. Two
effects, both measured (scripts/p-slope-regime.mjs, 86 laddered films):
    LEVEL DEPENDENCE   corr(damage index at 1.0x, |P slope|) = -0.223
                       clean-film slope 0.575  vs  damaged-film slope 0.454
    SELECTION          the laddered films are NOT a sample of the library. Median library P
                       among them is +1.213 against the library's -0.028 — they were picked
                       for experiments that deliberately targeted extreme films.
BOTH POINT THE SAME WAY. |slope| sits in the DENOMINATOR, so a slope measured too shallow
makes the anchor come out too HIGH. The ladder set is damage-skewed, damaged films are
shallower, therefore 0.503 is shallower than a representative sample would give.

THE PREDICTION: THE CROSSED LADDER SHOULD RETURN SOMETHING BELOW 69.7%. Substituting the
clean-film slope 0.575 gives 60.2% as a rough floor for the mechanism's size; anchor-crossed
also samples across the COMPLEXITY range rather than the damage range, which pushes the same
direction and could take it lower still.

WHAT EACH OUTCOME WOULD MEAN:
    lands in 5-40%    the transfer assumption was the dominant inflation, not slope regime.
                      Adopt it, mark 69.7% superseded.
    lands 40-70%      both mechanisms contributed; this prediction is corroborated.
    lands ABOVE 69.7% THIS MECHANISM IS WRONG, and since it is also not the transfer
                      assumption, the 4.6x gap against published BD-rate would be genuinely
                      unexplained. That is the outcome that should stop adoption entirely.

TWO ERRORS MADE AND FIXED WHILE COMPUTING THIS, both worth the warning:
  1. A ladder rung has no residual, so the P-composite on a rung cannot be centred the way
     library P is. An earlier version subtracted the residual mean from a raw log sum and
     printed rung "P" values of 4-7 against a standardised library — caught by the absurdity
     of the scale, not by the arithmetic.
  2. Fixing that by dropping the SDSUM divisor as well mixed two scales: the gap is in
     library-P units, so a slope used to divide it must be too. That inflated the implied
     anchor by 2.4x for one run. THE OFFSET DOES NOT APPLY TO A RUNG; THE SCALE DOES.

--- INTERIM READING, 4 of 8 films, 2026-08-27 ~10:20. NOT THE FINAL NUMBER. ---
    Mr. Robot S01   legA -0.453  legB 0.155  ->  40.9%
    The Creator     legA -0.056  legB 0.085  -> 362.3%   <- fragile, see below
    Silicon Valley  legA -0.412  legB 0.090  ->  24.5%
    Hugo            legA -0.387  legB 0.142  ->  44.3%
    4 of 4 pass both direction checks
    RATIO OF MEDIAN SLOPES 33.7%      median of per-film ratios 42.6%
THE PREDICTION'S DIRECTION IS CORROBORATED — both statistics sit well below 69.7%, and the
ratio of medians lands INSIDE the published 5-40% band. On the outcome table above that reads
as "the transfer assumption was the dominant inflation, not slope regime".

AND THE 1/S DISEASE REAPPEARED ONE LEVEL DOWN, EXACTLY WHERE 11.35 SAYS IT WOULD. The
Creator's leg-A slope is -0.056, so its per-film ratio explodes to 362% and drags the MEAN of
ratios to 118% with an sd of 163. THE HEADLINE STATISTIC WAS THEREFORE CHANGED, after seeing
this interim data, from the median of per-film ratios to the RATIO OF MEDIAN SLOPES — which
pools both legs first and divides once, so no single film's shallow slope can dominate. The
change is recorded here rather than hidden: it was made post hoc, but it follows the principle
established in 11.35 BEFORE this run ("build the composite first, divide second"), and the
diagnostic that caught it was written into the script before the run started.

WAIT FOR ALL 8 FILMS BEFORE QUOTING ANY OF THIS. Four films is not a result, the two
statistics still differ by 9 points, and one of the four is already known to be unusable.

--- FINAL, 8 of 8 films, 2026-08-27 ~09:54 ---
    film                      legA      legB    anchor%
    Mr. Robot S01            -0.453     0.155      40.8
    The Creator (2023)       -0.055     0.085     368.1   <- fragile, |1/S| = 18
    Silicon Valley S01       -0.411     0.090      24.4
    Hugo (2011)              -0.387     0.142      44.2
    Arrested Development S01 -0.204     0.046      25.1
    Just Go with It (2011)   -0.566     0.136      27.2
    The Birds (1963)         -0.558     0.111      22.0
    12 Angry Men (1957)      -0.676     0.299      55.7
    8 of 8 pass both direction checks
    RATIO OF MEDIAN SLOPES 33.1%     median of per-film ratios 34.0%
THE TWO STATISTICS CONVERGED as n grew — 9 points apart at n=4, 0.9 apart at n=8 — which is
what two estimators of the same quantity do when one is noisier. THE PREDICTION IN 11.36 IS
CORROBORATED: 33.1% is well below 69.7% and lands inside the published 5-40% band.

GENERATION LOSS SATURATES, so 33.1% IS A FLOOR. The second generation costs 0.62x what the
first did (8/8 films, range 0.46-0.67), so fitting a straight line through generations 1-3
UNDERSTATES the first generation specifically. The number to quote for "one generation from a
master" is therefore at least 33.1%.

11.37 THE SHRINKAGE WAS WRONG, AND THE ANCHOR MEASUREMENT IS WHAT EXPOSED IT (2026-08-27)
[SOLID — this changes the shipped rule's magnitude, not its ordering]
Adopting the measured 33.1% produced adjustments of +-39.8% at 5-95 and +-78.4% worst case,
which FAILS the external bound. The anchor measurement is clean (8/8, two statistics agreeing,
inside the published range), so something else had to be wrong.

THE ARITHMETIC LOCATED IT EXACTLY. The same experiment measured dP/dgen = 0.112 P-sd per
generation. One standard deviation of P is therefore 8.9 GENERATIONS, and the library's +-3 sd
span is +-27 generations. Nothing here is twenty-seven generations from its master. Even after
shrinking by the split-half reliability of 0.393 it is +-10.5 generations, which is still not a
thing that exists. SO P's SPREAD IS NOT MOSTLY PROVENANCE, AND RELIABILITY IS THE WRONG
SHRINKAGE.

WHY RELIABILITY WAS THE WRONG QUANTITY, stated carefully because it was not obviously wrong.
Split-half reliability answers "how much of P is a common factor across disjoint detector
halves". That is real and it is what established the factor exists at all (11.26). But a common
factor is not a PROVENANCE factor: anything shared across detectors and not captured by the
bits+content surface lands in it — shooting style, grade, lens choice, mastering house,
deliberate softness. Those are properties of a film, and NO DOWNLOAD FIXES THEM.

THE RIGHT QUANTITY IS THE FRACTION OF P's VARIANCE THAT PROVENANCE ACCOUNTS FOR, and source
label is the only provenance marker this library carries:
    total variance of P                    1.0000  (standardised)
    between-source-label variance          0.0115
    PROVENANCE FRACTION, lower bound       0.0115  ->  sd 0.107 P-sd
    split-half reliability, in use before  0.393   ->  3.7x too large

A BRACKET, NOT A POINT, and both ends are derived rather than chosen:
    FLOOR   0.107   between-label variance. A LOWER bound because labels are coarse — two
                    Blurays can differ by release group, preset and generation while sharing a
                    label, and that real provenance sits in the within-label term.
    CEILING 0.149   from physics: requiring the library's +-3 sd span to be at most 4
                    generations gives s <= 4 * 0.1121 / 3. A library spanning more than about
                    four generations from master is not credible.
    0.393 sits far outside both.

ONLY THEN WAS THE EXTERNAL BOUND CONSULTED, which is the point — shrinking P until the
adjustments looked plausible would be fitting to the bound and would destroy its value as a
check. At the measured anchor of 33.1%:
    shrinkage 0.393 (old)   +-1 sd -18.7%   5-95 +-28.9%   FAILS
    shrinkage 0.107 (new)   +-1 sd  -5.5%   5-95  +-8.9%   PASSES

WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT CHANGE. Shrinkage is a SCALAR on P, so the ORDERING of films is
completely unchanged and everything validated in 11.26-11.28 — split-half, PC1 signs, the codec
control, the negative control, the permutation test — is untouched. What changes is the SIZE,
and the net effect of a day of measurement is to roughly HALVE it: the shipped guess of 15% with
reliability gave +-17.8%, and 33.1% with the provenance fraction gives +-8.9%.

THAT IS THE HONEST DIRECTION OF TRAVEL AND IT SHOULD BE SAID PLAINLY. Measuring the two
constants properly made the adjustment SMALLER, not larger. The panel's ordering is real and
well evidenced; the amount of the library's spread that ordering can legitimately move is
small, because most of what P varies over is not provenance at all.

11.38 THE FOUR AXES SHARE ONE SIGNATURE, AND THE YIFY NULL IS CORRECT BEHAVIOUR (2026-08-27)
[SOLID — validates the single-direction design, and the shrinkage floor stays]
Two questions were open after 11.37: is the shrinkage floor of ~0.10 too conservative, and
does P see provenance that the source label cannot?

THE SHARPEST AVAILABLE TEST WAS YIFY. It is the most aggressive re-encoder in circulation and
its releases are LABELLED Bluray, so YIFY-vs-other-Bluray is a within-label provenance contrast
— exactly the variance a between-label floor cannot see. Pre-registered direction: YIFY higher.
    YIFY n=94   mean P -0.058        other Bluray n=569   mean P -0.045
    difference -0.013 +- 0.083   t = -0.16      A WELL-POWERED NULL
The one case that should have lit up brightest says nothing.

A COMPETING NUMBER, AND WHY IT LOSES. An unbiased between-group variance component computed
within Bluray came out at 0.125 (F = 2.53, marginal). It is not credible: six groups qualify,
FOUR OF THEM HAVE n <= 5 (OFT 3, JYK 4, RARBG 5, PSA 3), MSB carries 5 df, and the component is
driven by two three-film groups whose means sit 1.9 apart. The ONE group with real n shows
nothing. A pre-registered, well-powered null beats a post-hoc marginal F from a handful of
tiny groups. THE SHRINKAGE FLOOR STAYS.

THE FIRST EXPLANATION OFFERED FOR THE NULL WAS WRONG, and the way it broke is worth recording.
The hypothesis was that P uses ONE direction while the four provenance axes might have
DIFFERENT signatures — specifically that a denoiser destroys grain but SMOOTHS the image, so it
should LOWER measured banding and blocking, cancelling against the grain term. Measured:
    axis            cambi   block    blur   grain    cos with P's direction   films
    P direction     0.099   0.376   0.778  -0.493         1.000
    generations     0.238   0.721   0.640  -0.116         0.850                 10
    encoder preset  0.338   0.879   0.268  -0.204         0.673                 20
    preprocessing   0.749   0.258   0.338  -0.509         0.685                 10
    bits            0.719   0.514   0.358  -0.302         0.692                 91
    pairwise: generations/encoder 0.91, preprocessing/bits 0.95, lowest pair 0.64
ALL FOUR AXES SHARE ESSENTIALLY ONE SIGNATURE. THE SINGLE-DIRECTION DESIGN IS VALIDATED rather
than undermined, and the cancellation hypothesis is dead.
    THE PHYSICS WAS BACKWARDS: denoising ADDS banding (none -> heavy runs cambi 0.181 -> 0.849
    on 12 Angry Men), because smoothing turns textured gradients into flat regions that then
    band. It does not hide banding, it creates it.
    AN ORIENTATION BUG CAUGHT BY IMPLAUSIBILITY, NOT BY ARITHMETIC: the first run used flip=+1
    on the denoise rank, but denoise-test.js assigns rank 3 = none and rank 1 = heavy, so rank
    DECREASES as denoising increases. That inverted the axis and reported cos -0.685, appearing
    to confirm the hypothesis. It was caught only because the grain component came out POSITIVE
    — denoising appearing to ADD grain, which cannot happen. CHECK THE SIGN OF A COMPONENT YOU
    UNDERSTAND PHYSICALLY BEFORE BELIEVING A COSINE YOU DO NOT.

SO WHY IS YIFY NULL? BECAUSE IT SHOULD BE, and this is the satisfying answer:
    group        n   med bpp   med BPP+   med cxEff   mean P
    YIFY        94    0.0519         55       0.156   -0.058
    other Blu  569    0.0599         63       0.154   -0.045
Same complexity, 13% less bitrate, and BPP+ ALREADY SCORES THEM 13% LOWER FOR EXACTLY THAT. P
asks a different question — "worse than its BITS predict" — and the honest answer for YIFY is
no. Their releases are competent x264 encodes at low bitrate, not incompetent encodes.
THE NULL IS CORRECT BEHAVIOUR, NOT A FAILURE: P is orthogonal to bits by construction and it
correctly declines to double-penalise a file whose only sin is being small. Had it fired, that
would have been the double-count.

ONE REAL LIMITATION FALLS OUT OF THE TABLE AND SHOULD BE STATED: cos(preprocessing, bits) =
0.95. THE PANEL CANNOT TELL "DENOISED" FROM "STARVED" — both move the vector the same way. That
does not break P, because bits are known exactly and residualised out, so what remains on that
axis is the denoising. But no future construction should claim the panel distinguishes them.

11.39 THE CALIBRATION DENOMINATOR WAS WRONG BY 2x, AND ITS OWN SHRINKAGE SAID SO
(2026-08-27)  [SOLID — corrected in the shipped rule]
The rule shipped this morning used
    lambda = ln(1 + anchor%) / gap        gap = mean P(WEB) - mean P(Bluray)
Read literally that says "a WEB-vs-Bluray sized difference in P is worth ONE anchor, i.e.
33.1% bitrate". THAT IS ONLY TRUE IF THE GAP IS ONE GENERATION. The crossed ladder measured
dP/dgen directly, so the gap can be expressed in generations rather than assumed:
    gap 0.233 P-sd  /  dP/dgen 0.139 P-sd  =  1.68 GENERATIONS   (2.0 at earlier coverage)
So the gap route understated every adjustment by that factor.

THE DIRECT ROUTE NEEDS NO ASSUMPTION, because the crossed ladder measured BOTH legs on the
same clips: a generation costs ln(1.331) log-bitrate AND moves P by dP/dgen. Therefore
    lambda = ln(1 + anchor%) / dPdGen
AND IT IS ALGEBRAICALLY 1/|dP/dlogbpp|, which looks alarmingly like the 1/S division that
killed six constructions. It is not, and the reason is 11.35's lesson: this divides by P's
slope (~0.43), not a single detector's (blocking 0.103), because the composite is built FIRST
and the division happens SECOND. Both routes were computed and agree to 1%:
    ln(1+anchor)/dPdGen  2.297        1/|dP/dlogbpp|  2.314
They are the same quantity, so their agreement is a check that neither leg is misread, not
independent evidence.

THE ARGUMENT THAT DECIDES IT IS INTERNAL CONSISTENCY, NOT SIZE. The shrinkage implies a film
at the 99th percentile of |P| sits about 2 generations from a typical file. At 33.1% bitrate
per generation that is 1.77x bitrate, hence sqrt(1.77) = 1.33x in score, about +-33%. The
shipped lambda produced +-18%, i.e. ONE generation's worth. LAMBDA AND THE SHRINKAGE WERE
DISAGREEING WITH EACH OTHER ABOUT THE SAME LIBRARY. The direct route makes them consistent.

WHY THIS DOES NOT REOPEN 11.37. That section's argument was that P's spread implies absurd
generation counts unless the shrinkage is small. THE IMPLIED GENERATION COUNT DEPENDS ON THE
SHRINKAGE AND dP/dgen, NOT ON LAMBDA — lambda scales the OUTPUT, not the implied provenance.
So the plausibility check is unchanged by this correction and still passes at about 2
generations for the extremes. That is precisely why this correction is admissible where
adopting the raw anchor with the old shrinkage was not.

AND IT STILL PASSES THE EXTERNAL BOUND, checked before adoption as always:
    5-95 band  +-15.3%   PASS (inside +-25%)
    worst film +-29.4%   PASS (inside +-41%)
    library median BPP+ 66 -> 66, 35 of 1000 units cross the 100 line
The gap is now a VALIDATION quantity rather than a calibration input: "the WEB-vs-Bluray
difference is 1.7-2.0 generations" is a statement that can be checked for plausibility, and it
is plausible — a streaming delivery being one generation from a disc plus preprocessing plus a
different encoder should land somewhat above one.

11.40 FULL COVERAGE: SEVEN CHECKS PASS, ONE FAILS, AND THE CAUSE IS A DETECTOR FLOOR
(2026-08-27)  [ONE PRE-REGISTERED STRUCTURAL CHECK FAILED — fix in progress, do not quote the
rule as validated until it is re-run]
The 266 excluded Bluray movies (11.31) were measured, bringing coverage to 1048 — the whole
library. scripts/coverage-recheck.mjs fixed the n=782 baseline AND THE TOLERANCES BEFORE the
run, splitting them deliberately into structural claims that must not move and population
quantities that may.

STRUCTURAL — seven pass, one fails:
    PASS  PC1 sign pattern still 4/4
    PASS  PC1 direction vs physics direction   corr 0.957  (baseline 0.951)
    PASS  PC1 explains 36.1%                   (baseline 37.7%, noise floor 25%)
    PASS  split-half median r 0.272            (baseline 0.289, SE 0.036)
    PASS  generation ordering holds within h264
    PASS  720p negative control still silent   z -0.98  (baseline -0.71)
    PASS  direction still ranks 1 of 16 sign flips
    FAIL  random directions reaching our gap: 537 of 20000  (baseline 9; threshold was 400)

POPULATION — all moved, as anticipated, because the sample grew 34% non-randomly:
    WEB-vs-Bluray gap    0.271 -> 0.209   (t 2.58 -> 2.01)
    provenance fraction  0.0115 -> 0.0056   (-51%)
    shrinkage floor      0.107 -> 0.075
    adjustment 5-95 +-13.5%, worst +-26.1%   EXTERNAL BOUND STILL PASSES

THE CAUSE, DIAGNOSED RATHER THAN EXPLAINED AWAY. P uses log(cambi), and cambi is bounded below
with a noise floor. Near that floor the log is dominated by instrument noise:
    lowest 25% of cambi readings   med 0.120   mean |z_cambi| 1.196
    middle 50%                     med 1.024   mean |z_cambi| 0.494
    library-wide: 7.1% of units read cambi < 0.05, 11.3% < 0.1
The 266 added units are HIGH-bitrate Blurays and 20.1% of them sit below 0.05, against 0.3% in
the low-bitrate tercile. Their sd of z_cambi is 1.288 against 0.732. So the backfill injected a
large dose of pure log-of-near-zero noise, which inflated P's total variance (halving the
provenance fraction, since that fraction has total variance in its denominator), diluted the
gap, and lowered the bar that random directions had to clear.

THIS IS THE SAME MECHANISM AS 11.35, ARRIVING FROM A THIRD DIRECTION. There it made banding's
slope-vs-level correlation uninterpretable; here it degrades P itself. A detector that is
bounded below and read in logs will do this every time.

THE FIX IS A SOFT OFFSET, log(L + c), NOT A FLOOR OR A CLIP. It is smooth everywhere and has no
kink, which matters because 11.6 removed two hidden cut-offs and a hinge is one. c must be
MEASURED or it is exactly the arbitrary constant this project bans, so scripts/cambi-floor.js is
measuring it directly: read cambi at many independent positions in the same file and find the
level at which the spread of repeat readings equals the level itself. Below that the number
describes the sampler, not the film.

WHAT THE FAILURE DOES AND DOES NOT MEAN. It does NOT refute the factor: split-half, PC1 signs,
the codec control and the negative control all survived, and the direction still ranks first of
sixteen sign flips. 537 of 20000 is still p = 0.027. What it means is that the EVIDENCE FOR THE
DIRECTION IS MATERIALLY WEAKER AT FULL COVERAGE than it was on the truncated sample, and that
weakening is explained by a measurement artifact that has a principled fix.

DELIBERATE DEVIATION FROM THE PRE-REGISTRATION, RECORDED AS SUCH. The registered response was
"take the provenance rule off the Blend page's default until it is understood". That has NOT
been done, because the only alternative default is the legacy confidence-weighted rule, which
this same document records as producing m = 1e-58 and as containing two cut-offs. Switching to
a rule already known to be worse would mislead more, not less. Instead the page now carries a
prominent warning naming the failed check and its cause. THE SUBSTANCE OF THE PRE-REGISTRATION
— not presenting an unvalidated rule as validated — is preserved; its letter is not. Anyone who
disagrees with that call should change the default, and the reasoning is here to argue with.

11.41 THE FLOOR DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG, AND THE TRUTH IS BIGGER: CAMBI IS SCENE-SAMPLING
NOISE-LIMITED (2026-08-27)  [SOLID, and it explains the small effect sizes]
11.40 blamed the failed structural check on a DETECTOR FLOOR and proposed a soft offset
log(L + c). scripts/cambi-floor.js measured c directly. IT REFUTED THE PREMISE.

THE MEASUREMENT: 14 films spanning the cambi range, 8 independent 2-second reads each.
    level band   n   med level   med sd   med sd/level
    low          4      0.108     0.120        1.65
    mid          4      0.715     0.726        1.02
    high         6      2.380     2.452        0.92
sd/level is about 1 AT EVERY LEVEL. There is no crossover, so there is no floor, and the
c = 0.4711 the script printed is a computational artifact of assuming a monotone crossing that
does not exist. IT WAS NOT APPLIED.

AND log IS THE RIGHT TRANSFORM AFTER ALL. Constant sd/level means MULTIPLICATIVE noise, whose
variance-stabilising transform is exactly log. Measured per single read:
    sd(log cambi)   low 2.00   mid 2.06   high 1.68     -> roughly constant, as multiplicative
                                                            noise requires
So the transform was never the problem.

THE REAL PROBLEM IS THE SIZE OF THAT NOISE, and it is large enough to reframe the whole project:
    sd(log cambi), single 2-second read        1.68
    SE of a 4-clip mean (the backfill recipe)  0.84
    library residual sd for cambi              ~1.3
    => ABOUT 42% OF CAMBI'S RESIDUAL VARIANCE IS PURE SAMPLING NOISE
It is not instrument error. It is SCENE HETEROGENEITY: Marty Supreme's eight reads run 0.005,
0.254, 3.039, 3.375, 3.679, 3.833, 6.796, 8.5 — a 1700x range inside one film. Banding lives in
a handful of smooth-gradient scenes, and four 2-second clips estimate a film's mean badly.

WHAT THIS EXPLAINS, and it is a lot:
  - why the split-half reliability is only ~0.27 rather than something respectable
  - why the provenance fraction is small, hence why the shrinkage must be heavy
  - why every effect in this project has been modest. THE SHRINKAGE IS NOT PESSIMISM, IT IS THE
    CORRECT RESPONSE TO A NOISY MEASUREMENT, and the framework has been carrying that noise
    since the first backfill.

WHAT IT DOES NOT EXPLAIN: the low-cambi units still showed inflated |z| (1.196 vs 0.494) even
though sd(log) is constant. That is a SECOND, separate effect — the residual surface fits the
bulk better than the bpp extremes, exactly as 11.31 warned when the missing units turned out to
be the high-bitrate tail. Two mechanisms, not one.

THE FIX IS MORE SAMPLES, AND THE NUMBER IS COMPUTABLE. Noise SE scales as 1.68/sqrt(n):
    n = 4  (current)   SE 0.84   = 65% of the residual spread
    n = 16             SE 0.42   = 32%
    n = 26             SE 0.33   = 25%
Sixteen clips would roughly halve the noise VARIANCE, which should roughly double the provenance
fraction and therefore the shrinkage, and grow the adjustment accordingly. On 1048 units that is
a long measurement — the 286-unit backfill at 4 clips took about three hours — so it is an
overnight job, not an afternoon one. IT IS ALSO THE SINGLE HIGHEST-VALUE MEASUREMENT REMAINING,
because unlike every other lever it makes the existing signal bigger rather than reinterpreting it.

DO NOT ADJUST THE 11.40 RECHECK TOLERANCES. The failed permutation check stands as failed. What
changes is the explanation: not a floor, but a measurement that has always been noisier than the
analysis acknowledged.

FIRST ATTEMPT AT THE FIX WAS KILLED AFTER 4 UNITS, for two reasons both worth recording:
  1. TOO SLOW. Re-running artifact-backfill.js at 12 clips took 12.5 min/unit, not the ~2 min
     estimated, because --stratify interleaves by source label and therefore started on Bluray
     REMUXES, whose decode cost scales with source bitrate. 300 units would have been 60+ hours.
  2. IT COULD NOT HAVE ANSWERED THE QUESTION. artifact-backfill.js averages its clips and
     DISCARDS the per-clip values, so a 12-clip run yields one number per unit — a single noisy
     contrast against the 4-clip run, not a payoff curve. THE DESIGN WAS WRONG, not just slow.

REPLACED BY A WITHIN-UNIT DESIGN, which is cheaper AND strictly more informative
(scripts/clip-reliability.js): read every unit at 12 positions and KEEP EVERY CLIP. Two disjoint
k-clip means from the same unit, correlated across units, ARE the reliability of a k-clip
measurement — measured rather than modelled. Sweeping k traces the whole curve from one run, and
Spearman-Brown projects it to any clip count without measuring that count. Running at 1.9
min/unit, so 60 units in about two hours instead of sixty.

THE FALSIFICATION IS PRE-REGISTERED: if reliability is already flat by k=4, extra clips buy
nothing, the residual spread is NOT sampling-noise-dominated, and this section's explanation for
the small effect sizes is WRONG and the real cause must be found. That outcome would be more
useful than confirmation and must be reported as loudly.

--- INTERIM, 31 of 60 units, 2026-08-27. Shape is clear; wait for 60 before quoting numbers. ---
MEASURED RELIABILITY OF A k-CLIP MEAN (two disjoint k-clip means, correlated in logs):
      k    cambi    block     blur    grain
      1    0.640    0.659    0.209    0.338
      4    0.845    0.902    0.471    0.629
      6    0.865    0.935    0.574    0.736
    projected to 12    0.928    0.967    0.729    0.848
    projected to 26    0.965    0.984    0.854    0.924

SAMPLING NOISE IS CONFIRMED AS A REAL LIMIT, BUT THIS SECTION BLAMED THE WRONG DETECTOR.
cambi (0.81 at the library's 4 clips) and block (0.91) are the WELL-measured pair. The badly
measured ones are BLUR at 0.473 — more than half noise — and GRAIN at 0.650. The 42% figure
above came from cambi-floor.js's median sd(log) of 1.68, which was inflated by films reading
near zero (The General at 0.0001 has sd(log) 3.96) and by a sample of only 14. The direct
reliability measurement supersedes it.

AND THE PAYOFF IS MODEST WHERE IT MATTERS MOST. P is dominated by cambi and block, and both are
already on the flat part of the curve at k=4:
    cambi  provenance fraction would scale x1.14 (12 clips) / x1.19 (26)
    block                                  x1.07          / x1.09
    blur                                   x1.54          / x1.80
    grain                                  x1.30          / x1.42
So a full 26-clip re-measure of 1048 units — roughly 46 hours — would grow the adjustment by
something like 15%, not double it. THAT IS PROBABLY NOT WORTH THE HOURS, and saying so is the
point of having measured the curve before committing to them.

THE MORE INTERESTING CONSEQUENCE IS FOR BLUR. 11.20 and the handoff record blur as unusable in
the field because it correlates with nothing observable — bpp -0.162, year 0.139, resolution
0.090. AT RELIABILITY 0.473 THAT IS WHAT AN INFORMATIVE DETECTOR MEASURED BADLY WOULD ALSO LOOK
LIKE. A correlation is attenuated by sqrt(reliability), so blur's observed correlations are
running at about 69% of their true size. This does not rescue blur on its own, but it moves the
verdict from "carries nothing" to "cannot be read well enough at 4 clips to tell", which is a
different claim and a testable one.

11.42 THE RELIABILITY CURVE, AND WHY WEIGHTING BY IT IS NOT ADOPTED (2026-08-27)
[FINAL at 60 units. An interim version of this section written at 31 units was WRONG about
grain and its conclusion has been reversed — do not cite the interim numbers.]

MEASURED RELIABILITY OF A k-CLIP MEAN. Two disjoint k-clip means of the same unit, correlated
across units in logs, median over 60 random splits:
      k     cambi    block     blur    grain
      1     0.528    0.595    0.124    0.766
      4     0.798    0.849    0.404    0.937
      6     0.825    0.878    0.550    0.956
    projected to 12   0.904   0.935   0.709   0.977
    projected to 26   0.953   0.969   0.841   0.989

THE RANKING IS NOT WHAT 11.41 ASSUMED. GRAIN IS THE MOST RELIABLE DETECTOR (0.935 at the
library's four clips) and its SINGLE-clip reliability is already 0.766. Blur is the poor one at
0.449. That ordering is physically sensible and worth keeping in mind: grain is a global texture
property applied roughly uniformly across a film, so any two seconds of it look like any other,
whereas banding only exists where a scene has smooth gradients and blocking only where the
encoder ran short of bits. THE DETECTORS DIFFER IN HOW LOCAL THEIR ARTIFACT IS, and that is what
sets their sampling reliability.

*** THE INTERIM ESTIMATE AT 31 UNITS PUT GRAIN AT 0.629 AND WAS BADLY WRONG. A reliability
estimated from 31 units moved by 0.31 when the sample doubled. Do not act on one from a partial
run — this document did, in a section that has now been rewritten. ***

WHAT MORE CLIPS WOULD BUY, per detector, as a multiplier on the provenance fraction:
    cambi  x1.19 at 12 clips / x1.26 at 26        blur   x1.58 / x1.87
    block  x1.13            / x1.17               grain  x1.05 / x1.06
So a 12-clip re-measure of 1048 units — about 33 hours at the measured 1.9 min/unit — would grow
the shrinkage by roughly 10% and the adjustment with it. THAT IS A REAL BUT SMALL RETURN, and
knowing it is small is exactly what the curve was measured for.

RELIABILITY WEIGHTING: TESTED, NOT ADOPTED. Weighting each detector by its measured reliability
is the standard optimal combination for extracting a common factor, and it is permitted under
Brennan's rule because the weights are measured rather than chosen. Measured on 1048 units:
    weighting            split-half    gap      t   provFrac  720p control
    equal (shipped)         0.377     0.209   2.01   0.0056     -1.82
    by reliability          0.409     0.198   1.90   0.0062     -0.98
    by reliability^2        0.418     0.184   1.76   0.0070     -0.47
    drop blur entirely      0.432     0.188   1.85   0.0062     +0.60
IT PULLS TWO WAYS. Split-half and the provenance fraction rise, the negative control improves
markedly — but THE GAP AND ITS t FALL, and the gap is the quantity the calibration runs through.
provFrac and the gap can move oppositely because provFrac spans all four source labels while the
gap is WEB-vs-Bluray specifically. Given that 11.40's failed check was ALREADY about the gap
weakening, trading gap for specificity is the wrong trade. EQUAL WEIGHTING STAYS.

BLUR IS NOISY BUT INFORMATIVE, and the control column settles it. Dropping blur gives the
HIGHEST split-half of all — it removes the noisiest detector — while the GAP FALLS to 0.188.
That is the self-agreement trap made visible: a composite of the most mutually correlated
indicators looks more reliable while carrying less independent information. Blur was written off
for field use in 11.20 because it correlates with nothing observable; at reliability 0.449 those
correlations are attenuated to about 67% of their true size, and more decisively, REMOVING IT
COSTS THE GAP. It carries provenance the other three do not. IT STAYS IN THE COMPOSITE.

11.43 TWO WAYS TO TIDY THE RESIDUALS, BOTH REJECTED — AND THE SECOND ONE IMPROVED FOUR
STATISTICS WHILE BREAKING THE FIFTH (2026-08-27)  [both TESTED and REVERTED]
11.41 left a loose end the noise story did not explain: |z_cambi| is 1.196 in the bottom cambi
quartile against 0.494 in the middle, even though sd(log cambi) is level-independent. Two
candidate causes were tested. Neither survived, and the second failure is the instructive one.

FIRST: IS THE SURFACE TOO INFLEXIBLE? P's residuals come from a surface quadratic in log bpp and
log complexity. A low-order polynomial fits the bulk well and the tails badly, and 11.31 warned
that the 266 missing units were the HIGH-BITRATE END.
    surface        terms   hetero   split-h    gap      t    provFrac
    quadratic          8    0.456    0.377    0.209   2.01    0.0056
    cubic             12    0.455    0.375    0.181   1.73    0.0043
    quartic           15    0.456    0.374    0.180   1.71    0.0042
THE HETEROSCEDASTICITY DOES NOT MOVE AT ALL while the gap falls 13%. Extra flexibility in the
MEAN function cannot fix a VARIANCE problem; it can only absorb signal. Surface misfit is NOT
the second mechanism. Keep the quadratic.

AND PART OF THE PATTERN IS MERELY MECHANICAL. |z| was being compared against the OBSERVED level,
but the residual IS a component of the observed value, so some of that correlation is arithmetic.
Comparing against the FITTED value separates them:
    detector   |z| vs observed   |z| vs FITTED   reading
    cambi          -0.451            -0.312      real
    block          +0.795            +0.209      mostly mechanical
    blur           +0.092            +0.030      none
    grain          +0.488            +0.753      strongly real
Block's apparent heteroscedasticity is largely an artifact of the comparison. Cambi's and
grain's are real. ALWAYS COMPARE A RESIDUAL AGAINST THE PREDICTION, NEVER AGAINST THE OBSERVED
VALUE IT IS PART OF.

SECOND: STANDARDISE BY A LOCAL SD. Since the variance genuinely varies, model log|residual| on
the fitted value (quadratic, smooth, no kink) and divide each residual by its own predicted sd,
so a z of 1 means the same thing everywhere — which is exactly what summing four of them into P
already assumes. IT WORKED, ON EVERYTHING BEING LOOKED AT:
    standardisation        hetero   split-h    gap      t     provFrac
    global sd (shipped)     0.326    0.377    0.209   2.01     0.0056
    local sd                0.024    0.401    0.214   2.15     0.0058
Heteroscedasticity essentially eliminated, split-half up 6%, AND THE GAP AND ITS t BOTH ROSE —
unlike every other change tried today, which bought tidiness by eating the gap.

*** AND IT MADE THE PRE-REGISTERED RECHECK WORSE, WITH A NEW SECOND FAILURE. ***
    random directions reaching our gap   537 -> 1076 of 20000
    pre-registered direction's rank      1 -> 2 of the 16 sign flips     <- NEW FAILURE
EQUALISING THE VARIANCE MAKES THE FOUR DETECTORS MORE INTERCHANGEABLE. More arbitrary directions
then reach the same gap, and a DIFFERENT sign combination overtook the physics one. The change
improved four statistics while destroying the only thing that distinguishes the pre-registered
direction from an arbitrary combination — which is the whole evidential basis of 11.28.

REVERTED. Global sd stays, and the recheck is back to its single failure at 537/20000, rank 1.

THE LESSON, AND IT COST A ROUND TRIP: THE CHANGE WAS TESTED AGAINST THE NUMBERS BEING LOOKED AT
RATHER THAN AGAINST THE CHECK THAT WAS ACTUALLY FAILING. variance-standardise.mjs printed ADOPT
because it only measured heteroscedasticity and the gap. A local improvement that is not checked
against the failing test is not evidence of anything. RUN THE ARBITER, NOT A PROXY FOR IT.

11.44 WHY THE PERMUTATION CHECK WEAKENED — IT IS A PROPERTY OF THE CONTRAST, NOT OF THE
DIRECTION (2026-08-27)  [DIAGNOSED. Do NOT refit the direction on this.]
11.40's single surviving failure is the permutation test: 537 of 20000 random directions now
reach our WEB-vs-Bluray gap, against 9 at n=782. Two explanations were tested and one holds.

FIRST, THE COMPARISON DONE PROPERLY. The statistic is "how many random directions reach OUR
gap", so a shrinking gap lowers the bar. Both shrank, but not equally:
                our gap   random p95   random max   margin over p95
    n=782        0.2711      0.241        0.273        1.125x
    n=1048       0.2088      0.198        0.231        1.054x
So it is not merely that the bar dropped — our direction lost ground RELATIVE to arbitrary ones.

AND THE DIAGNOSTIC LINE SAYS EXACTLY WHERE. The best direction found by unconstrained search:
    n=782    0.67 cambi  0.52 block  0.38 blur  -0.37 grain    cos with ours 0.969
    n=1048   0.83 cambi  0.37 block  0.43 blur  -0.03 grain    cos with ours 0.825
GRAIN'S COMPONENT COLLAPSED FROM -0.37 TO -0.03. The physics direction gives grain a full
quarter of the composite; at full coverage the data says this contrast barely uses it.

A PLAUSIBLE STORY, TESTED AND REFUTED: that grain's residual carries FILM STOCK rather than
provenance — old films are grainy, so grain would track release year, which is content the
surface failed to remove. Measured corr(z, year): cambi 0.146, block 0.045, blur 0.044, GRAIN
-0.032. All four are clean of year and grain is the cleanest of them. The story is wrong.

WHAT IS ACTUALLY HAPPENING, and it needs no refitting to see:
    group    mean z_cambi   mean z_grain
    WEB          +0.256        -0.169
    Bluray       -0.026        -0.100
    contributes  +0.282        +0.069
Grain moves in the RIGHT direction — WEB has less retained grain, as a re-encode should — but it
contributes about a quarter of what cambi does, while carrying equal weight and therefore equal
noise.

AND 11.38's MEASURED AXIS DIRECTIONS ALREADY PREDICTED THIS:
    generations axis    cambi 0.238  block 0.721  blur 0.640  grain -0.116
    preprocessing axis  cambi 0.749  block 0.258  blur 0.338  grain -0.509
THE GENERATIONS AXIS ITSELF HAS ONLY A SMALL GRAIN COMPONENT. Grain is the PREPROCESSING
detector. WEB-vs-Bluray is primarily a GENERATIONS contrast — a streaming delivery is one or two
encodes from a disc, not necessarily a denoised one — so grain SHOULD contribute little to this
particular gap while remaining the right detector for a different axis.

THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE TEST, STATED CAREFULLY. The permutation test measures how special the
direction is AT SEPARATING ONE CONTRAST. A direction that spends a quarter of itself on a
detector aligned with a DIFFERENT provenance axis will look less special on that contrast than a
direction fitted to it. That is a limitation of using a single observable contrast as the
yardstick, not evidence that the equal-weight physics direction is wrong.

*** AND THIS IS EXACTLY THE POINT AT WHICH IT WOULD BE EASY, AND WRONG, TO DOWN-WEIGHT GRAIN. ***
The direction is pre-registered from what a re-encode physically does. Refitting it because the
data prefers something else on the same contrast the test uses is the circularity 11.28 exists
to prevent — the permutation result would then be guaranteed and would mean nothing. THE
DIRECTION STAYS. The failure stands as recorded, with its cause now understood.

WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT PROPERLY: a second, independent contrast that is preprocessing-dominated
rather than generations-dominated. The library has no clean one — which is itself worth stating,
because it means the covering-set framework currently has FOUR validated axes but only ONE
observable field contrast to test them against.

11.45 P IS MORE SENSITIVE TO PREPROCESSING THAN TO GENERATIONS — 11.44 CONFIRMED, AND A NEW
CAVEAT ON THE CALIBRATION (2026-08-27)  [SOLID]
11.44 argued that the permutation check weakened because the WEB-vs-Bluray contrast is
generations-dominated, so grain — the preprocessing detector — contributes little to it. That
argument has an obvious failure mode: maybe the equal-weight composite is simply BLIND to
preprocessing, which would be a defect in the construction rather than in the test. The
controlled denoise experiment settles it, using data already on disk.

    dP per denoise step (none/light/heavy hqdn3d, bits and content constant, 10 films)
        median 0.3353 P-sd      sign agreement 9/10
    dP per GENERATION (crossed ladder)
        median 0.1385 P-sd
    ratio 2.42x

P IS 2.4 TIMES MORE SENSITIVE TO A DENOISE STEP THAN TO A GENERATION. The composite is not blind
to preprocessing; it is unusually good at it. So 11.44 stands: the detectors and the direction
are sound, and it is THE LIBRARY'S ONE OBSERVABLE CONTRAST that lacks preprocessing variation.

AND IT RECONCILES WITH THE YIFY NULL (11.38). If P were weakly sensitive to preprocessing, YIFY
reading zero would be ambiguous. It is strongly sensitive, so YIFY reading zero means YIFY does
not heavily preprocess these files — it encodes at low bitrate with a competent x264, and BPP+
already scores that. Two independent results now agree on the same explanation.

*** A NEW CAVEAT ON THE CALIBRATION, AND IT WAS NOT VISIBLE BEFORE THIS MEASUREMENT. ***
The shipped rule converts P into bitrate through the GENERATION rate:
    lambda = ln(1 + 33.1%) / dPdGen
That assumes a film's P is generation-like. For a film whose P is driven by PREPROCESSING, the
same P corresponds to 2.42x fewer "steps", and the bitrate cost of a denoise step HAS NEVER BEEN
MEASURED — the crossed ladder varied generations only. So:
    THE CALIBRATION IS GENERATION-SPECIFIC, and a preprocessing-dominated film's adjustment is
    scaled by a rate that was measured on a different axis.
The DIRECTION of that error is unknown, because it depends on whether a denoise step costs more
or less bitrate-equivalent than a generation, and nothing here measures that. Do not guess it.

WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT: a crossed DENOISE x BITRATE ladder, exactly analogous to anchor-crossed.js —
leg A sweeps bitrate at no denoising, leg B sweeps denoise level at one bitrate, both on the same
clips. That yields the bitrate cost of a denoise step with no transfer assumption, and the
calibration could then be axis-aware instead of assuming everything is generations.

11.46 A PREDICTION ABOUT THE DENOISE ANCHOR, REGISTERED BEFORE IT LANDS (2026-08-27 ~17:30)
[PRE-REGISTRATION — do not edit after the run reports; append the outcome below it]
scripts/denoise-crossed.js is measuring what a denoise step costs in bitrate. This project has
explained numbers after seeing them often enough that the prediction goes in first.

THE ARITHMETIC THAT MAKES IT SHARP. The shipped rule uses ONE lambda, derived from the
generation axis:
    lambda = ln(1 + 33.1%) / dPdGen = ln(1.331) / 0.1385 = 2.065 per P-sd
For that single lambda to also be correct on the preprocessing axis, a denoise step would have
to satisfy ln(1 + X) = 2.065 * 0.3353 = 0.6925, i.e.
    *** X = 99.9%. A DENOISE STEP WOULD HAVE TO COST ROUGHLY 2x BITRATE. ***
That is not a modelling choice; it is what the current rule already implicitly asserts, and
until 11.45 measured dP per denoise step there was no way to see it.

THE PREDICTION: BELOW 99%, probably well below. Reasoning rather than data — published practice
treats denoising as a bitrate-SAVING technique, worth 10-30% at equal quality, which puts its
quality COST in the same order rather than at 2x. hqdn3d=6:4:9:6 is far more aggressive than
production denoising, so the measured figure should exceed those published numbers, but 2x would
be extraordinary.

WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS:
    lands near 99%      the single-lambda rule is accidentally right on both axes. Adopt nothing,
                        record the coincidence, and stop worrying about axis-awareness.
    lands 30-70%        lambda OVERSTATES adjustments for preprocessing-dominated films by the
                        ratio. The calibration should become axis-aware — but note there is
                        currently NO way to tell which axis a given library film sits on, so the
                        practical fix is a caveat, not a formula change.
    lands below 20%     the over-statement is large and preprocessing-dominated films are being
                        badly mis-scaled. That would matter enough to justify finding a per-film
                        axis discriminator, which is task #73's question arriving from a new
                        direction.
    leg B fails its direction check on most films
                        see the warning below — the experiment may be badly designed for this axis.

A DESIGN RISK SPOTTED WHILE THE RUN WAS IN FLIGHT, recorded now so it is not rationalised later.
Leg B applies denoising at the film's OWN base bitrate, which for a starved film is very low. On
the film visible in the log at 483 kbps, none -> heavy moved grain only 1.0223 -> 1.0034, under
2%, against 1.168 -> 0.975 (16%) in the controlled denoise-test experiment. THE REASON IS
PHYSICAL: a bitrate-starved encoder has already destroyed the grain, so the denoiser has little
left to remove. If leg B is weak on low-bitrate films, their ratios will be noisy and the
ratio-of-median-slopes may be dominated by the high-bitrate films in the set. Check the per-film
leg-B slopes before quoting the median.

11.47 THE DENOISE ANCHOR IS NOT A CONSTANT, AND THE PREDICTION IN 11.46 HOLDS ONLY WEAKLY
(2026-08-27)  [MEASURED. Do not quote a denoise anchor; keep the generation-based lambda.]
scripts/denoise-crossed.js ran the same crossed design that settled the generation anchor: leg A
four bitrates with no denoising, leg B three denoise levels at one bitrate, same clips, so no
transfer assumption operates. 8 of 8 films passed both direction checks.

    RATIO OF MEDIAN SLOPES 51.3%      median of per-film ratios 81.2%
    per-film range 9.8% .. 845.8%     p10 9.8%  p90 536.6%

11.46 PREDICTED "well below 99%" AND 51.3% IS BELOW IT — but the prediction should not be
credited with much. The two statistics DISAGREE by 30 points where the generation run's agreed to
0.9, and the per-film spread is two orders of magnitude. This is not a measurement of a constant.

WHY, AND IT IS THE REAL FINDING. The design risk flagged in 11.46 while the run was in flight is
confirmed, and larger than expected:
    film                      base kbps   dP/dstep
    Just Go with It                 483     0.0526
    Mr. Robot S01                  1015     0.1562
    Hugo                           1265     0.1979
    Silicon Valley S01             1925     0.1450
    The Creator                    2325     0.1528
    Arrested Development S01       4811     0.3964
    The Birds                      5387     0.3711
    12 Angry Men                  18614     0.5251
    corr(log base kbps, dP/dstep) = 0.946
A TEN-FOLD RANGE, ALMOST PERFECTLY ORDERED BY BITRATE. "A denoise step" is not a fixed unit: a
starved encoder has already destroyed the grain, so the denoiser has nothing left to remove. It
also explains why 11.45 measured dP/dstep = 0.335 on a different film set and this run measured
0.177 — the two sets differ in bitrate, not in physics.

*** AND THAT KILLS THE COMPARISON 11.45 SET UP. *** lambda_denoise depends entirely on which
dP/dstep is paired with the 51.3%: this run's 0.177 gives 2.34 (13% above lambda_gen = 2.065),
11.45's 0.335 gives 1.24 (40% below). The apparent agreement was an artifact of the pairing.

A CORRECTION TO A CLAIM MADE AN HOUR EARLIER IN THIS SAME INVESTIGATION. It was reported that
the generation anchor's RATIO is bitrate-independent (corr 0.024) while its legs are not, and
that this showed the crossed design cancelling the dependence. THAT WAS OVER-READ. Dropping The
Creator — whose leg-A slope of -0.068 makes it fragile, and which BOTH runs flag — the
correlations rise:
                              all 8    without The Creator
    generation anchor          0.024          0.273
    denoise anchor             0.209          0.406
AT n=8 THE SE OF A CORRELATION IS ABOUT 0.45. Every one of those numbers is within noise of zero
and of every other. The 0.024 was a single outlier masking whatever the real value is, not
evidence of cancellation. Only the 0.946 on dP/dstep is large enough to be real at this n.

WHAT STANDS, AND WHAT DOES NOT:
    SOLID     dP/dstep is strongly bitrate-dependent, so a denoise step is not a calibration unit
    NOT SHOWN that the anchor ratio cancels bitrate on either axis — unmeasurable at n=8
    NOT SHOWN that lambda differs between the axes, or that it does not
CONSEQUENCE: KEEP THE GENERATION-BASED LAMBDA. It rests on a run whose two statistics converged
(33.1% vs 34.0%) and whose per-film spread was an order of magnitude tighter. The
axis-awareness question raised in 11.45 CANNOT BE SETTLED AT THIS SAMPLE SIZE and should be
recorded as open rather than answered. Settling it needs many more films, and given that the
whole adjustment is +-13.5% at 5-95, it is not obviously worth them.

11.48 THE DIRECTION IS SPECIAL AT SPANNING PROVENANCE — TESTED WHERE PROVENANCE IS KNOWN
(2026-08-27)  [SOLID. Does NOT rescue 11.40; that failure is about the field yardstick.]
11.40's surviving failure is the FIELD permutation test, and #82 recorded why it is hard to read:
four validated axes, only ONE observable field contrast to test them against. The controlled
experiments have no such limit — provenance there is known and varied on purpose with bits and
content held constant — so the same permutation logic can run on them.

THE TAUTOLOGY THAT HAD TO BE AVOIDED. On a SINGLE axis the best direction is trivially that
axis's own, and the pre-registered signature was chosen to match what a re-encode does, so
"the physics direction scores well on generations" would be near-circular. The non-tautological
question is whether ONE direction works on ALL THREE AT ONCE — which is the actual covering-set
claim. So the statistic is the WORST of the three axes, never the average: a direction that
misses any single axis scores badly, where averaging would let one strong axis hide a failure.

    PRE-REGISTERED DIRECTION, t of the projected per-film change
        generations    6.84
        preprocessing  2.19
        encoder        3.41
        WORST AXIS     2.19        <- the statistic
    NULL, 20000 random unit directions, same axes and films
        p50 -0.67   p95 1.59   max 2.63
        matching or beating ours: 145 of 20000 = 0.73%

THE DIRECTION BEATS 99.3% OF ARBITRARY DIRECTIONS ON THE WORST-AXIS CRITERION. Aligning with one
axis is easy for a random direction; aligning with three at once is not, and that is what the
covering-set framing asserts.

THREE THINGS NOT TO OVERSTATE:
  - IT DOES NOT RESCUE 11.40. That failure is about how special the direction looks on the one
    FIELD contrast available, and it stands exactly as recorded. This is different evidence for a
    different claim, not a re-run of the same test with a friendlier yardstick.
  - OURS IS NOT OPTIMAL. The best random direction found (0.04 cambi, 0.42 block, 0.90 blur,
    -0.07 grain) scores 2.63 against our 2.19. Blur-dominated, which is striking given blur is the
    least reliable detector (0.449 at four clips, 11.42) — it is noisy but evidently spans the
    three axes well, consistent with 11.42's finding that removing blur COSTS the gap.
  - n IS SMALL: 10, 10 and 20 films.

A CONSISTENCY CHECK WORTH RECORDING. Preprocessing is our WEAKEST axis here (t = 2.19) even
though 11.45 measured P as 2.42x MORE sensitive per denoise step than per generation. There is no
contradiction: t is mean/sd ACROSS FILMS, so it rewards consistency, while dP measures SIZE. The
preprocessing response is larger but more variable between films — which is exactly what 11.47
then explained, since dP/dstep correlates with bitrate at 0.946. The two statistics answer
different questions and both are needed: t for comparing axes, dP for calibration.

11.49 THE GENERATION ANCHOR, REPLICATED ON 16 FILMS — AND THE GAP LANDS AT ONE GENERATION
(2026-08-27)  [SOLID. This is the calibration the shipped rule now uses.]
The 8-film crossed run (11.36) settled the anchor at 33.1% but rested on a thin sample with one
fragile film. Re-run at 16 films. THE STRIDE CHANGED, so only 2 of the 16 overlap with the
original 8 — this is close to an INDEPENDENT REPLICATION on 14 new films, not a refinement.

                        8-film run        16-film run
    ratio of medians       33.1%             35.6%
    median of ratios       34.0%             34.6%      (agrees)
    mean / sd         75.9% / 118.6      37.0% / 17.7   <- 6.7x tighter
    fragile films      1 (The Creator)         0
    direction checks        8/8               16/16
    p10 / p90          22.0% / 55.7%     15.2% / 53.6%

AND THE TWO SHARED FILMS REPRODUCE ALMOST EXACTLY: Mr. Robot 40.8% -> 40.9%, 12 Angry Men
55.7% -> 54.7%. Per-film reproducibility plus near-independent replication of the median is
about as much as this design can offer. THE ANCHOR IS 35.6%, AND IT IS THE FIRST NUMBER IN THIS
PROJECT TO SURVIVE A REPLICATION RATHER THAN A CONTROL.

*** AND THE GAP NOW LANDS AT ONE GENERATION. *** dP/dgen re-measured on 16 films is 0.2005
(8 films gave 0.1385), so the WEB-vs-Bluray gap of 0.209 P-sd is
    0.209 / 0.2005 = 1.04 GENERATIONS
The premise this entire contrast rests on — that a streaming delivery is about one generation
from a disc — was assumed at the start and has never been fitted. It is the ratio of two
independently measured quantities and it comes out at 1.04.
    HOW MUCH TO MAKE OF THAT: some, but not a great deal. The figure has ranged 1.04 to 2.21
    across successive measurements of dP/dgen (1.47 in 11.30, 2.0, then 1.68, now 1.04), because
    it inherits all the uncertainty in the denominator. What is fair to say is that every
    measurement has put it between one and two generations, which is the right order for
    "one generation plus preprocessing plus a different encoder", and the best-measured version
    sits at the bottom of that range.

EFFECT ON THE SHIPPED RULE. lambda = ln(1.356)/0.2005 = 1.533, down from 2.065, because dP/dgen
rose more than the anchor did:
    adjustment  p05 -9.1%   median 0.0%   p95 +9.9%   worst +-19.0%
    EXTERNAL BOUND: 5-95 inside +-25%, worst inside +-41%.  BOTH PASS.
    library median BPP+ 67 -> 67, and 22 of 1048 units cross the 100 line
The whole day's measurement has moved the adjustment from a guessed +-17.8% to a measured
+-9.9% at 5-95. SMALLER, AND EVERY CONSTANT IN IT NOW MEASURED.

LAB UPDATED: default anchor 36%, labelled MEASURED, 16 films. The slider still reaches 15% and
70% so the two superseded answers stay visible — 15% was the published-BD-rate guess the page
shipped with this morning, 70% was 11.30's transfer estimate — but neither is the default.

11.50 THE SHRINKAGE IS PROBABLY TOO CONSERVATIVE, AND IT SHOULD STAY THAT WAY (2026-08-27)
[ANALYSIS, no new data. Records the direction of the error, not a change.]
The 16-film dP/dgen of 0.2005 moves the shrinkage bracket, so it was recomputed:
    FLOOR    between-source-label variance          0.075   MEASURED
    CEILING  4 generations across +-3 sd = 4dP/3    0.267   ASSUMPTION (was 0.149 at the old dP)
    rejected reliability                            0.377

WHERE THE SHIPPED VALUE PUTS THE LIBRARY'S EXTREMES. p99 of |P| is 2.64 sd; shrunk by 0.075 that
is 0.198 P-sd, i.e. 0.99 GENERATIONS from a typical file. A library that spans Bluray remuxes at
one end and triple-re-encoded WEBRips at the other should have its 99th percentile MORE than one
generation from the middle. THE FLOOR IS ALMOST CERTAINLY CONSERVATIVE.
    to put the p99 film 2 generations out needs shrinkage 0.152
    to put it 3 generations out needs 0.228

*** AND IT STAYS AT 0.075 ANYWAY. *** The floor is a MEASUREMENT — the between-label variance of
P, computed from data. The ceiling is an ASSUMPTION about how many generations this library
spans. Moving the shipped value toward the ceiling would replace a measurement with an
assumption, which is the wrong direction of travel however conservative the measurement is.
11.37 chose the floor precisely because it errs toward saying too little, and 11.37's reasoning
has not changed.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR HOW THE OUTPUT SHOULD BE READ, and it is worth stating plainly to anyone
looking at the Blend page: THE ADJUSTMENT IS MORE LIKELY AN UNDER-ESTIMATE THAN AN OVER-ESTIMATE.
The +-9.9% at 5-95 is a lower bound on what the panel would say if the provenance share could be
measured with a finer marker than the source label.

AND THAT MARKER DOES NOT EXIST IN THIS LIBRARY. Release groups were the obvious candidate and
11.17 killed them: 65% of files were renamed by *arr and lost their tag, and the survivors skew
to small-file re-encoders. Without a finer marker the floor cannot be raised by measurement, and
raising it any other way is guessing.

A NOTE ON A COINCIDENCE, so nobody reads design into it: the shipped shrinkage putting the p99
film at 0.99 generations is NOT a designed property. It falls out of an independently measured
between-label variance meeting an independently measured dP/dgen. It looked like a sanity check
passing; it is arithmetic.

MORE CLIPS WOULD HELP ONLY MARGINALLY. The fraction has total variance in its denominator and
noise inflates that, so reducing noise raises it — but 11.42 measured the gain at x1.13-1.19 for
the two detectors that dominate P, which would take the shrinkage from 0.075 to about 0.082 and
the adjustment up by roughly 10%. FOR 33 HOURS OF BOX TIME. Still not worth it, and this is the
second independent route to that conclusion.

11.51 blockMean IS NOT LUMA-DEGENERATE — AND A CORRELATION IS NOT AN EFFECT SIZE (2026-08-27)
[SOLID. Closes the long-open luma degeneracy question, task #69.]
Blocking is the ENCODER-axis detector, the second most reliable of the four, and carries a
quarter of P. If its reading were partly a function of how BRIGHT a scene is rather than how
blocky it is, that confound would propagate into the encoder axis and into every adjustment.

THE TEST. Take a real clip, shift its LUMA without touching its structure, encode each shifted
version identically, measure. Edges, texture, motion and bitrate are constant by construction;
only brightness moves, and a brightness offset cannot change how blocky an image is.

A DESIGN FLAW CAUGHT ON THE FIRST OUTPUT ROW, worth recording because it would have produced
confident nonsense. Fixed offsets of +-0.25 in eq units are about +-64 luma levels, but these
films sit at base luma 38-86. Mr. Robot came out at LUMA 2.6 — crushed to black, its grain
reading collapsing from ~1.0 to 0.14. That is not a shifted image, it is a clipped one, and
clipping changes structure, which is exactly the premise the test rests on. Rewritten to scale
each film to its own headroom, min(base, 255-base), plus a guard that drops any rung still
landing near a limit. The guard earned its place immediately: Mr. Robot is the darkest film in
the set and its extreme rung still lands at luma 9.1, so it is excluded rather than regressed.

THE RESULT, and the first column is the misleading one:
    detector   corr(luma, log det)   slope per 50 luma   as % of residual sd
    cambi            0.591                0.0053                0.4%
    block            0.047               -0.0006                0.2%
    blur             0.666                0.0195               11.8%
    grain            0.649                0.0612               23.5%

*** THREE DETECTORS "TRACK LUMA" AT r ABOUT 0.6 AND TWO OF THEM MOVE BY UNDER HALF A PERCENT OF
THEIR OWN SPREAD. *** With five rungs and a tiny monotone trend, a correlation can sit near 0.7
while the effect is nothing. cambi at r = 0.591 is a high-precision measurement of a 0.4% effect.
ALWAYS CONVERT A CORRELATION INTO AN EFFECT SIZE AGAINST A SPREAD THAT MATTERS BEFORE CALLING IT
A CONFOUND. This is the same discipline that killed the 4.64x BD-rate, arriving from the
opposite direction: there a tight error bar hid an implausible magnitude, here a strong
correlation hides a negligible one.

BLOCKING IS CLEAN: r = 0.047, effect 0.2% of its residual sd. TASK #69 IS ANSWERED, NO.

GRAIN IS THE ONE WITH A REAL EFFECT, 23.5% of its residual sd per 50 luma levels, and films here
span roughly 85 luma. Grain carries weight -1 in P, so it was checked against the contrast the
calibration actually runs through:
    WEB minus Bluray mean luma   -5.2 +- 3.4   t = -1.54   (302 Bluray, 22 WEB-DL)
    contributes 0.006 P-sd to a gap of 0.209 P-sd  =  UNDER 3%
    corr(luma, P) -0.104    corr(luma, z_grain) 0.254    corr(luma, z_blur) 0.007
THE TWO GROUPS BARELY DIFFER IN BRIGHTNESS, so the grain-luma response adds a little NOISE to P
and essentially no BIAS to the gap. Blur is the odd one: an 11.8% controlled effect that does not
appear in the field at all (r = 0.007), which is what a real but small effect looks like once
everything else a library varies in is piled on top of it.

THE LIMIT ON THIS CHECK: only 330 of 1048 units carry a luma reading, and the WEB side of that is
22 units, so the -5.2 +- 3.4 is weak. Measuring luma library-wide is cheap — signalstats alone,
no libvmaf — and would both firm this up and allow luma into the residual surface, which would
clean grain's residual of a 23.5% contaminant.

11.52 DOES ARTIFACT VISIBILITY TRACK BPP+? — THE FIRST INDEPENDENT HANDLE ON WHERE 100 SITS
(2026-08-27)  [SOLID for banding. The blocking "anomaly" was mine and is withdrawn.]
Brennan asked whether the library median of ~67 is in the right place. That is the
HEADROOM_TARGET question and it has never had independent evidence. The four thresholds now
give one: if 100 really means "just enough", films well below it should show visible damage.

    BPP+ band      n   banding>T   blocking>T
    <50          155        25%         0%
    50-74        501        22%         2%
    75-99        228        17%         8%
    100-124      108        11%         8%
    >=125         56         2%         7%

BANDING FALLS CLEANLY AND MONOTONICALLY, 25% to 2%, and it keeps doing so INSIDE every
complexity stratum (smooth 42/23/11, mid 25/11/4, grainy 12/4/0). That is real validation of
BPP+ itself, from a detector with a published visibility threshold that owes nothing to the
score's construction.

WHAT IT SAYS ABOUT THE MEDIAN. At 50-74, where the library median lives, 77% of files show no
visible banding or blocking at all. If 100 meant "barely enough", most files at 67 should be
visibly short and they are not. THAT IS EVIDENCE THE SCALE IS PESSIMISTIC AND THE MEDIAN BELONGS
HIGHER — the first such evidence this project has produced.
    THE CAVEAT THAT MUST TRAVEL WITH IT: "no banding or blocking" is not "transparent". A file
    can be visibly softer than its source without tripping either, and our detail detector (blur)
    is unusable in the field (11.20). This is evidence, not proof.

*** AND A CORRECTION TO MY OWN READING OF THE SAME TABLE. *** Blocking appears to RISE with BPP+
(0% to 8%), which I reported as an anomaly and guessed was blockdetect reading grain or texture
as blocking on high-bitrate content. THE GUESS WAS WRONG:
    corr(block, cxEff) -0.063     corr(block, grain) -0.132
    above threshold: med cx 0.129, med grain 0.857
    below threshold: med cx 0.149, med grain 0.957
The blocky films are SMOOTHER and LESS grainy, not grainier — which is physically right, because
block edges show in flat areas and are MASKED by grain. That is 11.23's "grain blinds the
detectors" applied to blocking.

BUT THE REAL ANSWER IS THAT THE OBSERVATION WAS NEVER SUPPORTABLE. Only 42 of 1048 units clear
the blocking threshold, so the apparent trend is 41 films spread over five bands — counts of
0, 10, 18, 9, 4. Stratifying by complexity makes it worse, not better: smooth runs 3/4/11%, mid
runs 1/18/4%, grainy runs 1/9/0%, on cells of 26 to 128. THERE IS NO BLOCKING TREND HERE, ONLY
NOISE, AND I SHOULD HAVE CHECKED THE COUNTS BEFORE CALLING IT AN ANOMALY AND BEFORE OFFERING A
MECHANISM FOR IT. A percentage computed on single-digit counts deserves the same suspicion as a
correlation computed on five points (11.51).

WHAT WOULD MOVE THE MEDIAN QUESTION FORWARD: the banding gradient is the usable signal. Finding
the BPP+ level at which banding visibility falls to some agreed floor would give a data-driven
anchor for where 100 belongs, and unlike everything else in section 11 it needs no new
measurement — only a decision about what visibility rate counts as "enough".

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
12. HOW THE SCORE IS ACTED ON
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Audit tab is where BPP+ becomes decisions. Precision is the stated goal:
neither false positives (flagging good copies) nor false negatives (missing bad
ones).

12.1 OVER-SUPPLY (the Disk section)
    OVERSUPPLY_R = 2            flag only past 2x transparent
    OVERSUPPLY_MIN_GB = 2       below this it is not worth a human's attention
    overBytes = bytes * (1 - OVERSUPPLY_R / R)
    SEASON_DISAGREE_MAX = 0.25  seasons whose episodes disagree are ineligible
Rows are admitted on band OR over-supply and sorted by overBytes.

*** A CORRECTION WORTH KEEPING: over-supply is measured above the 2x FLOOR, not
above transparency. An early report described a Westworld swap as "cutting into
the picture" citing 0.24 GB of surplus; the real surplus above transparency was
8.0 GB (44%). BPP+ already IS a surplus measure — a candidate at BPP+ 100 lands
exactly on the transparency anchor, not below it. The genuine objection to that
swap was generation loss, not bit starvation. ***

12.2 UPGRADE ELIGIBILITY
    upgradeEligible = short || unprobed || reenc || bppPlus < UPGRADE_BPP_PLUS_MAX
Serve-time filtering in rankCands() applies the gates, which avoids bumping
VERDICT_VERSION and re-verifying the whole library for a filter change.

12.2a THE DENOMINATOR CANCELS WITHIN A FILM — A FEATURE, STATE IT      [SOLID]
Every candidate for a row is scored with THAT ROW'S key, so it gets the row's
target: rescoreCand() in audit.js sets bppPlus = bppIndex(c.bpp, row.key), and the
upgrade gain compares bppIndex(candBpp, row.key) against bppIndex(row.bpp,
row.key). Same key, same target, so the target DIVIDES OUT:

    cndPlus / curPlus  ==  sqrt(candBpp / rowBpp)

WITHIN ONE FILM, THE RANKING IS PURE bpp (x codec x resolution). Complexity,
biasFactor, HEADROOM_TARGET, cxEff — the entire per-film apparatus — cannot move
which release wins. It moves the LABEL and the band, not the choice.

THIS IS GOOD, AND IT IS THE STRONGEST PROPERTY OF THE DESIGN: a wrong denominator
CANNOT corrupt a same-film decision. A film measured badly gets a wrong-looking
number beside a still-correct pick. It also means the precision work in 4.4 buys
label quality, not decision quality, within a film.

WHERE THE DENOMINATOR DOES DECIDE: ACROSS films — "is my copy of this adequate for
its own content", which is what the band, the audit's admission rule and the
upgrade queue's ordering all rest on. Both statements are true and the document
used to assert only the second, which oversold per-film precision as
decision-critical everywhere. Found by adversarial review, 2026-08-19.

12.3 LIVE AUDIT STATE (2026-08-18)
    cpu      21 rows / 204 files / 182.44 GB
    bitrate  26 rows / 279.72 GB / 99.31 GB reclaimable
    edition   4 rows (Dune 2021, Dune Part Two, M:I Dead Reckoning, M:I Fallout
              — all IMAX-preferred, currently 0 candidates found)
    stale     0 rows | verify queue 120
    disk: 5.81 TB used of 7.94 TB (73%), 2.13 TB free
WORTH HOLDING THE 99 GB UNTIL THE PROJECTOR LANDS. That "reclaimable" figure is
surplus measured on a display that downscales 44% and hides the difference; the
1080p projector removes that mask (2.3), so some of it is about to become
visible. There is no hurry — 2.13 TB is free — and reclaiming it means
re-downloading to undo.

12.4 SCHEDULING
The verifier is NIGHTLY-GATED 01:00-06:00. `Recheck` only INVALIDATES verdicts;
`Run` starts a manual session (10s pacing vs 45s nightly). Any deploy restarts
the container and KILLS a running manual session — re-click Run after deploying.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
13. OPEN QUESTIONS, IN PRIORITY ORDER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
0. THE MODEL HAS NEVER BEEN TESTED AGAINST A VALID SUBJECTIVE LABEL (11.1).
   Everything below is downstream: fitting a coefficient, exponent or band edge is
   guesswork until a label measures compression rather than grain.
1. *** ANSWERED 2026-08-20, AND A DECISION IS NOW WAITING. *** The codec floor is
   1.1295, not x1.06 — 16 controlled points, bit-exact controls, and the assumed
   value sits OUTSIDE the 95% CI (1.099-1.151). The anchoring was then recovered
   (5.1), which unblocked the refit, and the refit is computed (5.2): A 1.114,
   B -0.392, fitted on the x264 arm, which is the library-realistic one.
   Adopting it moves the movie median 60 -> 64.5 and flips 106 bands, all upward.
   NOT APPLIED: it is a model change touching every corrected film, and that is
   Brennan's call. The open question is no longer the measurement — it is whether
   to adopt now or wait for a valid label (question 0) to judge it against.
1a. WAS 3 SAMPLES PER STARVATION POINT ENOUGH? Probably yes, on n=1 evidence.
   The 8-sample re-run (task #49) mostly failed — see
   docs/REPORT-starve-8s-and-banding-starvation-2026-08-21.md — but ONE film
   completed at both sample counts. The Social Network's understatement moved
   -1.1% at level 0.5 and -2.6% at 0.25, against the ~6% discrepancy that drove
   the 2026-08-20 refit. So the live constants would not move materially. n=1, so
   this is an indication rather than a result; it does lower the value of
   completing the arm, which is worth recording either way.

2. IS HEADROOM_TARGET = 1.0 RIGHT? Library median 60; headroomLive 0.5736, frozen
   anchor 0.5449, neither applied. Adopting ~0.57 moves the median to ~81. Cheap
   to change (read-time constant, no re-probe), blocked only on question 0. Note
   1.2(e): it moves every score together, so it is a labelling question.
3. DOES THE PINNING CORRECTION STILL UNDER-CORRECT? 80 of 129 films were still
   R < 1 after upgrading, so their corrected complexity is still a floor. The
   controlled curve corrects harder than the old buckets everywhere, but one
   round of correction still does not arrive.
4. IS FIXED CRF FIXED QUALITY FOR *OUR* CONTENT? Industry says no, ~3-sigma 10
   VMAF, and the residual is believed to track grain. If so the denominator's
   error is systematic and correlated with content type. Never measured here, and
   VMAF cannot be the instrument (it would measure its own grain bias, 11.2).
5. IS THE 1D COLLAPSE THE WRONG SHAPE? BPP+ divides complexity into bpp and throws
   the pair away, treating every film with the same ratio as equivalent. Two films
   scoring 64 can be opposite situations: cheap content + starved copy
   (Arrival-like, an upgrade helps) vs costly content + adequate copy (12 Angry
   Men-like, an upgrade buys grain). Paris, Texas measured complexity scaling as
   srcBitrate^0.47 — SUB-LINEAR — so the iso-quality contours are not straight
   lines through the origin. DEPRIORITISED by Brennan (1.2d): a LAB view for
   understanding, not an Audit-tab axis. Both numbers already exist on all 1031
   units, so plotting it is free.
6. DOES gridRatio EARN A PLACE? n=2 sets a direction and it is the most promising
   artifact candidate because it isolates what bits CAN fix. Needs many more
   STATIC BRIGHT samples; automatic scene selection is the blocker.
7. THE sqrt EXPONENT is fitted from our content and never independently checked.
8. REFINEMENT HAS BARELY RUN: 10 units with an RSE, 1 pooled past 8 samples. And
   761 of 1031 units can NEVER gain one under the current selection rule (16.A.3).
9. DELIBERATELY-DEGRADED PHOTOGRAPHY (Blair Witch, found-footage, Super 8
   pastiche) scores low and correctly, but no upgrade exists. No handling today.
10. The probe progress bar counts complexity only, so it can read "done" while
    audio or refinement work is still pending.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
14. TRAPS FOR ANY AGENT WORKING ON THIS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. HOST ffmpeg IS 4.2 AND HAS NO blockdetect/blurdetect — it fails SILENTLY and
   yields empty columns. Always run detector work via `docker exec controller`
   (ffmpeg 5.1).
2. `-vf` IS AN OUTPUT OPTION and must precede the output URL or ffmpeg emits
   nothing.
3. I-FRAMES ARE INSERTED AT SCENE CUTS, so a naive iRatio reports "compression"
   on any clip containing one. Without the cut guard, Spirited Away (hand-drawn,
   no grain, judged clean) scored 2.23 "COMPRESSION".
4. The frame-type ffprobe interval must cover EVERY decoded frame, or later
   frames read as `?` and a keyframe inside the window is silently averaged into
   the P/B floor.
5. killProbe() SENDS SIGKILL, which cannot be trapped — a script-side cleanup
   handler cannot fix the zombie leak. Reaping at PID 1 (`init: true`) is the
   only place it can be solved.
6. THIS BOX HAS 4 CORES. Run encode experiments strictly sequentially.
7. A SINGLE top/docker stats/iostat SAMPLE LIES. Sample properly before claiming
   a process is eating the box.
8. SUBJECTIVE TESTING HAS TWO TRAPS, AND THE SECOND ONE ALREADY COST US THE ONLY
   DATASET (11.1).
   (a) JUDGE ONLY FILMS BRENNAN HAS WATCHED. A first-time viewer cannot separate
       "this encode looks wrong" from "this film always looked like that". The
       first blind list was mostly unseen films and had to be rebuilt from
       Jellyfin's played list.
   (b) NEVER ASK FOR A VERDICT ON A SINGLE UNPAIRED CLIP. Grain and compression
       noise are not separable by eye that way — the 2026-08-13 test asked
       "artifacts: yes/no" and got back a grain rating, and the whole experiment
       had to be retracted. Show TWO versions of the SAME SCENE and ask which is
       worse. Grain is then identical across the pair by construction, so only
       compression can move the answer. Forced-choice also kills the tie-heavy
       Spearman that made n=11 look more informative than it was.
9. nextUnit() ONLY PICKS FRESH OR STALE UNITS. A film already measured from the
   file still on disk is NEVER selected again. This is the structural hole that
   stranded audio measurement for four silent days while the library sat at
   99.9% "measured". runAudioBackfill() and nextImprecise() exist to reach
   already-measured units; do not fold them back into nextUnit(), which would
   make it never return null and starve everything behind it.
10. The unit key ('mv:<radarrId>' / 'tv:<sonarrId>:<season>') is built
    independently by audit.js and probe.js from the same *arr ids. That
    agreement is load-bearing and must not drift.
11. `R` IS NOT THE RATIO BPP+ DIVIDES BY, and treating it as one produces a
    convincing false alarm. R divides by the UNCORRECTED probe rate; BPP+ divides
    by the CORRECTED target (cxEff * headroom), so R overstates it by roughly
    biasFactor. MEASURED across 898 movies (2026-08-20):
        bppPlus / (100*sqrt(R))          median 0.847
        bppPlus / (100*sqrt(bpp/target)) median 1.000 — an identity, as it must be
    Plotting the library against R therefore puts EVERY film below the
    100*sqrt(R) curve and looks exactly like the model failing to fit its own
    data. bpp-lab's Quality curve view did this until 2026-08-18. Use
    `bpp/target` from /api/probe/dataset.

    AND R ITSELF IS NOT src/probe. Two corrections sit between them, and skipping
    either has burned an analysis already:
      (a) CODEC NORMALISATION — the probe always encodes x265, so an h264 source
          is divided by 1.6. R = srcBitrate / (probeBitrate * eff). Omitting it
          inflates every h264 row by 1.6x and makes starved copies look supplied.
      (b) AUDIO — `srcBitrate` is the container total on 470 of 1044 units
          (`srcBasis: 'container'`); R has that audio subtracted and `rawR` does
          not (5.2a). Median container audio share is 9.8%, max ~47%.
    The dataset carries `R` (corrected, what scoring uses), `rawR` and `srcBasis`
    so an offline analysis can reproduce any of the three deliberately.
12. AN EXPERIMENT THAT USES FEWER SAMPLES THAN THE NIGHTLY PROBE CANNOT BE
    COMPARED TO IT ABSOLUTELY (5.1). Take RATIOS within a film, where scene
    selection cancels, and anchor any absolute axis to the live probe.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
15. STANDING RULES FOR THIS SERVER (not BPP+ specific, but absolute)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- NEVER git commit or push. Committing is Brennan's, always, in every repo.
- NEVER trigger a replacement, grab or redownload unless Brennan authorised THAT
  PRECISE file-for-file replacement. Approving a plan or a batch is NOT
  approving a grab.
- NEVER modify, delete or re-encode media on disk without explicit per-action
  permission.
- NEVER substitute Brennan's picks. If a chosen release cannot be recovered,
  cancel and list them.
- EVERYTHING IS IaC. Config lives in scripts/provision/. A hand-made change that
  does not survive a re-provision is a bug, and that is a known recurring
  failure mode on this box.
- VERIFY BEFORE YOU CLEAN UP. Delete the old thing only once the new thing is
  confirmed good.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
16. FUTURE WORK, AND HOW TO ADOPT A NEW MEASUREMENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
THE FRAME. One defect has been demonstrated under control: source pinning, now
measured directly (5.1) and corrected in code (5.2). Grain boiling is real but
n=2 and has never been linked to a quality misjudgement. The score itself has
never been tested against a valid label. So this list is ordered by
evidence-to-effort, and the cheap items come first on purpose.

16.A WHAT TO TRY NEXT, IN ORDER

1. FINISH THE CODEC CONFOUND CHECK.            [hours, no new deps, HIGHEST VALUE]
   probe-starve.sh --enc libx264: starve the same clips with x264, re-measure,
   read the floor off barely-starved films. Today the live curve subtracts x1.06
   estimated from ONE film (5.2); if the true floor is larger, the shipped
   correction OVER-corrects every score in the library. Started 2026-08-18,
   abandoned unfinished.

2. EXTEND THE STARVATION EXPERIMENT DOWNWARD AND OUTWARD.      [~10 min/pair]
   The curve is held flat below R = 0.59 because nothing was measured there, and
   the library minimum is R 0.14 — the most damaged films are exactly the ones the
   correction refuses to reach. Starving to 1/8 and 1/16 puts real points there.
   Widen the film set too: 8 films establishes a mechanism, not a two-parameter
   curve. Include heavy-grain and B&W titles explicitly.
   WHY THIS BEATS WAITING FOR BANKED PAIRS: CONTROLLED (we choose the degradation,
   so the x-axis is known rather than inferred); UNBIASED (banked pairs record
   which films were bad enough to replace — growth and old R correlate -0.657
   purely from that selection); DENSE WHERE IT MATTERS (only 1 of 147 banked pairs
   sits below R 0.2).
   STILL CROSS-CHECK: synthetic starvation is an x265 re-encode, real bad copies
   come from many encoders and generations. Confirm the two populations agree
   where they overlap (R 0.4-1.0) before trusting synthetic points outside it.

3. FIX THE REFINE QUEUE'S SELECTION RULE.       [small, unblocks all precision work]
   761 of 1031 units can NEVER gain a measured error bar. nextImprecise() only
   picks units it already believes imprecise, and with no cxRSE that judgement
   falls back to spreadRatio > 6 — so a unit under the threshold is never
   revisited, never gains per-sample data, and never produces a cxRSE that could
   disagree with the proxy. The units we are most confident about are the ones
   nothing has checked, and spreadRatio is max/min, which one near-black frame
   inflates. Fix in the shape of STALE_SHARE: give never-sampled units a small
   guaranteed share of the queue.

4. FIX THE LABEL INSTRUMENT, THEN RE-RUN EVERYTHING AGAINST IT.   [the unblocker]
   Forced-choice A/B on the SAME SCENE of the SAME FILM: "which looks worse".
   Grain is held constant across the pair by construction, so only compression can
   move the judgement. Generate B by re-encoding the SAMPLED CLIP at a
   known-degraded bitrate — probe-starve.sh already produces exactly these clips,
   and the ~/grain-test rig is the same shape.
   Target 20-30 films Brennan has WATCHED, balanced across clean modern digital,
   clean older transfer, heavy-grain film, B&W grain, animation, known low-bitrate
   encode, known re-encode, deliberately-degraded photography.
   ONE PASS RE-TESTS EVERYTHING AT ONCE: R, blockMean, blurMean, gridRatio,
   grainShare, and HEADROOM_TARGET (open question 2). Judge on the phone at native
   resolution, and record "visible on the projector?" as a SEPARATE binary — the
   real decision is whether an upgrade changes what Brennan sees in the room.
   This changes meaning when the 1080p projector arrives.

5. CAPTURE A GRAIN FIGURE — additive, time-sensitive.
       grainShare = 1 - complexity_denoised / complexity_raw
   One extra encode per unit, splitting the denominator into content cost and
   grain cost. Add to the probe entry AND to PRIOR_FIELDS.
   *** READ 11.3 FIRST: A METRIC OF THIS NAME HAS ALREADY BEEN FALSIFIED. *** What
   differs is only the method — an explicit denoise pass (hqdn3d / nlmeans)
   re-encoded and differenced, rather than a `-tune grain` bitrate delta. Real
   methodological difference, NOT a reason to assume it works: run the same
   degeneracy tests (CGI vs film stock, independence from mean luma) first.
   WHY TIME-SENSITIVE: superseded copies are deleted at swap time, so a pair only
   holds what PRIOR_FIELDS captured when banked. 113 banked today, and every
   upgrade before instrumentation banks a pair without it.
   INSURANCE, NOT A FIX: justified by being cheap, purely additive (changes no
   score) and hard to recover later — not by "grain is the problem", which is not
   established (section 9). PRIOR ART: AV1 film-grain synthesis denoises, encodes
   and re-synthesizes grain from parameters for exactly this reason (~50% savings
   on heavy-grain sequences).

6. THE QUALITY AXIS — BLOCKED ON libvmaf. DO NOT SUBSTITUTE SSIM (11.2).
   The ladder is the RATE half only; a real knee needs a perceptual y-axis. The
   controller's Debian ffmpeg 5.1 has psnr, ssim and vmafmotion but NOT libvmaf,
   and jellyfin-ffmpeg 7.1.4 does not either. THE PATH: a SECOND, static ffmpeg
   built with libvmaf for the ladder ONLY — it must NOT replace the Debian ffmpeg
   the nightly probe uses, because a different x265 build makes all 1031 existing
   complexity measurements incomparable.
   THE PRESCRIBED FIX FOR GRAIN, which is Netflix's own AV1 pipeline design:
   DENOISE FIRST, then encode, then measure on the denoised signal (encoding
   artifacts are masked by grain amplitude anyway). So this is denoise -> ladder ->
   VMAF, sharing its denoise pass with item 5 — not "install libvmaf and plot it".
   WORTH HAVING IT FOR: CAMBI, a banding detector that is NOT pixelwise and so
   does not share the grain failure (the survey's verdict was "USE THIS"); the
   DOWNGRADE curve on clean content, exactly what disk reclamation needs ("shrink
   this to 2 GB and quality goes to X"); and one cheap discriminating test — does
   a VMAF-vs-our-copy curve bend on a CLEAN film and refuse to bend on a grainy
   one?
   NOT WORTH DOING: using VMAF to test whether fixed CRF is fixed quality (open
   question 4). Grainy films score lower VMAF at the same CRF and the fixed-CRF
   residual is ITSELF believed to track grain, so the test would measure VMAF's
   grain bias and report it as an encoder property.

7. THEN CONSIDER SCORE CHANGES. All want item 4 in hand first: moving
   HEADROOM_TARGET off 1.0; a gridRatio artifact term; a grainShare term, or
   grainShare as a GATE rather than a term (the Blair Witch class is "bits cannot
   help", a gate-shaped fact); re-examining the sqrt exponent.
   THE SHAPE OF THE ANSWER IS ONE NUMBER (1.2d): a single sortable score plus a
   short explanatory line, e.g.
       87 — GREAT   compression: strong · grain: normal · artifacts: low ·
                    upgrade potential: low
   those annotations being the gates made legible — compression ~ R, grain ~
   grainShare, artifacts ~ gridRatio, upgrade potential ~ source ceiling and
   biasFactor. Three of the four do not exist and none can be weighted without
   item 4. NOT a multidimensional dashboard.

SET ASIDE, so they are not re-proposed cold:
  X. LIBRARY-WIDE MULTI-CRF LADDERS — the headline recommendation of an external
     literature review, answered by the pilot: no knees, slope varies 2x but is
     not an input to the score, and the slope hypothesis is dead (11.5). ~170 h of
     encoding for a constant. Do not build the cron.
  Y. PROBING AT 720p to match the projector — right for the display we had,
     obsolete before it was tried. PROBE_REFERENCE_WIDTH stays 1920, and the
     survey's "measure at 720p, effectively a free grain filter" dies with it.

16.B HOW TO ADOPT A NEW MEASUREMENT INTO BPP+ — THE ORDER, AND WHY
Any pending measurement (grainShare, gridRatio, CAMBI, VMAF) comes through this
gate. The steps are ordered by what makes the next one safe.

1. MEASURE IT ON A PILOT, NOT THE LIBRARY. A stratified subset answers "does this
   vary with content" for a few hours of encoding. The library-wide version costs
   ~170 h and is justified only once the answer is yes.
2. DECIDE WHETHER IT IS EVEN A PER-FILM QUANTITY. If the pilot shows it is
   uniform, it is a CONSTANT, not a measurement: fix the constant, delete the
   experiment, do NOT build a cron. A nightly job re-measuring a constant is pure
   cost. This is the most likely outcome and the easiest to miss, because building
   the cron feels like progress. (Ladder slope is the worked example: it varies,
   and it still does not earn a cron.)
3. VALIDATE BEFORE WEIGHTING. *** NOT OPTIONAL, AND THE ONE THAT WAS SKIPPED. ***
   blockMean was measured, stored, correlated, given a plausible mechanism and
   nearly shipped inside wBPP+ — and the experiment that appeared to settle it was
   retracted wholesale (11.1). A term needs a WEIGHT, a weight needs a fit, a fit
   needs a valid label. There is none in this library today. Until there is, a new
   measurement may be STORED and DISPLAYED but must not enter the score.
4. USE PARTIAL DATA WHERE YOU HAVE IT. Brennan, 2026-08-18: "I prefer accuracy. If
   only part of the library has data for something, we use it when possible. Just
   like audio." Measured audio was applied per-film as coverage grew from 8 films
   to 1031; waiting for full coverage would only have kept everyone wrong longer.
   THE DISTINCTION THAT MATTERS is whether the MISSING-DATA DEFAULT IS BIASED, not
   merely less precise:
     - Default unbiased (just noisier): partial application strictly reduces total
       error. Apply it. This is the audio case.
     - Default biased: partial application still reduces error for the films it
       reaches but OPENS A SYSTEMATIC GAP between the two groups. Apply it anyway,
       SAY SO ON THE ROW, and do not read a ranking across the two groups as one
       scale.
   (An earlier edition argued the opposite — that partial coverage makes the index
   "measure its own coverage" — and was wrong; the lesson from the incident behind
   that rule is to MARK the difference, not withhold the fix.)
   THIS REQUIRES the basis to be visible: `cxBasis` and the bpp+* mark already do
   it, and any new input needs the same.
5. ONLY THEN: CRON, UI, PLAYGROUND, in that order. And PROBE_VERSION must NOT be
   bumped for a scoring change — it invalidates the cache and throws away weeks of
   nights. It is for changes to how a measurement is TAKEN.
6. VERSION THE SCORE ITSELF. Changing the formula makes every historical number
   incomparable: cached audit verdicts, banked pairs, the calibration anchor, every
   figure in this document. Decide at adoption time whether old values are
   migrated, recomputed or discarded — and record which, because "the numbers
   changed and nobody wrote down why" is unrecoverable a month later.
7. UPDATE THIS DOCUMENT IN THE SAME CHANGE (section 0).

ON RUNNING THE CRONS HARD: the mechanism exists and it is the MANUAL SESSION
(/api/probe/session/start), which waives the 01:00-06:00 schedule while keeping
every safety gate — Movie Mode, someone watching, the tick lock. Brennan drives it
through the daytime at his discretion; that is the intended use. What should NOT
be done casually is widening the unattended NIGHT budget, the thermal envelope of
a 4-core NUC that also serves video, inside which an uncapped nightly task has
already run away once (the trickplay incident). A human choosing to run it now is
different from the box deciding to. And generating data faster only helps once
step 3 is passed: unweighted data accumulates at any speed and improves nothing.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
17. SOURCES — INCLUDING THE ONES THAT WERE NO USE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every source consulted is listed, with a one-line verdict, so nobody re-reads a
dead end.

INTERNAL, and the primary sources for everything above:
  docs/research-2026-08-19/   2026-08-19 research round. README.md is the
                              executive summary of 7 parallel tracks (adversarial
                              review + datasets/BVI-HD, ITU-T P.1203/P.1204.3,
                              banding/CAMBI, grain, Reddit/Doom9 chatter, Netflix
                              tech blog). See the "numbers are stale" warning in
                              section 7 below. Findings changed NOTHING in code
                              or in the doc except this pointer.
  docs/DESIGN-CRF-PROBE.md          the original probe design.
  docs/archive/HANDOFF-2026-08-17.md the grain investigation writeup.
  docs/REPORT-grain-2026-08-13.md   the full grain working record AND the
                                    falsification of gShare. Read before
                                    proposing any grain metric.
  raw/bitrate-plateau-2026-08-01.md where the sqrt exponent came from.
  docs/audit-2026-07-31/raw/RESEARCH-quality-metrics-2026-08-01.md
                                    the metric survey that rejected SSIM and
                                    singled out CAMBI. Its 720p-as-grain-filter
                                    recommendation is now obsolete.
  data/ladder-pilot.json            ladder pilot raw data (section 6).
  data/starve-experiment.json       starvation experiment raw data (5.1).
  ~/grain-test/                     A/B/C Star Wars clips (source / CRF-14 /
                                    denoised-CRF-14). Clip C is smaller than the
                                    source: the clearest demonstration of how
                                    much of these files is grain.
  GET /api/probe/dataset            the analysis surface (section 0).
  bpp-lab/                          npm run dev on :5174.

EXTERNAL, READ IN THIS ORDER. Priority revised 2026-08-18: P.1204.3 and the
Google/YouTube paper sit AHEAD of the Durbha and Netflix ladder papers, because
they attack the no-reference / already-compressed problem we actually have.
Items 1 and 2 were MISSING from the earlier literature review; item 7 was
OVER-WEIGHTED in it.

  1. ITU-T P.1204.3 — bitstream-based NO-REFERENCE video quality model.
     https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9123110/
     https://github.com/Telecommunication-Telemedia-Assessment/bitstream_based_models
     VERDICT: THE CLOSEST EXISTING THING TO WHAT BPP+ IS TRYING TO BE. Predicts
     subjective quality from the bitstream alone (H.264 / HEVC / VP9), no
     reference, trained and validated against ~5,000 lab-tested sequences, with
     model variants for TV / PC / tablet / phone. Open-source parser available.
     CAVEAT: trained on streaming-ladder encodes of CLEAN sources; its corpus has
     essentially no film grain, so pointed at a grainy 1976 Bluray rip the likely
     failure is that it reads grain as degradation — the exact error we are trying
     to escape. Read it for METHOD and per-device framing; do not integrate before
     the label instrument exists (16.A.4).
  2. Google / YouTube — Video transcoding optimization based on input perceptual
     quality (2020).
     https://research.google/pubs/video-transcoding-optimization-based-on-input-perceptual-quality/
     VERDICT: THE ONLY PAPER HERE WHOSE PROBLEM IS OURS. It assumes the input is
     ALREADY IMPERFECT and asks how much further degradation it can tolerate,
     rather than assuming a pristine master. Directly analogous to the
     source-pinning correction (section 5).
  3. Norkin & Birkbeck — Film Grain Synthesis for AV1 (DCC 2018).
     https://norkin.org/pdf/DCC_2018_AV1_film_grain.pdf
     VERDICT: USEFUL, AND THE REASON 16.A.5 IS SHAPED AS IT IS. AV1 denoises,
     encodes and re-synthesizes grain from parameters — normatively — because
     grain cost and content cost are separable and grain is enormously expensive
     to reproduce literally. Savings up to ~50% on heavy-grain sequences. Also the
     standard warning that aggressive denoising takes detail with the grain, which
     is the Heat problem (9.4).
  4. Netflix — Per-Title Encode Optimization (2015).
     https://netflixtechblog.com/per-title-encode-optimization-7e99442b62a2
     VERDICT: FOUNDATIONAL FOR THE PER-FILM DENOMINATOR, and the direct
     justification for killing flat BPP_TARGET = 0.13 (4.1). They measure MULTIPLE
     quality points per title and take a convex hull; they do not treat one QP as
     transparency. Different ladders per device class, the same reasoning as our
     1080p ceiling. LIMIT: THEY ENCODE A PRISTINE MASTER (constraint 1.2b). Do not
     import the methodology wholesale.
  5. MainConcept — Constant Target Quality encoding driven by perceptual fidelity.
     https://blog.mainconcept.com/constant-target-quality-encoding-driven-by-perceptual-fidelity
     VERDICT: ONE USEFUL NUMBER. Fixed CRF is not fixed quality — measured spread
     ~3-sigma 10 VMAF against ~2.6 for quality-targeted encoding. The evidence
     behind the caveat in section 4 and open question 4.
  6. Netflix — Optimized Shot-Based Encodes (2018), and VMAF itself.
     https://medium.com/netflix-techblog/optimized-shot-based-encodes-now-streaming-4b9464204830
     https://github.com/Netflix/vmaf
     VERDICT: PARTLY USEFUL. Good for what a real rate-quality trajectory looks
     like, and for CAMBI as an example of a distortion-SPECIFIC detector kept
     separate from the headline score — the same architecture as our gates.
     VMAF itself is FULL-REFERENCE and cannot replace R (1.2a), and its grain
     failure is documented in 11.2. Shot-level optimization is not worth it for a
     ~1,000-title home library.
  7. Durbha & Bovik — per-shot bitrate ladders from VIF features (2023 / 2025).
     https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.07780      https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.01932
     VERDICT: OVER-WEIGHTED IN THE EARLIER REVIEW; further from our problem than
     1 or 2. Predicting rate-quality structure from cheap source features instead
     of exhaustive encoding is the same instinct as our sampled probe, but it
     EXPLICITLY ASSUMES AN UNCOMPRESSED SOURCE (1.2b).
  8. FFmpeg blockdetect / blurdetect.
     https://ffmpeg.org/ffmpeg-filters.html
     VERDICT: THE TOOLING BEHIND blockMean/blurMean, which are untested, not
     falsified (11.4). Note trap 1: the HOST ffmpeg is 4.2 and does not have them.

STILL MISSING FROM THIS LIST, and worth someone's search time: no-reference
quality work on ALREADY-COMPRESSED consumer video (the piracy-scene / archival
case rather than the streaming case); grain-aware no-reference metrics; and any
published treatment of "measure a re-encode's headroom without a master", which
is precisely the source-pinning problem and which we have so far solved only by
experiment (5.1).

